hown. |\n| `entire checkpoint explain <decision-checkpoint-id>` for each of the four IDs below | Local checkpoint transcripts displayed each corresponding user decision and its response. They are marked temporary; summaries are not generated. |\n| Local transcript phrase inspection at `/home/maxg/.codex/sessions/2026/10/14/rollout-2026-10-14T05-40-46-01a139c9-256b-73d2-b559-bdb14329e346.jsonl` | Found the four first/second/third/fourth decision messages at JSONL lines 80, 92, 104, and 116. The transcript was inspected in place and not copied into this repository. |\n\nThe four decision checkpoint IDs are:\n\n- First, hold only affected work: `d03d4ca0a0a4776cff52fe30e39992498ce51179`\n- Second, revalidate affected dependencies after material corrections: `516f2428078430af2a67323298d7a60884a8279a`\n- Third, provisional financial completion conditions: `5eaa1447b407a65f7befb2a5abb6011c5ae22907`\n- Fourth, owner-grouped repair queue and partial responses: `d4229dc925600b4421fde8fd5bdfc5a981a25e99`\n\nThe four decisions are verified in **local Entire.io evidence**. Remote checkpoint synchronization is **not verified** because the checkpoint remote was unreachable. The current verification turn was still active at inspection time, so this record does not claim a completed checkpoint for this turn.\n\n## Failure and correction retained\n\nAn earlier structural test run produced **1 failed, 23 passed**. The repair-queue update test expected `load_repair_queue()` to return the derived `by_owner` display field; the loader intentionally returns the normalized queue without that persisted view. The assertion was corrected to read `by_owner` from the persisted JSON while continuing to validate the normalized queue through the loader. Subsequent full-suite runs passed, including the 25-test run recorded above.\n\n## Generated-output review and blockers\n\n- Reviewed `sources.json` and `parsed-records.json` structurally and verified their retained-content bindings. The Notion policy remains unavailable; the case clock remains unresolved. Both records retain affected scope, owner, next action, and needed evidence in `parsed-records.json`.\n- Read back the README and Skill Markdown. No generated batch snapshots, claims CSV, or operations report exist; this stage has not presented incomplete records as final case outputs.\n- Policy eligibility, entitlement, approval readiness, batch consumption, and financial reconciliation remain blocked. The facilitator must supply the authoritative scenario case clock; the Travel Administration Lead/facilitator must provide accessible original Notion policy content for a fresh native read.\n- No linter or type checker was available. No scenario-level result, approval, or payment is asserted by this verification record.\n{\n  \"enabled\": true,\n  \"checkpoints\": {\n    \"primary\": {\n      \"type\": \"git-refs\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n{\n  \"run_id\": \"source-capture-20261014T100034Z-follow-up-milestone\",\n  \"source_version\": \"native-source-manifest/1\",\n  \"case_clock\": null,\n  \"files\": [\n    {\n      \"source_id\": \"policy-pol-2026-2\",\n      \"path\": null,\n      \"sha256\": null,\n      \"status\": \"unavailable\",\n      \"error\": \"HTTP response contained a generic Notion shell; policy page content was not accessible\",\n      \"url\": \"https://private-pecorino-70e.notion.site/REDACTED\",\n      \"locator\": \"Notion policy page body\",\n      \"observed_at\": \"2026-10-14T10:01:29Z\",\n      \"version\": null,\n      \"scope\": \"Travel reimbursement policy POL-2026.2\",\n      \"missing_scope\": [\n        \"Travel reimbursement policy POL-2026.2\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_id\": \"initial-claim-binder\",\n      \"path\": \"sources/initial-claim-binder.pdf\",\n      \"sha256\": \"b42a28754b1877363e1a25ed4fa1f3865915f44ec9995f7ed15fc9b6eefb08b7\",\n      \"status\": \"read\",\n      \"error\": null,\n      \"url\": \"https://drive.google.com/file/d/13sRj6s_kMeP1hsYGstWWmfkb6SCN1YOS/view\",\n      \"locator\": \"Complete PDF document\",\n      \"observed_at\": \"2026-10-14T10:01:30Z\",\n      \"version\": \"Sat, 12 Sep 2026 02:56:31 GMT\",\n      \"scope\": \"Initial claim binder\",\n      \"missing_scope\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_id\": \"claim-updates-binder\",\n      \"path\": \"sources/claim-updates-binder.pdf\",\n      \"sha256\": \"03c15787d18e2928457cd0164b573dc03a3baade0ed26a391ba79c73a568da3b\",\n      \"status\": \"read\",\n      \"error\": null,\n      \"url\": \"https://drive.google.com/file/d/REDACTED/view\",\n      \"locator\": \"Complete PDF document\",\n      \"observed_at\": \"2026-10-14T10:01:31Z\",\n      \"version\": \"Sat, 12 Sep 2026 02:56:29 GMT\",\n      \"scope\": \"Claim updates binder\",\n      \"missing_scope\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_id\": \"receipt-binder\",\n      \"path\": \"sources/receipt-binder.pdf\",\n      \"sha256\": \"4c3dc47f187ccc5cdd0112e18afaadd3b6363bbb4d96793205ef8f3d4c06fcc8\",\n      \"status\": \"read\",\n      \"error\": null,\n      \"url\": \"https://drive.google.com/file/d/REDACTED/view\",\n      \"locator\": \"Complete PDF document\",\n      \"observed_at\": \"2026-10-14T10:01:32Z\",\n      \"version\": \"Sat, 12 Sep 2026 02:56:31 GMT\",\n      \"scope\": \"Receipt binder\",\n      \"missing_scope\": []\n    },\n    {\nREADME.md:3:This repository is the Project D implementation for Alderbridge Consulting. The [Project D task](https://private-pecorino-70e.notion.site/REDACTED) and [snapshot schema](snapshot.schema.json) define the deliverables and retained-state contract. The [public template](https://github.com/GitRollTraining/travel-expense-claim) is the repository starting point.\nREADME.md:24:Every new capture invokes all five native URLs again. The implementation has no cached/offline fallback. The caller supplies a case clock from the authorized scenario inputs; omit it only for source-acquisition-only capture before a batch clock is available.\nREADME.md:27:travel-claim capture-sources <run-id> artifacts/runs/<run-id> --case-clock '<scenario ISO timestamp>'\nREADME.md:36:`parse-retained` first verifies the manifest and retained-byte hashes, then writes `parsed-records.json`. PDF text stays grouped by page, workbook data stays grouped by tab and row, and every extracted record includes its source ID, SHA-256 and page/row locator. This is a transcription layer; it does not evaluate policy or infer claim outcomes. Its exit status is `2` for partial inputs or an unavailable case clock and `1` for a verification or parsing error.\nREADME.md:46:This command does **not** make native-source requests. It checks that parsed records bind the current `sources.json`, integrates arrival batches in numeric order, and writes immutable `batches/arrival-NNN.json` snapshots linked by predecessor hashes. It writes the latest `claims.csv`, `repair-queue.json`, `offline-replay-state.json`, `report.md`, and `follow-up-drafts.md` alongside that run's retained inputs. The report, sidecar, and each snapshot label the result as an offline replay. Existing batch chains or output files are never overwritten; start a separately identified retained run to create a new chain. When the case clock is unresolved, snapshots retain the blocker and are only structural replay projections; no clock-dependent, policy-dependent, approval, or entitlement result is inferred.\nREADME.md:48:The report separates open claim repairs and trip/permit repairs by responsible owner from run-level external blockers. Each repair retains its subject, revision, source references, opening run/batch, and next action. Resolved items remain in the full queue history. `follow-up-drafts.md` contains one unsent message per responsible owner, with separate claim, trip/permit, and external-blocker sections. Only unresolved items produce drafts; every message requires human review and retains reasons, requested actions, and evidence references. Independent valid records continue processing while other claims remain unresolved.\nREADME.md:86:The implementation supports fresh source capture, byte-bound manifests, loss-aware transcription, schema/hash-checked snapshots, policy-neutral arrival-ordered replay, source-classified Finance reconciliation, exact CSV output, and local owner-grouped repair tracking. `offline-replay` is a structural replay only: the inaccessible reimbursement policy and unconfirmed case clock keep policy conclusions, approval readiness, entitlement, and closure unresolved. Human replies are not imported automatically, and the command never sends repair drafts or moves money. Do not treat an offline replay as a fresh source read or completed claim processing.\nsrc/travel_claim/parsing.py:224:        \"case_clock\": manifest[\"case_clock\"],\nsrc/travel_claim/parsing.py:225:        \"case_clock_state\": \"supplied\" if manifest[\"case_clock\"] else \"not_in_accessible_retained_sources\",\nsrc/travel_claim/parsing.py:226:        \"case_clock_issue\": None if manifest[\"case_clock\"] else {\nsrc/travel_claim/parsing.py:227:            \"reason\": \"No supplied scenario case-clock value was present in the retained native PDFs, workbook, or interview record used for this run.\",\nsrc/travel_claim/parsing.py:229:            \"next_action\": \"Provide the authoritative scenario case clock before consuming any arrival batch.\",\nsrc/travel_claim/parsing.py:258:                \"record_id\": \"scenario-case-clock\",\nsrc/travel_claim/parsing.py:260:                \"subject_id\": \"arrival-batch-clock\",\nsrc/travel_claim/parsing.py:263:                \"reason\": \"The task calls for a supplied case clock, but no explicit scenario date, time, or timezone was confirmed in the original task text or complete stakeholder interview export.\",\nsrc/travel_claim/parsing.py:265:                \"next_action\": \"Provide the authoritative scenario case clock before consuming any arrival batch.\",\n"},"started_at_ms":1792006210736,"completed_at_ms":1792006210790}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-14T19:30:11.383Z","ordinal":302,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a13be4-c736-7292-8090-f4a9cf0ea27b","call_id":"call_d84b4d107c514e13913c4e21707eec5e","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.8 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"86407d\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.05336958,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":14798,\"output\":\"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 14798)\\nTotal output lines: 1007\\n\\n---\\nname: travel-expense-claim\\ndescription: Prepare traceable Alderbridge travel claim reviews, independent trip and permit work, repair drafts, and financial reconciliation from freshly retrieved native sources and authorized batch decisions. Never approve, invent evidence, send requests, book travel, or move money.\\n---\\n\\n# Alderbridge Travel Expense Claim Skill\\n\\n## Inputs\\n\\n- A new run ID and the scenario's supplied case clock.\\n- The five native source locations listed below, retrieved afresh on every new run.\\n- The authorized arrival batch and its boundary/version, supplied by the exercise operator.\\n- Any prior retained snapshot only when performing an explicitly labeled offline replay; this never substitutes for fresh reads on a new run.\\n- Human reviewer, employee, and Finance responses only when present in supplied records.\\n\\nNever infer an approval, exception, source value, reply, or payment from a missing record.\\n\\n## Invocation\\n\\nInstall as described in the repository [README](../../README.md). Capture sources for a new run with:\\n\\n```bash\\ntravel-claim capture-sources <run-id> artifacts/runs/<run-id> --case-clock '<scenario ISO timestamp>'\\n```\\n\\nInspect the manifest status and verify retained bytes before processing a batch:\\n\\n```bash\\ntravel-claim verify-sources artifacts/runs/<run-id>\\ntravel-claim parse-retained artifacts/runs/<run-id>\\n```\\n\\nSource-only acquisition does not consume a batch. Batch processing must preserve supplied arrival boundaries and use the public [`snapshot.schema.json`](../../snapshot.schema.json). Current foundation helpers are documented in [`travel_claim.sources`](../../src/travel_claim/sources.py), [`travel_claim.parsing`](../../src/travel_claim/parsing.py), [`travel_claim.workflow`](../../src/travel_claim/workflow.py), [`travel_claim.snapshots`](../../src/travel_claim/snapshots.py), and [`travel_claim.outputs`](../../src/travel_claim/outputs.py).\\n\\n`parse-retained` hash-verifies the run before transcribing the PDFs and workbook into page- and row-referenced records. The extracted source text and workbook values are evidence only. If the case clock or policy source is unavailable, leave it unresolved and do not consume policy-dependent decisions.\\n\\nStructural workflow helpers preserve every claim, trip and permit revision; identify whether a supplied decision references a found historical or latest source revision without applying its outcome; deduplicate repeated event IDs and report payload conflicts; and track independent trip/permit cancellation states. They do not calculate policy results. Repair items can be updated with new owner, evidence, reason, action, and subject revision while preserving prior updates. Revision advances reopen the item.\\n\\n## Native sources\\n\\n1. [Travel reimbursement policy — POL-2026.2](https://private-pecorino-70e.notion.site/REDACTED)\\n2. [Initial claim binder](https://drive.google.com/file/d/13sRj6s_kMeP1hsYGstWWmfkb6SCN1YOS/view)\\n3. [Claim updates binder](https://drive.google.com/file/d/REDACTED/view)\\n4. [Receipt binder](https://drive.google.com/file/d/REDACTED/view)\\n5. [Travel registers workbook](https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/REDACTED/edit)\\n\\nRetain complete relevant bytes actually accessed. A response code or URL alone does not establish content access. Record partial and unavailable scopes separately. Never silently reuse a previous run's bytes in a new run.\\n\\n## Processing and authority boundaries\\n\\n1. Bind every fact and decision to its source, subject, and revision.\\n2. Hold only claims or dependent work affected by missing/conflicting facts. Continue independent valid work. Each unresolved item retains subject type and exact ID, revision, missing/conflicting fact, responsible owner, source reference, and next action.\\n3. Preserve every claim revision. After a material correction, use the corrected revision and revalidate affected evidence, calculations, trip/permit consistency, approvals, and Finance authorization. Preserve unrelated evidence only where it remains valid. Retain historical commitments, transfers, and refunds; do not use stale approvals as current.\\n4. Keep trip cancellation and permit cancellation as independent processes, including processes without claims. Cancellation does not itself erase entitlement or Finance obligations. Record Finance disposition and monetary resolution separately when required.\\n5. Use the owner-grouped local repair queue. Keep each item individually traceable. A partial response may resolve only the item(s) explicitly matched and supported by retained source evidence; unanswered, insufficient, or conflicting items stay open. Draft locally; never send requests.\\n6. Preserve immutable Finance event identifiers. Reconcile only confirmed transfers and refunds. Do not duplicate events or obligations on replay.\\n7. The provisional financial closure choice is to require current evidence and approvals, agreement between reviewed entitlement and authorized obligation, net confirmed payment equal to the authorized amount, required Finance resolutions, and no blocking issue. After the first actual processing result, pause for the operator to inspect reconciliation and unresolved cases and confirm final completion conditions and delivery scope.\\n\\nPeople retain business approval, exception, and payment authority. This Skill prepares work, validates supplied decisions, and records effects; it does not make discretionary approval recommendations, create replies, issue Finance exceptions, book travel, or move funds.\\n\\n## Outputs\\n\\n- `artifacts/runs/<run-id>/sources.json` and retained source bytes.\\n- One immutable schema-valid `batches/<batch-id>.json` per consumed batch, with relative source and predecessor paths plus SHA-256 hashes.\\n- `claims.csv` with exactly `claim_id,revision,trip_id,status,allowed_cents,paid_cents,balance_cents,next_owner,reason`.\\n- Owner-grouped local repair queue with individual items and run/batch update history.\\n- `report.md` describing completed work, obligations, unresolved items, owners, next actions, cancellations without claims, and source/run references.\\n\\nUnknown money is null in JSON and blank in CSV. Zero is a known amount. Never label incomplete work complete.\\n\\n## Errors, retries, and limitations\\n\\n- On source failure, retain the attempt URL, locator, UTC observation time, error, and missing scope. Hold only affected work and report its owner and resumption evidence.\\n- On partial source access, retain usable pieces and explicitly list missing scope. Do not claim the source was fully read.\\n- On invalid or conflicting batch data, preserve the input evidence, reject admission of unsupported effects, and create an owned issue; do not silently discard it.\\n- Retry a failed fresh retrieval only by making another live request and recording the new observation. Do not overwrite a prior run's manifest, retained bytes, or sealed snapshot.\\n- On replay, preserve obligation/event identity and money; detect conflicting content under an existing event ID.\\n- Foundation implementation currently covers source capture/verification, retained PDF/workbook transcription, snapshot sealing/verification, the claims CSV writer, and local repair queue primitives. Scenario-state processing, policy/evidence decisions, approvals, Finance reconciliation, replay orchestration, and the complete operations report remain to be implemented and verified.\\n# Project D — Travel Expense Claim Interview\\n\\nParticipant: Max Gabel · Exported: 10/13/2026, 6:10:03 PM\\n\\n## Travel Administration Lead · Travel Administration Lead\\n\\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:13 PM\\n\\nHello, thank you for your time.\\n\\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:13 PM\\n\\nI coordinate travel reimbursements at Alderbridge. Much of my time goes into checking evidence, chasing reviews, and finding out what Finance actually paid, especially when a claim changes. I would like help making the whole process repeatable. You can ask me about how we work, policy, the records we use, or the problems that keep coming back.\\n\\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:14 PM\\n\\nWhat policies?\\n\\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:14 PM\\n\\nWe follow policy POL-2026.2, which governs how expenses are reviewed and reimbursed. It outlines separate requirements for international permits and claims, specifies approval roles like the budget owner and supervisor, and sets limits on categories like lodging and meals. You can find all the details in the Travel reimbursement policy.\\n\\n- [Travel reimbursement policy](https://private-pecorino-70e.notion.site/REDACTED)\\n\\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:14 PM\\n\\nWhich part of the reimbursement workflow would you like to explore next?\\n\\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:14 PM\\n\\nAre those all the policies?\\n\\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:14 PM\\n\\nYes, policy POL-2026.2 is our primary reimbursement guide for 2026. It covers all the rules regarding approvals, expense categories and limits. Are there any specific categories you had in mind?\\n\\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:15 PM\\n\\nI want to know what happens when there are exceptions to the policies.\\n\\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:15 PM\\n\\nWhen there are exceptions, like for late filings or specific amount limits, we need a Finance exception. This official exception is issued by Finance for a specific claim version to resolve a set issue, like late filing or an unapproved permit. However, it doesn't waive missing evidence like receipts or proof of payment.\\n\\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:15 PM\\n\\nWhen you say Finance, is that a department?\\n\\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:15 PM\\n\\nYes, Finance refers to our financial department. They are responsible for issuing monetary tables like currency rates and spending caps, providing official exceptions for policy issues, and handling actual payment execution and reconciliation for claims.\\n\\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:16 PM\\n\\nI want to make sure I understand this.\\n\\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:16 PM\\n\\nI'm happy to go over\\n\\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:16 PM\\n\\nThe financial department holds the sole authority to issue official exceptions for policy discrepancies. on a specific claim version. They cannot waive a missing evidence such as receipts or proof of payment.\\n\\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:16 PM\\n\\nThat is correct. They are responsible for issuing official exceptions for specific issues on a claim version, but they cannot waive missing evidence like receipts or proof of payment.\\n\\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:16 PM\\n\\nWhere are receipts or a proof of payment stored?\\n\\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:16 PM\\n\\nReceipts are stored in the Receipt binder, while proof of payment, such as settled merchant transactions, is found in the Merchant payments tab of the Travel registers workbook.\\n\\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:16 PM\\n\\nWhich part of the work would you like to explore next?\\n\\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:17 PM\\n\\nDo receipts ever get destroyed or lost?\\n\\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:17 PM\\n\\nIf a receipt is lost, the employee must provide alternative evidence or an explanation, but since Finance cannot waive missing receipts, the claim will remain unresolved until the evidence is provided.\\n\\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:17 PM\\n\\nIs the Travel registers workbook a physical workbook or online?\\n\\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:17 PM\\n\\nThe Travel registers workbook is an online spreadsheet that we use to track merchant payments and other financial activities.\\n\\n- [Travel registers](https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/REDACTED/edit)\\n\\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:17 PM\\n\\nWhich part of the workflow would you like to discuss next?\\n\\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:18 PM\\n\\nWhat are the financial activities?\\n\\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:18 PM\\n\\nFinancial activities include the pending, failed, or settled outcomes of payment requests, as well as accepted commitments, adjustments, refunds, and cancellations. These are all tracked and owned by the Finance department.\\n\\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:18 PM\\n\\nTell me more about cancellations.\\n\\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:18 PM\\n\\nCancellations can target an entire trip or just a permit version independently of a claim. They must be initiated by the employee and confirmed by the supervisor, and these actions are tracked in the Travel cancellations workbook. If there were paid costs or Finance commitments, Finance will need to issue a specific disposition and resolve the money separately.\\n\\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:19 PM\\n\\nHow would they resolve the disposition?\\n\\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:19 PM\\n\\nFinance will issue a specific disposition bound to the cancellation ID, which confirms the reimbursement eligibility and provides a new allowed amount and reason for the affected costs.\\n\\n- [Travel cancellations](https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/REDACTED/edit)\\n\\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:19 PM\\n\\nIs there a limit to the allowed amount?\\n\\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:19 PM\\n\\nYes, limits apply to specific categories. Lodging and meals are limited by daily or nightly caps set by Finance, based on the destination and date, which are then multiplied by the documented duration. Transport and conference expenses are not subject to these caps.\\n\\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:19 PM\\n\\nHow is the lodging and meals determined? Like what pricing metrics?\\n\\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:19 PM\\n\\nThe limits for lodging and meals are determined by daily or nightly caps set by Finance, based on your destination, expense currency, and the payment date. You can see these metrics in the Caps tab of the Travel registers workbook.\\n\\n- [Travel registers workbook](https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/REDACTED/edit)\\n\\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:20 PM\\n\\nWhich part of the workflow would you like to discuss next?\\n\\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:20 PM\\n\\nHow do incomplete claims affect independent work?\\n\\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:20 PM\\n\\nIncomplete claims are held while the missing facts are addressed. You can continue processing independent claims separately while others are held, as long as the business impact is made clear.\\n\\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:20 PM\\n\\nWhen you say made clear, what types of data do you mean?\\n\\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:20 PM\\n\\nWe need to identify the exact claim version, specify which facts are missing, name the owner responsible for supplying them, and outline the next action to resolve the issue.\\n\\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:21 PM\\n\\nWhat actions do you have to choose from to resolve the issue?\\n\\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:21 PM\\n\\nThe available actions include requesting missing evidence from the employee, seeking an official exception from Finance for specific issues, or holding the claim until Finance confirms the resolution or provides the missing definitions.\\n\\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:21 PM\\n\\nOn average, how long does it take Finance to confirm resolution or provide the missing definitions?\\n\\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:21 PM\\n\\nI don't have a set average for Finance response times. Their replies come in batches, and the time it takes depends on the volume and complexity of the request.\\n\\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:21 PM\\n\\nWhen was the most recent time a Finance needed to confirm resolution that you can remember?\\n\\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:21 PM\\n\\nI don't track specific dates for those resolutions in my head. Our Finance replies are delivered in versioned batches, and we process the needed resolutions as they arrive based on the specific claims and costs.\\n\\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:22 PM\\n\\nHow many people are in a batch?\\n\\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:22 PM\\n\\nThe batch size isn't fixed; it depends on the number of claims, requests, or reviewer replies being processed by Finance or the relevant budget owner at that time.\\n\\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:22 PM\\n\\nHow are batches organized? For example, could they be organized by a time slot or with a certain amount of people inside?\\n\\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:22 PM\\n\\nBatches aren't organized by fixed time slots or a specific number of people. They are versioned delivery packets supplied by the trainer based on the number of claims, requests, and reviewer replies processed and ready at that moment.\\n\\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:22 PM\\n\\nHow are they assigned to a trainer?\\n\\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:22 PM\\n\\nI don't assign claims to a trainer. The replies you see come from versioned batches provided by Finance or other appropriate reviewers based on the business workflow.\\n\\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:23 PM\\n\\nHow many trainers are there?\\n\\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:23 PM\\n\\nI don't have information about trainers. My work focuses on coordinating with the Finance department, budget owners, supervisors, and employees to process travel claims.\\n\\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:23 PM\\n\\nWhat are the boundaries for trainers, meaning what can and can't they do in this?\\n\\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:23 PM\\n\\nI don't have information about trainers. My role is Travel Administration Lead, and the replies I receive come from Finance, budget owners, supervisors, and directors within the established business workflow.\\n\\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:23 PM\\n\\nTell me about the boundaries of Finance.\\n\\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:23 PM\\n\\nFinance owns setting currency rates, managing spending caps, and issuing official policy exceptions. They also execute payments and resolve any monetary issues or cancellations, but they cannot waive missing evidence like receipts or proof of payment.\\n\\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:24 PM\\n\\nTell me about the boundaries of budget owners.\\n\\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:24 PM\\n\\nBudget owners have the authority to approve, return, or reject a claim based on the complete department ledger. They assess funding availability and make discretionary decisions, which I receive in supplied reviewer replies.\\n\\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:24 PM\\n\\nTell me about a discretionary decision.\\n\\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:24 PM\\n\\nA discretionary decision is when the budget owner evaluates the department ledger and funding availability to decide whether to approve, return, or reject a claim. These judgments come directly from the budget owner's assessment and arrive in supplied reviewer replies.\\n\\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:24 PM\\n\\nTell me a…5298 tokens truncated…laim binder](https://drive.google.com/file/d/13sRj6s_kMeP1hsYGstWWmfkb6SCN1YOS/view)\\n- [Claim updates binder](https://drive.google.com/file/d/REDACTED/view)\\n\\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:01 PM\\n\\nFor permit cancellation lifecycle. Without an associated expense claim. What specific document or log entry serves as verification?\\n\\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:01 PM\\n\\nFor a permit cancellation without a claim, the verification is a confirmed cancellation record in the Travel cancellations tab of the Travel registers workbook. This record shows the initiation by the employee and confirmation by their supervisor.\\n\\n- [Travel registers](https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/REDACTED/edit)\\n\\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:02 PM\\n\\nIf a supervisor approves a business's purpose but the budget owner rejects the claim due to department funding shortages. How will this be handled? And who becomes the next owner?\\n\\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:02 PM\\n\\nWhen a claim is rejected by the budget owner due to funding, the claim is closed without payment, and the specific non-payment reason is recorded. Since it is a rejection, there is no next owner; the process concludes unless the employee chooses to submit a correction later.\\n\\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:02 PM\\n\\nWhen you say the process concludes, does that mean there is no reimbursement?\\n\\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:02 PM\\n\\nThat is correct. A rejection by the budget owner means the claim is formally closed, and no reimbursement will be paid for that specific submission, with the non-payment reason clearly documented.\\n\\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:03 PM\\n\\nSo, I want to clarify, that's not a payment execution failure, that's more of a resolution?\\n\\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:03 PM\\n\\nExactly. A budget rejection is a funding decision regarding entitlement, not a payment execution failure. It is documented as a rejection with its specific reason, separate from any Finance transfer issues.\\n\\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:03 PM\\n\\nSo, in the case of a payment execution failure, what types of codes or labels does Finance use?\\n\\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:03 PM\\n\\nFinance provides specific outcome labels such as \\\"failed,\\\" \\\"pending,\\\" or \\\"cancelled\\\" in the Finance activity log. These indicate the status of the monetary transfer rather than a decision on the claim's entitlement.\\n\\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:03 PM\\n\\nWhat's the difference between those three? Are they interchangeable?\\n\\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:03 PM\\n\\nThey are not interchangeable. \\\"Failed\\\" indicates an attempted transfer was unsuccessful, \\\"pending\\\" means the transfer is awaiting completion, and \\\"cancelled\\\" means the proposed payment was formally revoked before or after being initiated.\\n\\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:04 PM\\n\\nSo the data is held between Google Sheets workbook and PDFs, or are there more operational types of data?\\n\\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:04 PM\\n\\nWe rely on a combination of sources for operational data. This includes the Travel reimbursement policy on Notion, the Initial claim and Claim updates PDFs, the Receipt binder, and the Travel registers Google Sheets workbook.\\n\\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:05 PM\\n\\nSo, there's no contradiction between those sources?\\n\\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:05 PM\\n\\nThe operational sources are designed to be complementary and consistent. Each serves a different purpose in the workflow, such as policy definitions or payment records, and together they provide a complete case record.\\n\\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:05 PM\\n\\nDoes that ever lead to partial reviewer or Finance with replies with outdated revisions?\\n\\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:05 PM\\n\\nYes, when a reply comes with an outdated revision, it is rejected as not current authorization. We only accept responses that match the most recent claim revision and specific subject ID.\\n\\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:05 PM\\n\\nHow often is that encountered?\\n\\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:05 PM\\n\\nIt varies depending on when the reviewer or Finance processes the request, but I do not track the specific frequency, as my focus is on verifying each individual case.\\n\\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:06 PM\\n\\nTell me about a situation on how an agent should handle when Finance replies from an old claim version after an employee already submitted a correct version.\\n\\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:06 PM\\n\\nWhen Finance replies for an outdated version, we reject that response since it isn't current authorization. We retain that record in the history but do not apply it to the new claim, simply waiting for the reply that matches the correct claim revision.\\n\\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:06 PM\\n\\nSo, the newer source takes legal and operational precedence?\\n\\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:06 PM\\n\\nIt matches the specific version of the claim or permit. A newer version supersedes an older version on the same subject, but every decision must match the correct subject and revision to be structurally valid.\\n\\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:07 PM\\n\\nWhen you say valid, can that also mean single evidence can be missing and be marked as resolved?\\n\\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:07 PM\\n\\nNo, core evidence such as a receipt or settled merchant payment cannot be marked as resolved if it is missing. Those are structural requirements under the policy, and the claim must be held until the required evidence or an explicit Finance exception is provided.\\n\\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:09 PM\\n\\nTo make sure I understand this, I want to summarize: an incoming batch component either there's a decision/reply input or a new claim correction, and if it's a decision/reply input, it's checking the version is current, whereas a new claim correction increments revisions and wipes downstream approvals.\\n\\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:09 PM\\n\\nThat is correct. Incoming batches contain either reviewer decisions or claim corrections; decisions must match the current revision, while corrections increment revisions and necessitate re-evaluation of subsequent approvals.\\n# Project D incremental verification record\\n\\nThis record captures verification performed against the current uncommitted workspace on 2026-10-14. It distinguishes local Entire.io evidence from remote synchronization and structural tests from scenario processing. No batch was consumed; no project-branch commit or push was made. Entire did create temporary local shadow checkpoints as part of capture.\\n\\n## Code readback and available tools\\n\\n- Read back `pyproject.toml`, `src/travel_claim/workflow.py`, the changed sections of `src/travel_claim/outputs.py`, `src/travel_claim/parsing.py`, `tests/test_workflow_structure.py`, `tests/test_parsing.py`, `tests/test_project_d_foundation.py`, `tests/test_validate_snapshot.py`, `README.md`, and `skills/travel-expense-claim/SKILL.md`.\\n- `command -v ruff`, `command -v flake8`, `command -v pylint`, `command -v mypy`, and `command -v pyright` returned no paths. The virtual environment's `pip list` likewise had none of these tools. Linting and static type checking were therefore unavailable; no result is claimed for them.\\n- `.venv/bin/python -m compileall -q src tests` completed with exit status 0.\\n\\n## Commands and results\\n\\n| Command or inspection | Result |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| `git status --short && git diff --check` | Passed whitespace/error check. Workspace contains uncommitted project files; no commit or push was made. |\\n| `.venv/bin/python -m pytest -q` | **25 passed in 9.89s** (final full-suite run for this verification): `test_parsing.py` (3), `test_project_d_foundation.py` (12), `test_validate_snapshot.py` (3), and `test_workflow_structure.py` (7). |\\n| `.venv/bin/python -m pytest -q` (repeat after verification-record readback) | **25 passed in 9.91s**; this is the latest full-suite result. |\\n| `.venv/bin/python -m pytest --collect-only -q` | Collected the same 25 tests across those four files; no tests were skipped during collection. |\\n| `.venv/bin/travel-claim verify-sources artifacts/runs/source-capture-20261014T100034Z` | Passed: manifest and retained source hashes verified. |\\n| Parsed artifact integrity inspection using `.venv/bin/python` | Passed: exact manifest top-level fields; 5 source entries (4 retained, 1 unavailable); 2,411 extracted record/page references bound to retained source IDs, hashes, and locators; case clock remains null and both owned issues are present. No batches, `claims.csv`, or `report.md` exist in the run. |\\n| `entire status` | Entire reports enabled on `main`; it also reports the session as “active now · exited (run `entire doctor`)”. |\\n| `entire session current --json` | Reports Codex session `01a139c9-256b-73d2-b559-bdb14329e346`, status `active`, branch `main`, 12 checkpoints, and this workspace. |\\n| `entire checkpoint list --json` | Listed the local checkpoints for session `01a139c9-256b-73d2-b559-bdb14329e346`; warned that the checkpoint remote could not be reached and only local checkpoints were shown. |\\n| `entire checkpoint explain <decision-checkpoint-id>` for each of the four IDs below | Local checkpoint transcripts displayed each corresponding user decision and its response. They are marked temporary; summaries are not generated. |\\n| Local transcript phrase inspection at `/home/maxg/.codex/sessions/2026/10/14/rollout-2026-10-14T05-40-46-01a139c9-256b-73d2-b559-bdb14329e346.jsonl` | Found the four first/second/third/fourth decision messages at JSONL lines 80, 92, 104, and 116. The transcript was inspected in place and not copied into this repository. |\\n\\nThe four decision checkpoint IDs are:\\n\\n- First, hold only affected work: `d03d4ca0a0a4776cff52fe30e39992498ce51179`\\n- Second, revalidate affected dependencies after material corrections: `516f2428078430af2a67323298d7a60884a8279a`\\n- Third, provisional financial completion conditions: `5eaa1447b407a65f7befb2a5abb6011c5ae22907`\\n- Fourth, owner-grouped repair queue and partial responses: `d4229dc925600b4421fde8fd5bdfc5a981a25e99`\\n\\nThe four decisions are verified in **local Entire.io evidence**. Remote checkpoint synchronization is **not verified** because the checkpoint remote was unreachable. The current verification turn was still active at inspection time, so this record does not claim a completed checkpoint for this turn.\\n\\n## Failure and correction retained\\n\\nAn earlier structural test run produced **1 failed, 23 passed**. The repair-queue update test expected `load_repair_queue()` to return the derived `by_owner` display field; the loader intentionally returns the normalized queue without that persisted view. The assertion was corrected to read `by_owner` from the persisted JSON while continuing to validate the normalized queue through the loader. Subsequent full-suite runs passed, including the 25-test run recorded above.\\n\\n## Generated-output review and blockers\\n\\n- Reviewed `sources.json` and `parsed-records.json` structurally and verified their retained-content bindings. The Notion policy remains unavailable; the case clock remains unresolved. Both records retain affected scope, owner, next action, and needed evidence in `parsed-records.json`.\\n- Read back the README and Skill Markdown. No generated batch snapshots, claims CSV, or operations report exist; this stage has not presented incomplete records as final case outputs.\\n- Policy eligibility, entitlement, approval readiness, batch consumption, and financial reconciliation remain blocked. The facilitator must supply the authoritative scenario case clock; the Travel Administration Lead/facilitator must provide accessible original Notion policy content for a fresh native read.\\n- No linter or type checker was available. No scenario-level result, approval, or payment is asserted by this verification record.\\n{\\n  \\\"enabled\\\": true,\\n  \\\"checkpoints\\\": {\\n    \\\"primary\\\"