he exact same reimbursement policy and workflow. Finance personnel still need reviews from budget owners, supervisors, and directors for their claims, depending on the amount and travel type.\n   636\t\n   637\t**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:54 PM\n   638\t\n   639\tIs Finance allowed to handle their own files and cases?\n   640\t\n   641\t**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:54 PM\n   642\t\n   643\tTo maintain impartiality, Finance personnel cannot approve their own claims; they must still go through the required budget owner, supervisor, and director reviews based on the amount.\n   644\t\n   645\t**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:54 PM\n   646\t\n   647\tDoes that rule apply with every other person, including the budget owner, supervisor, and director?\n   648\t\n   649\t**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:54 PM\n   650\t\n   651\tYes, the rule applies universally; no individual can approve their own travel claim, regardless of their role or authority level within the company.\n   652\t\n   653\t**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:55 PM\n   654\t\n   655\tIs there a limit to how many travel claims can be made per person?\n   656\t\n   657\t**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:55 PM\n   658\t\n   659\tThere is no limit on the number of travel claims a person can make; each claim is processed individually based on the specific trip, expenses, and required approvals.\n   660\t\n   661\t**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:56 PM\n   662\t\n   663\tIs inflation accounted for for the specific types of reimbursements?\n   664\t\n   665\t**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:56 PM\n   666\t\n   667\tReimbursement caps for categories like lodging and meals are defined periodically by Finance, taking specific costs into account as set in the annual policy.\n   668\t\n   669\t**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:59 PM\n   670\t\n   671\tHello.\n   672\t\n   673\t**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:59 PM\n   674\t\n   675\tHello! Which part of the reimbursement workflow would you like to explore next? Hello. Since we've discussed how our processes work and the roles involved, which specific part of the workflow would you like to explore next?\n   676\t\n   677\t**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:00 PM\n   678\t\n   679\tI want to discuss more about the re-entry lifecycle in regard to an employee resubmits a correction that changes a claim. How do you structurally invalidate the old record? while preserving its history?\n   680\t\n   681\t**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:00 PM\n   682\t\n   683\tWhen a correction is submitted, it receives a new version number while the old version is retained in the history. The material changes structurally invalidate the prior reviews and approvals, requiring the claim to go through the required approval process again based on the new facts. You can see how we track these in the Initial claim binder and the Claim updates binder.\n   684\t\n   685\t- [Initial claim binder](https://drive.google.com/file/d/13sRj6s_kMeP1hsYGstWWmfkb6SCN1YOS/view)\n   686\t- [Claim updates binder](https://drive.google.com/file/d/REDACTED/view)\n   687\t\n   688\t**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:01 PM\n   689\t\n   690\tFor permit cancellation lifecycle. Without an associated expense claim. What specific document or log entry serves as verification?\n   691\t\n   692\t**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:01 PM\n   693\t\n   694\tFor a permit cancellation without a claim, the verification is a confirmed cancellation record in the Travel cancellations tab of the Travel registers workbook. This record shows the initiation by the employee and confirmation by their supervisor.\n   695\t\n   696\t- [Travel registers](https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/REDACTED/edit)\n   697\t\n   698\t**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:02 PM\n   699\t\n   700\tIf a supervisor approves a business's purpose but the budget owner rejects the claim due to department funding shortages. How will this be handled? And who becomes the next owner?\n   701\t\n   702\t**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:02 PM\n   703\t\n   704\tWhen a claim is rejected by the budget owner due to funding, the claim is closed without payment, and the specific non-payment reason is recorded. Since it is a rejection, there is no next owner; the process concludes unless the employee chooses to submit a correction later.\n   705\t\n   706\t**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:02 PM\n   707\t\n   708\tWhen you say the process concludes, does that mean there is no reimbursement?\n   709\t\n   710\t**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:02 PM\n   785\t\n   786\t**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:07 PM\n   787\t\n   788\tWhen you say valid, can that also mean single evidence can be missing and be marked as resolved?\n   789\t\n   790\t**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:07 PM\n   791\t\n   792\tNo, core evidence such as a receipt or settled merchant payment cannot be marked as resolved if it is missing. Those are structural requirements under the policy, and the claim must be held until the required evidence or an explicit Finance exception is provided.\n   793\t\n   794\t**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:09 PM\n   795\t\n   796\tTo make sure I understand this, I want to summarize: an incoming batch component either there's a decision/reply input or a new claim correction, and if it's a decision/reply input, it's checking the version is current, whereas a new claim correction increments revisions and wipes downstream approvals.\n   797\t\n   798\t**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:09 PM\n   799\t\n   800\tThat is correct. Incoming batches contain either reviewer decisions or claim corrections; decisions must match the current revision, while corrections increment revisions and necessitate re-evaluation of subsequent approvals.\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"src/travel_claim/readiness.py:21:    \"sources.json\", \"parsed-records.json\", \"offline-replay-state.json\",\nsrc/travel_claim/readiness.py:60:    parsed = objects.get(\"parsed-record