{"timestamp":"2026-10-14T19:48:41.883Z","ordinal":582,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_02f4b2bd528e1b90016acfdc6c901081908575360843db96fe","status":"completed","call_id":"call_b779953a3cad43d79e74c96c17affbc2","name":"exec","input":"text(await tools.exec_command({cmd:\"python3 - <<'PY'\\nfrom pathlib import Path\\np=Path('artifacts/verification/project-d-evidence-audit-f98e044.md')\\ncontent='''# Project D retained-evidence audit — f98e044\\n\\nRead-only audit of the user's existing interview and retained native-source evidence. No live retrieval, interview automation, policy adjudication, financial changes, commit, or push.\\n\\n**Assignment limitation:** The current user message says the original assignment has been provided, but contains no assignment text or attachment visible here. No separate assignment export was found in the repository, including hidden workspace files outside Git/Entire internals. The README contains a native assignment URL, not its contents. The coverage comparison below is therefore provisional against the existing interview, skill, schema and implementation; exact assignment compliance cannot be established until the text or local path is supplied.\\n\\n## Evidence inspected\\n\\n- Complete original interview: `interviews/project-d-travel-expense-claim-20261013-2210.md`, all 800 lines.\\n- All three `artifacts/runs/*/sources.json` manifests; each verifies successfully. Their four retained source payloads have identical hashes and their observation metadata is identical. These directories represent retained-input copies, not three independently observed native-source reads.\\n- All 229 PDF pages (120 initial claims, seven updates, 102 receipts), PDF metadata and image inventory (zero embedded image objects), all ten workbook tabs and nonempty cells, workbook properties, and the workbook ZIP's XML/relationship parts for clock/cutoff/boundary declarations.\\n- Re-extracted PDF page objects and all workbook record objects exactly match the original `parsed-records.json`. Workbook table counts: People 14; Trips 123; Merchant payments 201; FX 2; Caps 2; Budget 3; Review ledger 307; Review packets 307; Finance activity 220; Travel cancellations 9.\\n- Latest replay: `artifacts/runs/source-capture-20261014T100034Z-follow-up-milestone/`. All four snapshots pass existing schema/source/predecessor verification. No output was regenerated.\\n\\nSource reference registry (paths relative to `artifacts/runs/source-capture-20261014T100034Z/`; append the locator to the source ID and hash):\\n\\n| ID | Retained path | SHA-256 |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| `initial-claim-binder` | `sources/initial-claim-binder.pdf` | `b42a28754b1877363e1a25ed4fa1f3865915f44ec9995f7ed15fc9b6eefb08b7` |\\n| `claim-updates-binder` | `sources/claim-updates-binder.pdf` | `03c15787d18e2928457cd0164b573dc03a3baade0ed26a391ba79c73a568da3b` |\\n| `receipt-binder` | `sources/receipt-binder.pdf` | `4c3dc47f187ccc5cdd0112e18afaadd3b6363bbb4d96793205ef8f3d4c06fcc8` |\\n| `travel-registers-workbook` | `sources/travel-registers-workbook.xlsx` | `579691b671acc735d1fdbb4944a2daf397b13307075ba74f15fa8bc24daa75b8` |\\n\\n## Case clock: still unresolved; batch membership is available\\n\\nNo explicit authoritative scenario date + time + timezone or per-batch temporal cutoff was found in the searched retained content. Explicit event times and batch membership exist and must remain distinct:\\n\\n- Interview line 3: export date/time `10/13/2026, 6:10:03 PM`; dialogue headings have conversational times. Neither identifies a scenario clock or supplies its timezone. Lines 189–205 describe versioned delivery packets, rather than fixed time slots. No concrete batch cutoff is stated.\\n- `initial-claim-binder#PDF page 1`: C01 revision 1 has `Arrival batch: 1` and submitted date `2026-09-01`. This is a submission date, not a scenario timestamp.\\n- `claim-updates-binder#PDF pages 1–6`: batch 3; page 7: C19 revision 3, batch 4. These are explicit arrival labels.\\n- `receipt-binder#PDF page 1`: issued date `2026-09-01` is a receipt date.\\n- `travel-registers-workbook#tab 'Finance activity', row 3`: arrival batch differs from activity time. Row 6 has F-C01-1 in batch 2, activity time `2026-09-12 12:00:00`, under an explicit UTC header (row 5). Row 31 redelivers F-C01-1 in batch 4 with that same event time. A maximum event timestamp is therefore not a supported arrival clock or cutoff.\\n- `travel-registers-workbook#tab 'Travel cancellations', row 3`: arrival order differs from activity time; an empty effective time on initiation is not confirmation. Rows 6–14 provide request/confirmation events in UTC, not a global clock.\\n- Workbook `People!A3` labels ordinary dates as local calendar dates without specifying a named timezone. Date-only cells represented internally as midnight do not establish midnight as an event time.\\n- Source observation `2026-10-14T10:01:29Z`, HTTP version metadata, PDF creation/modification metadata (`2000-01-01`), and workbook core-property timestamps are document/acquisition metadata. None may be promoted to a scenario clock. All three manifests retain `case_clock: null`.\\n\\nExplicit batch membership from PDFs and workbook tables:\\n\\n| Arrival batch | Claim revisions | Review decisions | Finance event occurrences | Cancellation event occurrences |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| 1 | 120 | 289 | 100 | 2 |\\n| 2 | 0 | 0 | 100 | 4 |\\n| 3 | 6 | 15 | 10 | 2 |\\n| 4 | 1 | 3 | 10 | 1 |\\n\\nThese packet memberships support structural replay ordering. Whether they alone satisfy the assignment's arrival-boundary requirement remains to be confirmed from the assignment; no temporal boundaries should be inferred.\\n\\n## Native Notion policy: inaccessible, with limits on what can be proved\\n\\nPrimary observation: `artifacts/runs/source-capture-20261014T100034Z/sources.json`, `files[source_id=policy-pol-2026-2]` (lines 7–21):\\n\\n- Locator: `Notion policy page body`; observed at `2026-10-14T10:01:29Z`.\\n- Status: `unavailable`; recorded error: `HTTP response contained a generic Notion shell; policy page content was not accessible`.\\n- `path`, `sha256`, and `version` are null; missing scope is the entire reimbursement policy. No policy HTML/export is retained in any of the three source directories.\\n- The recorded result supports that the retrieval reached an unusable HTML response. It does not prove the exact shell contents or HTTP status: no response bytes, response hash, or status code were preserved. The rejection code uses a keyword heuristic (`src/travel_claim/sources.py:81–95`) and does not prove complete policy access on a future successful response.\\n- `parsed-records.json#unresolved_issues/reimbursement-policy-pol-2026-2` additionally mentions an earlier HTTP 401. That is a secondary retained narrative, not a separately retained HTTP response in this evidence set.\\n- Interview lines 21–35 identify POL-2026.2; workbook `Review packets`, row 6, references that policy revision. References and interview descriptions are accessible, but are not the native policy body. The URL slug contains POL-2026-1; neither that slug nor references establish the accessible policy version.\\n\\n## Material implementation gaps\\n\\n| Area and retained requirement | Existing support | Material gap |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Connected ordinary path — interview lines 397–425 and 583–587 | Source transcription, revision matching, cash-event reconciliation and owned repairs | No connected receipt → merchant-payment → trip/permit → FX/caps → reviewed entitlement → role approvals → request → closure execution. `src/travel_claim/integration.py:534–546` emits pending claims with empty lines and null entitlement; line 577 emits no requests. Reading the policy and supplying a clock will not by themselves implement this path. |\\n| Corrections — interview lines 277, 683 and 776–800 | Historical revisions/decisions preserved; five revalidation task categories; six correction transitions retained | Tasks identify work rather than execute evidence/calculation/role/authorization revalidation. Example: C19 revisions 2 and 3 in updates pages 2 and 7 carry explicit source Finance statements; neither statement alone establishes final eligibility/closure. See `integration.py:165–205`. |\\n| Independent cancellations — interview lines 111–127, 433 and 694 | Separate trip/permit event reduction and duplicate handling; TC122/T122 survives without a claim | All projected cancellations hardcode unresolved financial status and empty original request IDs (`integration.py:298–315`). No connected cancellation disposition/obligation resolution or complete original permit approval lifecycle. Workbook `Travel cancellations`, rows 6–9, documents independent trip TC122 and permit TC121; latest output links C121 only to TC121, and no claim to TC122. |\\n| Financial closure — interview lines 285–301 and 481 | Confirmed transfers/refunds kept separate from noncash Finance events; conflicts and stale authorization checked | Offline runner does not supply the independent authorized-obligation input used by `integration.py:464–470`; no integrated closure decision is made. Native Finance evidence is not absent: workbook `Finance activity`, row 33, records F-C01-accepted/REQ-C01; row 25 records F-C19-4 resolution. There are 104 accepted event occurrences. The 127 open missing-obligation queue entries describe missing supported input to the current integration, not proof that no obligation records exist. Current revision/request/evidence authority still needs validation. |\\n| Full-record verification | All PDF/workbook transcriptions checked in this audit; byte/schema/chain checks and deterministic snapshot replay | No independently verified end-to-end expected business results across all retained records, including People, Merchant payments, FX, Caps, Budget and Review packets. `readiness.py:132–145` reruns the same integrator: useful consistency/tamper detection, not an independent policy oracle. Clock validation at lines 82–93 checks consistent caller-supplied timestamps, not an attached authority record. |\\n\\nThe existing skill's final limitations paragraph (`skills/travel-expense-claim/SKILL.md:78`) is stale relative to implemented replay/reconciliation/report tooling; it should not be treated as the definitive coverage inventory. This audit does not change it.\\n\\n## Remaining questions\\n\\n1. Where is the original assignment text/export? Its exact scope, clock requirement, batch-boundary definition, connected-path acceptance criteria and full-record expectations need direct comparison.\\n2. Does an authoritative source explicitly supply the scenario clock (date, time, timezone) and any temporal cutoffs, or does the assignment authorize packet labels alone for arrival boundaries? Who supplies that authority?\\n3. Can the original Notion policy body/version be made available through an authorized native export? Are there access records beyond the retained shell-failure narrative?\\n4. What is the authoritative linkage from native accepted/adjustment/resolution records and review packets to current claim-revision obligations and closure? Preserve existing monetary conclusions while this is established.\\n\\nNo existing source, interview, replay, readiness report, or financial output was altered.\\n'''\\nwith p.open('x',encoding='utf-8') as f:f.write(content)\\nprint(p)\\nPY\",\"max_output_tokens\":1000}));\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a13bf2-628b-75c2-a43b-f19b5878b298","create_time":1792007276.5650084}}}
