history, force-push, or modify retained source artifacts.\n\nReport the commit hash, test results, push status, and any warnings.","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a13bd7-4146-7772-a00f-d514bd3f3bd7","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1792005325.126499,"turn_id":"01a13bd7-3aa0-7972-adeb-ce27fff74914"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Continue implementing Project D in ~/travel-expense-claim.\n\nThe latest verified GitHub commit is 0f0acf3. Implement the next coding milestone: owner-specific follow-up drafts based on the existing repair queue.\n\nInspect the current repair-queue schema, report generation, and tests.\n\nGenerate a new artifact, follow-up-drafts.md, from unresolved repair items only.\n\nGroup draft messages by responsible owner, preserving claim IDs, revisions, evidence references, unresolved questions, and specific requested next actions.\n\nClearly distinguish claim-specific repairs, trip/permit repairs, and external run-level blockers.\n\nDo not generate messages for resolved items.\n\nTreat all messages as drafts requiring human review. Do not send emails or contact anyone.\n\nDo not invent email addresses, deadlines, reimbursement rules, approval decisions, payment events, or missing evidence.\n\nPreserve independent claim processing and existing immutable snapshot history.\n\nAdd automated tests for grouping, traceability, resolved-item exclusion, and human-review labeling.\n\nRun the complete test suite and git diff --check.\n\nRegenerate the offline replay outputs in a new retained-input run without overwriting prior artifacts.\n\nImplement the code now, not just a plan.\n\nDo not commit or push yet. Report the files changed, test results, generated draft count, and remaining blockers.","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a13bdd-4cde-7952-b26f-5b8f1fb8fc4b","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1792005721.3107705,"turn_id":"01a13bdd-4a66-7290-846d-c41f22484a9c"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Preserve the completed Project D owner-specific follow-up draft milestone in Git and Entire.io.\n\n    Inspect git status and the exact source diff.\n\n    Stage ONLY:\n\n        README.md\n\n        src/travel_claim/follow_up.py\n\n        src/travel_claim/offline_replay.py\n\n        tests/test_follow_up.py\n\n        tests/test_offline_replay.py\n\n    Do NOT stage artifacts/runs/ or unrelated files.\n\nRun git diff --cached --check and the complete pytest suite.\n\nIf validation passes, commit with the message:\nfeat: generate owner-specific Project D follow-up drafts\n\nPush the commit to origin main, allowing the existing Entire.io hooks to run.\n\nVerify the remote main commit and the Entire.io checkpoint references.\n\nDo not rewrite history, force-push, or alter retained source artifacts.\n\nReport the commit hash, test results, push status, and any warnings.","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a13be0-1cec-73d0-bc1d-1b0b7751b362","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1792005905.6445575,"turn_id":"01a13be0-1976-7601-8575-d05090b6f8b0"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Continue implementing Project D in ~/travel-expense-claim from verified commit efc7e91.\n\nImplement a submission-readiness validator, not just a plan.\n\nInspect the assignment requirements, existing skill, source manifest, snapshot chain, CLI, and generated outputs.\n\nAdd a reusable validation command that checks source completeness, manifest integrity, snapshot chain integrity, required output files, exact claims.csv header, report and repair-queue traceability, follow-up draft status, and Entire.io evidence where locally verifiable.\n\nDistinguish PASS, BLOCKED, and FAIL. A missing external policy or unconfirmed authoritative case clock must be BLOCKED, never silently passed.\n\nExplicitly distinguish retained-input offline replay from a fresh native-source execution. Never report the offline replay as final live-source compliance.\n\nProduce a machine-readable readiness report and a human-readable summary, with evidence paths and specific next actions.\n\nAdd automated tests for complete, incomplete, blocked, and tampered cases.\n\nRun the full test suite and git diff --check.\n\nValidate the latest retained-input replay without overwriting existing artifacts.\n\nDo not invent policy rules, source observations, approvals, financial conclusions, or case-clock values. Do not commit or push yet.\n\nReport changed files, test results, readiness findings, and remaining blockers.","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a13be4-8d6d-7932-9e24-6a59da779d9a","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1792006196.5898845,"turn_id":"01a13be4-8a38-7ef3-b2cb-8b3e8591f919"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Preserve the completed Project D submission-readiness validator milestone.\n\n1. Inspect `git status`, the exact source diff, and any staged changes.\n2. Stage ONLY:\n   - `README.md`\n   - `src/travel_claim/cli.py`\n   - `src/travel_claim/offline_replay.py`\n   - `src/travel_claim/readiness.py`\n   - `tests/test_readiness.py`\n3. Do not stage `artifacts/runs/`, `artifacts/verification/`, or unrelated files.\n4. Run `git diff --cached --check` and the complete pytest suite.\n5. If validation passes, commit with:\n   `feat: validate Project D submission readiness`\n6. Push to `origin main`, allowing the existing Entire.io hooks to run.\n7. Verify the remote main commit and Entire.io checkpoint references.\n\nDo not force-push, rewrite history, or modify retained artifacts.\n\nReport the exact commit hash, test results, push status, checkpoint verification, and any warnings.","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a13beb-f8b7-7ca0-99dc-37685f8b0ed4","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1792006682.8071113,"turn_id":"01a13beb-f5b1-7430-847f-20bf35334bf7"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Continue Project D from verified commit f98e044.\n\nI have now provided the original Project D assignment requirements. Before implementing more features, audit the existing interview and retained native-source evidence for the two outstanding blockers.\n\nSearch the complete original interview export and all retained source content for an explicit authoritative case clock, including date, time, timezone, and arrival-batch boundaries.\n\nExamine the retained sources.json and source observations for the Notion reimbursement policy. Determine exactly what content was accessible and what was not.\n\nDistinguish an explicit scenario fact from a document timestamp, an inference, or an invented default.\n\nCompare the assignment requirements against the current implementation and identify material coverage gaps, especially the connected ordinary path, corrections, independent cancellations, financial closure, and full-record verification.\n\nProduce a concise evidence audit with exact file paths, source references, findings, and remaining questions.\n\nDo not alter existing source artifacts, change financial conclusions, or invent missing policy rules or case-clock values.\n\nDo not commit or push.\n\nThis is an evidence audit, not a request to automate the stakeholder interview. Use only the interview export already created by me and the existing retained evidence.","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a13bf2-65cf-7fc3-a667-222215bed839","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1792007103.9514213,"turn_id":"01a13bf2-628b-75c2-a43b-f19b5878b298"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Continue implementing Project D in ~/travel-expense-claim from commit f98e044.\n\nThe evidence audit identified a material integration gap: the retained workbook contains accepted-obligation records, but the offline replay does not consistently connect those records to claim revisions and financial reconciliation.\n\nImplement the next coding milestone now.\n\n    Inspect the existing Finance parser, native retained workbook records, finance.py, integration.py, and related tests.\n\n    Identify the exact meaning of each accepted-obligation field from source headers and retained evidence. Do not assume an accepted Finance event is equivalent to a human-approved reimbursement entitlement unless the source supports that interpretation.\n\n    Connect supported obligation records to the correct claim ID and revision, preserving event identity, source references, arrival batches, and history.\n\nReconcile supported obligations against confirmed payments, refunds, reversals, and corrections without double-counting duplicate events.\n\nDistinguish known financial facts from missing approvals, unresolved policy eligibility, and unconfirmed closure authority.\n\nRevalidate affected financial dependencies after material claim corrections, preserving unaffected records.\n\nUpdate claim projections, repair queue items, and report outputs only where supported by evidence.\n\nAdd regression tests using representative retained records, including duplicate events, corrections, and independent cancellations.\n\nRun the full test suite and git diff --check.\n\nGenerate a new labeled offline replay without overwriting prior artifacts.\n\nDo not invent approvals, payment confirmations, policy limits, or case-clock dates. Do not commit or push yet.\n\nReport changed files, tests passed, which financial facts were successfully linked, and what remains blocked.","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a13bf7-8871-7d10-8b35-9dea435e5af1","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1792007440.497253,"turn_id":"01a13bf7-84ed-7580-8f9b-351b17474778"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Preserve the completed Project D financial source reconciliation milestone.\n\n    Inspect git status, the exact source diff, and all staged changes.\n\n    Stage ONLY these files:\n\n        README.md\n\n        snapshot.schema.json\n\n        src/travel_claim/finance.py\n\n        src/travel_claim/integration.py\n\n        src/travel_claim/offline_replay.py\n\n        tests/test_retained_finance.py\n\n        tests/fixtures/retained_finance_records.json\n\n    Do not stage artifacts/runs/, artifacts/verification/, or unrelated files.\n\n    Run git diff --cached --check and the complete pytest suite.\n\n    If validation passes, commit with:\n    feat: reconcile retained Finance obligations and refunds\n\n    Push to origin main, allowing existing Entire.io hooks to run.\n\n    Verify the exact remote main commit and Entire.io checkpoint references.\n\nDo not force-push, rewrite history, or modify retained artifacts.\n\nReport the commit hash, tests passed, push verification, and any warnings.","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a13bff-70d1-7ac3-8f43-c0fef55fce73","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1792007958.737347,"turn_id":"01a13bff-6c35-74c3-badb-76002d27261c"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Continue Project D from verified GitHub commit 74fad6313b1f65ce7fbf015c3cbfa35122d09896.\n\nImplement Step 152: the connected ordinary travel-expense claim workflow.\n\nUse the existing code, original interview export, retained source records, and financial reconciliation implementation.\n\nBuild and connect these stages:\n\nTravel permission and trip-to-claim linkage.\n\nClaim revision, receipts, and supporting-evidence validation.\n\nPolicy eligibility checks, but only where actual source evidence supports them.\n\nRequired human review and approval evidence.\n\nReimbursement request and recorded Finance obligation linkage.\n\nConfirmed payments, refunds, adjustments, and outstanding balance reconciliation.\n\nFinancial closure when every required condition is supported; otherwise preserve a pending state and an owner-specific repair action.\n\nDemonstrate the workflow with an actual retained claim, showing its source references and which stages pass or remain blocked.\n\nIf no real claim can legitimately close, use a clearly labeled synthetic test fixture to verify the successful closure path. Do not fabricate approval or closure for real claims.\n\nPreserve all four arrival batches, corrections, cancellations, event histories, and previous run artifacts.\n\nAdd regression tests checking claim identities, revisions, source references, monetary amounts, and state transitions.\n\nGenerate a new labeled offline replay without overwriting previous artifacts.\n\nRun the complete pytest suite and git diff --check.\n\nDo not commit or push yet.\n\nReport changed files, the real claim demonstrated, successful and blocked transitions, test results, and remaining gaps.","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a13c07-b4f9-7913-b15e-0a8645c80fc3","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1792008500.4738793,"turn_id":"01a13c07-abde-7561-8a83-1bb986e74dae"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Preserve the completed Project D Step 152 connected ordinary workflow milestone.\n\n    Inspect git status, all source diffs, and the staging area.\n\n    Stage ONLY these ten files:\n\n        README.md\n\n        snapshot.schema.json\n\n        src/travel_claim/ordinary.py\n\n        src/travel_claim/integration.py\n\n        src/travel_claim/offline_replay.py\n\n        src/travel_claim/outputs.py\n\n        src/travel_claim/readiness.py\n\n        tests/test_ordinary.py\n\n        tests/test_offline_replay.py\n\n        tests/fixtures/ordinary_claim_c01.json\n\n    Do not stage artifacts/runs/, artifacts/verification/, or unrelated files.\n\nRun git diff --cached --check and the full pytest suite.\n\nIf validation passes, commit with:\nfeat: connect evidence-driven travel claim workflow\n\nPush to origin main using the existing Entire.io hooks.\n\nVerify the exact remote main commit and Entire.io checkpoint references.\n\nVerify the original interview and all 64 previously retained artifact files remain unchanged.\n\nDo not force-push or rewrite history.\n\nReport the commit hash, test count, push verification, checkpoint results, and any warnings.","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a13c16-ac5e-7c43-8e67-9420eb202f9d","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1792009481.310437,"turn_id":"01a13c16-a6a3-7421-b7b1-82744f6027a9"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"\u003cskills_instructions\u003e\n## Skills\nA skill is a set of local instructions to follow that is stored in a `SKILL.md` file. Below is the list of skills that can be used. Each entry includes a name, description, and a short path that can be expanded into an absolute path using the skill roots table.\n### Skill roots\n- `r0` = `/home/maxg/.codex/skills/.system`\n- `r1` = `/home/maxg/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote`\n- `r2` = `/home/maxg/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/work-pets/0.1.6/skills`\n### Available skills\n- imagegen: Generate or edit raster images when the task benefits from AI-created bitmap visuals such as photos, illustrations, textures, sprites, mockups, or transparent-background cutouts. Use when Codex should create a brand-new image, transform an existing image, or derive visual variants from references, and the output should be a bitmap asset rather than repo-native code or vector. Do not use when the task is better handled by editing existing SVG/vector/code-native assets, extending an established icon or logo system, or building the visual directly in HTML/CSS/canvas. (file: r0/imagegen/SKILL.md)\n- openai-docs: Use for Codex models/pricing, scheduled tasks, skills, settings, setup, troubleshooting, customization, automations, and self-knowledge—including 'you,' 'your,' 'this app,' or 'this coding agent' when they refer to Codex—and for OpenAI APIs/products and ChatGPT Work. Also use for model choice/migration, prompting, SDKs, Responses, Realtime, agents, evals, and Chat/Work/Codex comparisons. Do not use for generic app/software tasks that merely mention Codex. (file: r0/openai-docs/SKILL.md)\n- skill-creator: Create or update a Codex skill with appropriately scoped instructions and any needed supporting resources. (file: r0/skill-creator/SKILL.md)\n- skill-installer: Install Codex skills into $CODEX_HOME/skills from a curated list or a GitHub repo path. Use when a user asks to list installable skills, install a curated skill, or install a skill from another repo (including private repos). (file: r0/skill-installer/SKILL.md)\n- plugin-management:plugin-management: Discover and suggest relevant plugins, inspect app permissions and dependencies, and manage plugin connections or removal. Use when the user asks about plugins or when a task would materially benefit from an external app, account, service, or data source that available tools cannot access. (file: r1/plugin-management/0.1.0/skills/plugin-management/SKILL.md)\n- sites:sites: Use Sites when the user wants a website built for them (including a landing page, portfolio, dashboard, portal, tracker, hub, game, event registration, or tool), requests changes to a website already built with Sites, wants to publish a website with Sites, or wants to manage Sites hosting. (file: r1/sites/1.0.0/skills/sites/SKILL.md)\n- work-pets:create-pet: Create, repair, validate, preview, upload, or activate an animated v2 pet in ChatGPT Work mode from a character idea, brand cue, or reference image. Use for new ChatGPT Pets, custom mascots, non-pixel styles, and unattached sprite sheets requiring nine animation states plus sixteen look directions. Preserve Library artifacts and Pets MCP lifecycle behavior. Exclude real pets, standalone images, and other-app pets; clarify before invoking. (file: r2/create-pet/SKILL.md)\n- work-pets:pets: List, inspect, select, download, or delete animated pets in ChatGPT Work mode. Use for an explicit pet in ChatGPT or established ChatGPT Pets context. Exclude real-world and other-app pets; clarify before invoking. (file: r2/pets/SKILL.md)\n- work-pets:update-pet: Inspect, validate, preview, repair, or update a custom pet in ChatGPT Work mode, including its name, description, or sprite sheet. Use for a pet in ChatGPT or established ChatGPT Pets context. Exclude real-world and other-app pets; clarify before invoking. (file: r2/update-pet/SKILL.md)\n\u003c/skills_instructions\u003e","type":"input_text"},{"text":"\u003cpermissions instructions\u003e\nFilesystem sandboxing defines which files can be read or written. `sandbox_mode` is `workspace-write`: The sandbox permits reading files, and editing files in `cwd` and `writable_roots`. Editing files in other directories requires approval. Network access is restricted.\n# Escalation Requests\n\nCommands are run outside the sandbox if they are approved by the user, or match an existing rule that allows it to run unrestricted. The command string is split into independent command segments at shell control operators, including but not limited to:\n\n- Pipes: |\n- Logical operators: \u0026\u0026, ||\n- Command separators: ;\n- Subshell boundaries: (...), $(...)\n\nEach resulting segment is evaluated independently for sandbox restrictions and approval requirements.\n\nExample:\n\ngit pull | tee output.txt\n\nThis is treated as two command segments:\n\n[\"git\", \"pull\"]\n\n[\"tee\", \"output.txt\"]\n\nCommands that use more advanced shell features like redirection (\u003e, \u003e\u003e, \u003c), substitutions ($(...), ...), environment variables (FOO=bar), or wildcard patterns (*, ?) will not be evaluated against rules, to limit the scope of what an approved rule allows.\n\n## How to request escalation\n\nIMPORTANT: To request approval to execute a command that will require escalated privileges:\n\n- Provide the `sandbox_permissions` parameter with the value `\"require_escalated\"`\n- Include a short question asking the user if they want to allow the action in `justification` parameter. e.g. \"Do you want to download and install dependencies for this project?\"\n- Optionally suggest a `prefix_rule` - this will be shown to the user with an option to persist the rule approval for future sessions.\n\nIf you run a command that is important to solving the user's query, but it fails because of sandboxing or with a likely sandbox-related network error (for example DNS/host resolution, registry/index access, or dependency download failure), rerun the command with \"require_escalated\". ALWAYS proceed to use the `justification` parameter - do not message the user before requesting approval for the command.\n\n## When to request escalation\n\nWhile commands are running inside the sandbox, here are some scenarios that will require escalation outside the sandbox:\n\n- You need to run a command that writes to a directory that requires it (e.g. running tests that write to /var)\n- You need to run a GUI app (e.g., open/xdg-open/osascript) to open browsers or files.\n- If you run a command that is important to solving the user's query, but it fails because of sandboxing or with a likely sandbox-related network error (for example DNS/host resolution, registry/index access, or dependency download failure), rerun the command with `require_escalated`. ALWAYS proceed to use the `sandbox_permissions` and `justification` parameters. do not message the user before requesting approval for the command.\n- You are about to take a potentially destructive action such as an `rm` or `git reset` that the user did not explicitly ask for.\n- Be judicious with escalating, but if completing the user's request requires it, you should do so - don't try and circumvent approvals by using other tools.\n\n## prefix_rule guidance\n\nWhen choosing a `prefix_rule`, request one that will allow you to fulfill similar requests from the user in the future without re-requesting escalation. It should be categorical and reasonably scoped to similar capabilities. You should rarely pass the entire command into `prefix_rule`.\n\n### Banned prefix_rules \nAvoid requesting overly broad prefixes that the user would be ill-advised to approve. For example, do not request [\"python3\"], [\"python\", \"-\"], or other similar prefixes that would allow arbitrary scripting.\nNEVER provide a prefix_rule argument for destructive commands like rm.\nNEVER provide a prefix_rule if your command uses a heredoc or herestring. \n\n### Examples\nGood examples of prefixes:\n- [\"npm\", \"run\", \"dev\"]\n- [\"gh\", \"pr\", \"check\"]\n- [\"cargo\", \"test\"]\n The writable roots are `/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim`, `/tmp`.\n\u003c/permissions instructions\u003e","type":"input_text"},{"text":"\u003ccollaboration_mode\u003e# Collaboration Mode: Default\n\nYou are now in Default mode. Any previous instructions for other modes (e.g. Plan mode) are no longer active.\n\nYour active mode changes only when new developer instructions with a different `\u003ccollaboration_mode\u003e...\u003c/collaboration_mode\u003e` change it; user requests or tool descriptions do not change mode by themselves. Known mode names are Default and Plan.\n\n## request_user_input availability\n\nUse the `request_user_input` tool only when it is listed in the available tools for this turn.\n\nUse the `request_user_input` tool only for optional questions where the answer would materially improve the quality of the work.\n\nIf `request_user_input` returns no answers, continue with best judgment instead of asking again or treating the turn as blocked.\n\nNever use the `request_user_input` tool for permission requests or permission-related escalations.\n\u003c/collaboration_mode\u003e","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a13c1c-e45d-75a0-8b9b-bd128072e0ea","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["host_skills.instructions","permissions.instructions","collaboration_mode.instructions"],"turn_id":"01a13c1b-19a6-7c30-952c-e0611fda1bab"},"role":"developer","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"\u003cmulti_agent_role\u003eYou are `/root`, the primary agent in a team of agents collaborating to fulfill the user's goals.\n\nAt the start of your turn, you are the active agent.\nYou can spawn sub-agents to handle subtasks, and those sub-agents can spawn their own sub-agents.\nAll agents in the team, including the agents that you can assign tasks to, are equally intelligent and capable, and have access to the same set of tools.\n\nYou can use `spawn_agent` to create a new agent, `followup_task` to give an existing agent a new task and trigger a turn, and `send_message` to pass a message to a running agent without triggering a turn.\n`send_message` calls may be read by a human, so ensure they are legible. Always put proper spaces between words and/or numbers.\nChild agents can also spawn their own sub-agents.\nYou can decide how much context you want to propagate to your sub-agents with the `fork_turns` parameter.\n\nYou will receive messages in the analysis channel in the form:\n```\nMessage Type: MESSAGE | FINAL_ANSWER\nTask name: \u003crecipient\u003e\nSender: \u003cauthor\u003e\nPayload:\n\u003cpayload text\u003e\n```\nThey may be addressed as to=/root\n\nNote that collaboration tools cannot be called from inside `functions.exec`. Call `spawn_agent`, `send_message`, `followup_task`, `wait_agent`, `interrupt_agent`, and `list_agents` only as direct tool calls using the recipient shown in their tool definitions, such as `to=functions.collaboration.spawn_agent`, since they are intentionally absent from the `functions.exec` `tools.*` namespace. Available tools in `functions.exec` are explicitly described with a `tools` namespace in the developer message.\n\nAll agents share the same directory. In detail:\n- All agents have access to the same container and filesystem as you.\n- All agents use the same current working directory.\n- As a result, edits made by one agent are immediately visible to all other agents.\n\nWhen calling `wait_agent`, prefer longer waits (minutes) to avoid busy polling.\n\nThere are 4 available concurrency slots, meaning that up to 4 agents can be active at once, including you.\n\nFull-history forks (`fork_turns` omitted or `\"all\"`) inherit the parent model and reasoning effort and do not accept overrides. Only set `model` or `reasoning_effort` when explicitly requested by the user, applicable `AGENTS.md` instructions, or skill instructions; when doing so, set `fork_turns` to `\"none\"` or a positive integer string.\u003c/multi_agent_role\u003e","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a13c1c-e45d-75a0-8b9b-bd24cec1ffd4","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["multi_agent.role_instructions"],"turn_id":"01a13c1b-19a6-7c30-952c-e0611fda1bab"},"role":"developer","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"\u003cmulti_agent_mode\u003eAny earlier instruction enabling proactive multi-agent delegation no longer applies. Do not spawn sub-agents unless the user or applicable AGENTS.md/skill instructions explicitly ask for sub-agents, delegation, or parallel agent work.\u003c/multi_agent_mode\u003e","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a13c1c-e45d-75a0-8b9b-bd3a7231e77a","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["multi_agent.mode_instructions"],"turn_id":"01a13c1b-19a6-7c30-952c-e0611fda1bab"},"role":"developer","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"\u003cenvironment_context\u003e\n  \u003ccwd\u003e/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim\u003c/cwd\u003e\n  \u003cshell\u003ebash\u003c/shell\u003e\n  \u003ccurrent_date\u003e2026-10-14\u003c/current_date\u003e\n  \u003ctimezone\u003eAmerica/New_York\u003c/timezone\u003e\n  \u003cfilesystem\u003e\u003cworkspace_roots\u003e\u003croot\u003e/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim\u003c/root\u003e\u003c/workspace_roots\u003e\u003cpermission_profile type=\"managed\"\u003e\u003cfile_system type=\"restricted\"\u003e\u003centry access=\"read\"\u003e\u003cspecial\u003e:root\u003c/special\u003e\u003c/entry\u003e\u003centry access=\"write\"\u003e\u003cpath\u003e/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim\u003c/path\u003e\u003c/entry\u003e\u003centry access=\"write\"\u003e\u003cspecial\u003e:slash_tmp\u003c/special\u003e\u003c/entry\u003e\u003centry access=\"write\"\u003e\u003cspecial\u003e:tmpdir\u003c/special\u003e\u003c/entry\u003e\u003centry access=\"read\"\u003e\u003cpath\u003e/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/.git\u003c/path\u003e\u003c/entry\u003e\u003centry access=\"read\"\u003e\u003cpath\u003e/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/.agents\u003c/path\u003e\u003c/entry\u003e\u003centry access=\"read\"\u003e\u003cpath\u003e/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/.codex\u003c/path\u003e\u003c/entry\u003e\u003centry access=\"read\"\u003e\u003cpath\u003e/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/.aws\u003c/path\u003e\u003c/entry\u003e\u003c/file_system\u003e\u003c/permission_profile\u003e\u003c/filesystem\u003e\n\u003c/environment_context\u003e","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a13c1c-e45d-75a0-8b9b-bd401bc4f3d1","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["environments.environment_context"],"turn_id":"01a13c1b-19a6-7c30-952c-e0611fda1bab"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Continue Project D from verified commit 475e8e2dd2b8dd249f360095f7fae584900a9dc0.\n\nImplement Step 157: complete correction revalidation and independent trip/permit cancellation processing.\n\nInspect the original retained interview, source records, existing integration, ordinary workflow, Finance reconciliation, snapshots, and tests.\n\nTrace all material claim corrections across arrival batches and determine exactly which dependent evidence, approvals, requests, and financial states require revalidation.\n\nExecute the required rechecks rather than merely recording revalidation tasks. Preserve valid unaffected evidence and full revision history.\n\nProcess trip and permit cancellations independently of claim existence. Preserve initiation, confirmation, source references, and any unresolved financial disposition.\n\nPropagate cancellation effects to linked claims and requests without incorrectly blocking unrelated claims.\n\nDistinguish a cancelled transfer from a cash refund, and preserve actual Finance event identity and history.\n\nDemonstrate representative retained correction and cancellation cases with exact source references and before/after states.\n\nAdd regression tests for stale approvals, changed receipts, revised amounts, duplicate events, partial cancellation information, and independent no-claim cancellations.\n\nGenerate a new labeled offline replay and verification report without overwriting prior artifacts.\n\nRun the complete pytest suite, snapshot integrity checks, and git diff --check.\n\nDo not invent approvals, policy rules, cancellation confirmations, refunds, or the missing case clock. Preserve all earlier artifacts and the original interview.\n\nDo not commit or push yet.\n\nReport changed files, demonstrated cases, executed transitions, remaining blockers, and tests passed.","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a13c1b-1f15-76b0-86df-f439e8dab010","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1792009772.8211133,"turn_id":"01a13c1b-19a6-7c30-952c-e0611fda1bab"},"role":"user","type":"message"}],"replacement_history_metadata":[{"client_authored":false,"mcp_attribution":{"status":"none"},"retained_source":{"complete":true,"id":{"message_id":"msg_01a13bd3-423a-7082-8f73-b169f3b37bba","role":"user","turn_id":"01a13bd3-3cec-7081-b847-b31e41528363"},"revision":"retained_01a13bd3-423a-7082-8f73-b18698ae74cc"},"user_input_order":0},{"client_authored":false,"mcp_attribution":{"status":"none"},"retained_source":{"complete":true,"id":{"message_id":"msg_01a13bd7-4146-7772-a00f-d514bd3f3bd7","role":