ates. |\n| [POL-2026.2-browser-copy.txt:13](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/user-provided-sources/step-167/POL-2026.2-browser-copy.txt:13) | Administration checks and full department/project/period budget | **Major missing calculation** | Packets only check that cited budget IDs exist; no allocation minus net settled expenditure minus remaining commitments calculation, ledger scope, pre-batch obligations or accepted-new-request accumulation is performed. Native Budget rows 6–8 contain allocation and prior obligation/settled data. Do not subtract both the full commitment and its settled component. [ordinary.py:213](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/src/travel_claim/ordinary.py:213). | No full-ledger/no-double-subtraction tests. Budget sufficiency must remain separate from human budget-owner approval. |\n| [POL-2026.2-browser-copy.txt:13](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/user-provided-sources/step-167/POL-2026.2-browser-copy.txt:13) | Supervisor/director judgment and review context | **Partial; packet sufficiency gap** | Current packet matching compares trip business fields and expense IDs, but not calculated reviewed entitlement, complete estimate/director basis or full budget snapshot/position. Purpose/client/schedule/alternatives fields are retained. Automation correctly does not invent discretionary judgment. [ordinary.py:220](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/src/travel_claim/ordinary.py:220). | [test_ordinary.py:192](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/tests/test_ordinary.py:192) checks changed trip facts; add altered reviewed amount, estimate, budget-owner position and full packet source-version checks. |\n| [POL-2026.2-browser-copy.txt:15](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/user-provided-sources/step-167/POL-2026.2-browser-copy.txt:15) | Decision subject/version/role/source validation; return/reject/withdrawal | **Partial; state transitions missing** | Claim current revision, roles and stable actor checks exist; rejected imports remain in history. Source revisions, affected fields and reason/repair requirements are not fully validated. Return/reject stop approvals but do not execute reviewer-specific reopening or legitimate rejected/withdrawn nonpayment terminal states. [workflow.py:115](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/src/travel_claim/workflow.py:115); [ordinary.py:202](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/src/travel_claim/ordinary.py:202); [ordinary.py:318](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/src/travel_claim/ordinary.py:318). | [test_ordinary.py:213](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/tests/test_ordinary.py:213) tests conflicting review IDs; add stale source versions, wrong subject/actor, missing reason, concrete return routing, rejection/clean withdrawal and preserved paid effects. |\n| [POL-2026.2-browser-copy.txt:17](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/user-provided-sources/step-167/POL-2026.2-browser-copy.txt:17) | Material corrections and dispatch/replacement readiness | **Partial; affected authority gaps** | All current ordinary/Finance checks rerun after revision increases with before/after audit and historical decisions retained. However inherited checks omit deadlines, exceptions, budget and cancellation-aware approvals. The audit trigger is revision increase; changed source authority under an unchanged claim revision is not independently represented as correction history. No dispatched/replacement-request state machine waits for explicit Finance status. [integration.py:212](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/src/travel_claim/integration.py:212); [revalidation.py:26](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/src/travel_claim/revalidation.py:26); [integration.py:725](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/src/travel_claim/integration.py:725). | [test_revalidation.py:25](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/tests/test_revalidation.py:25) covers stale approvals/receipt/amount changes; add source-policy/authority-only change and before/after-dispatch replacement holds. |\n| [POL-2026.2-browser-copy.txt:19](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/user-provided-sources/step-167/POL-2026.2-browser-copy.txt:19) | Stable requests, shared/prepaid costs, linked adjustments | **Major missing cross-claim ledger** | Recorded request IDs and historical obligations are retained, but formal request linkage requires a separate normalized request record and explicit Finance claim revision absent from native headers. There is no cross-claim cost obligation index or subtraction of already committed/settled cost. C24 update page 3 reuses COST-03 and adds COST-24; this is a retained regression candidate, not a new entitlement result. [ordinary.py:250](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/src/travel_claim/ordinary.py:250); [finance.py:94](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/src/travel_claim/finance.py:94). | [test_retained_finance.py:31](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/tests/test_retained_finance.py:31) covers ledger observations only. Add shared cost C03/C24, stable request redelivery, original request/cost/claim adjustment links and no duplicate reimbursement. |\n| [POL-2026.2-browser-copy.txt:21](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/user-provided-sources/step-167/POL-2026.2-browser-copy.txt:21) | Finance event admission, failed/retry authorization and immutable conflicts | **Critical defects** | Cash admission checks EUR and exact nonnegative cents, but does not require a valid request, payee, directory Finance actor or event time; zero cash is allowed. Conflict recomputation excludes all occurrences including already admitted payments, reducing synthetic prior net cash 1000 to 0. Native retry-authorized events are classified but new attempts are not validated against them. [finance.py:177](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/src/travel_claim/finance.py:177); [finance.py:239](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/src/travel_claim/finance.py:239); [finance.py:268](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/src/travel_claim/finance.py:268). | [test_finance.py:85](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/tests/test_finance.py:85) currently asserts zero cash for a conflict; distinguish first-seen conflicted event from a later conflicting redelivery of admitted money. Existing synthetic payment tests omit mandatory authority fields. |\n| [POL-2026.2-browser-copy.txt:21](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/user-provided-sources/step-167/POL-2026.2-browser-copy.txt:21) | Confirmed refunds and unrecovered transfer amount | **Partial** | Only supplied refund events change cash; linked original payment and aggregate upper bound exist. Refund request/payee/actor/time are not independently validated, and no chronological admission ledger preserves an already admitted refund when later invalid refund candidates exceed the original. [finance.py:274](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/src/travel_claim/finance.py:274). | [test_finance.py:107](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/tests/test_finance.py:107) covers orphan/over-refund and [test_retained_finance.py:111](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/tests/test_retained_finance.py:111) duplicate refund. Add wrong payee/request/actor/time, sequential remaining-recoverable limit and preservation of earlier admitted refunds. |\n| [POL-2026.2-browser-copy.txt:23](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/user-provided-sources/step-167/POL-2026.2-browser-copy.txt:23) | Balance, overpayment, partial settlement and financial closure | **Partial; excessive and missing gates** | Authorized obligation minus net paid exists, including negative balances; incomplete/unclassified events hold. Closure demands extra per-claim closure_authorities and an exact timestamp, which this policy does not require as separate blanket documents. It also holds every linked cancellation permanently, even after a possible valid disposition, and generic failed/cancelled controls require synthetic resolved-ID fields. Conversely correction after acceptance/payment does not universally require explicit current Finance resolution unless it happens to be a listed control event. [finance.py:365](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/src/travel_claim/finance.py:365); [ordinary.py:277](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/src/travel_claim/ordinary.py:277); [ordinary.py:294](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/src/travel_claim/ordinary.py:294). | [test_ordinary.py:108](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/tests/test_ordinary.py:108) closes only a synthetic extra-authority path. Add policy-native closure, overpayment repair, partial settlement, negative balance, correction/post-payment resolution, late transfer reopening and no-payment closure. |\n| [POL-2026.2-browser-copy.txt:25](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/user-provided-sources/step-167/POL-2026.2-browser-copy.txt:25) | Independent progress, human authority and imported instructions as data | **Mostly covered; incomplete-policy caveat** | Integration processes claims independently and preserves owner-specific repairs. It does not send drafts, book travel or create human decisions. Imported text is transcribed data rather than execution permission. Passing local stages must not be advertised as full policy compliance while these gaps exist. [integration.py:588](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/src/travel_claim/integration.py:588); [live_run.py:44](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/src/travel_claim/live_run.py:44). | [test_ordinary.py:181](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/tests/test_ordinary.py:181) checks unaffected claim closure; add malicious imported comment/receipt text and all-policy readiness reporting. |\n| [POL-2026.2-browser-copy.txt:27](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/user-provided-sources/step-167/POL-2026.2-browser-copy.txt:27) | Cancellation actors, exact targets, initiation and strict times | **Critical defects** | Reducer admits structural confirmation before integration checks actors; wrong actor can remain confirmed in snapshot. It does not enforce no initiation/effective fields on request, full associated trip+permit target equality (trip-target projections also null the associated permit fields), strictly-later confirmation or effective==occurrence. Equal times and scheduled effective times pass synthetic probes. Blank commitment is not implemented as explicit no-commitment. [workflow.py:216](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/src/travel_claim/workflow.py:216); [workflow.py:247](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/src/travel_claim/workflow.py:247); [integration.py:371](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/src/travel_claim/integration.py:371); [integration.py:417](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/src/travel_claim/integration.py:417). | [test_revalidation.py:75](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/tests/test_revalidation.py:75) covers orphan/missing-effective/backward time but not equal time, scheduled/backdated effective, wrong actor or complete target tuples. |\n| [POL-2026.2-browser-copy.txt:29](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/user-provided-sources/step-167/POL-2026.2-browser-copy.txt:29) | Arrival dependency recheck, conflicting redelivery and authority retirement | **Critical/missing lifecycle behavior** | Sorting by arrival batch then row causes a confirmation before its request in row/delivery order to remain unresolved even after the initiation is present. Conflicting IDs are excluded wholesale, so prior admitted history can disappear. No active/retired travel-authority state prevents old approval replies or later claim versions restoring permission; current versus historical claim linkage alone is insufficient. Future observations have no case-date cutoff. [workflow.py:169](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/src/travel_claim/workflow.py:169); [workflow.py:200](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/src/travel_claim/workflow.py:200); [workflow.py:234](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/src/travel_claim/workflow.py:234); [ordinary.py:94](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/src/travel_claim/ordinary.py:94). | [test_workflow_structure.py:111](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/tests/test_workflow_structure.py:111) and [test_revalidation.py:63](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/tests/test_revalidation.py:63) cover structural lifecycle/dedup. Add deferred reference resolution, stale targets, future observations, process action conflicts and trip-versus-permit retirement/renewal. |\n| [POL-2026.2-browser-copy.txt:31](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/user-provided-sources/step-167/POL-2026.2-browser-copy.txt:31) | Requested holds, no-financial-effect and cancellation-aware eligibility/reviews | **Major missing semantics** | Linked payment readiness holds, but new-arrangement pause is not modeled. Every process is projected financial_status=unresolved; supported no-cost/no-obligation processes cannot resolve to no-financial-effect. Cancellation exceptions are observed without covered-issue validation/application. Packet Travel cancellation references is present in retained evidence but ignored by current review checks. Existing lawful arrangements must not gain permit_unapproved solely from later cancellation. [integration.py:330](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/src/travel_claim/integration.py:330); [integration.py:380](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/src/travel_claim/integration.py:380); [integration.py:391](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/src/travel_claim/integration.py:391); [ordinary.py:220](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/src/travel_claim/ordinary.py:220). | [test_revalidation.py:63](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/tests/test_revalidation.py:63) currently expects an unresolved no-claim process; absence of a claim alone is not proof of no financial effect. Add scoped disposition coverage, post-confirmation packets, preserved planned end/initial filings and same-day ambiguity. |\n| [POL-2026.2-browser-copy.txt:33](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/user-provided-sources/step-167/POL-2026.2-browser-copy.txt:33) | Pre-acceptance versus post-acceptance/payment cancellation | **Major lifecycle over-hold and missing resolution linkage** | Ordinary closure rejects any affected cancellation, regardless of timing/disposition. Policy permits pre-acceptance cancellation with bound disposition and fresh cancellation-aware reviews without a separate Finance resolution event. Post-acceptance/payment requires explicit resolution while preserving original obligations and observing late money; business confirmation cannot release commitment. Current Finance-cancelled classification does not update an accepted-commitment state, and financial_status never becomes resolved. [ordinary.py:309](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/src/travel_claim/ordinary.py:309); [integration.py:380](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/src/travel_claim/integration.py:380); [finance.py:81](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/src/travel_claim/finance.py:81). | [test_retained_finance.py:95](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/tests/test_retained_finance.py:95) distinguishes transfer cancellation/refund and [test_revalidation.py:108](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/tests/test_revalidation.py:108) retains dispositions; add both timing branches, original-request late transfer, post-payment refund/resolution agreement and eventual supported cancellation closure. |\n\n## Prioritized findings\n\n1. **Admission/history defects:** prevent unsupported Finance money and wrong-actor/invalid-time cancellation admission; preserve previously admitted money and process history on conflicting redelivery. These are correctness defects, not newly missing human approvals.\n2. **Policy calculations:** implement native category/personal-zero behavior, integer cap units, EUR=1, line half-up FX, date-based deadlines and scoped Finance exceptions. Budget accounting and shared-cost/prepayment exclusion are missing.\n3. **Authority/lifecycle:** validate complete review packets/source versions, permit director/combined-role rules, returns/rejections/withdrawals, dispatch/replacement holds and cancellation authority retirement. Add the separate pre-/post-acceptance cancellation resolution paths.\n4. **Source/clock integration:** retain user-copy provenance and derive reviewed policy definitions without masquerading as native HTTP content; represent date+zone without invented time. Revisit extra exact-clock/per-claim closure-authority gates now that policy completion conditions are supplied.\n\n## Resolved knowledge blockers versus remaining evidence\n\n**Resolved for this audit:** the exact supplied policy wording and POL-2026.2 identifier; policy definition of category eligibility, amounts/caps/FX, roles/threshold, filing, correction, money and cancellation rules; the authoritative case date and timezone; the general financial-completion rule. The earlier “policy unknown” and “clock completely unknown” descriptions are historical evidence, not current audit findings. No earlier run is retroactively reclassified.\n**Still not established:** a fresh programmatic native Notion read and independent remote completeness; a provenance-aware adapter linking this browser copy to future runs; implementation coverage; any missing claim-specific receipt/payment/rate/cap/role/review/exception/status/refund/resolution evidence; discretionary human decisions; timing only where actually material. Do not demand a separate generic facilitator closure approval or fabricated timestamp merely because the old synthetic implementation expects those fields.\n**Concrete retained cases (observations, not new closure decisions):**\n- C121: claim-updates-binder PDF page 6 contains the TC121-bound Finance exception; Review ledger/Review packets rows 307–309 contain r2 reviews with TC121 context; Travel cancellations rows 8–9 show the permit process; Finance activity row 224 first accepts REQ-C121 on 2026-09-13 and row 225 settles on 2026-09-14, after the 2026-09-12 confirmation. This is the policy line-33 pre-acceptance branch candidate. Absence of a separate resolution event is not automatically an external defect for that branch; full source validation and the other policy checks still remain.\n- C19: claim-updates-binder PDF page 7 and Review ledger/Review packets rows 310–312 contain current TC019 disposition/context. Finance activity rows 44, 22–25 retain acceptance, payment, adjustment, linked refund and resolution. Those observations exist and must be semantically validated, not replaced by invented new approvals/events.\n- C18: claim-updates-binder PDF page 4 reassigns T18 to T18B. Review ledger contains r1 decisions at rows 47–49 but no C18 r2 claim decisions in the retained input. Current r2 reviews are an independent missing scenario input; old-trip Finance request REQ-C18 and late payment must remain observable. Do not let the historical cancellation retire the different T18B travel basis.\n- Source anchors above refer to `artifacts/runs/project-d-live-20261014T204849Z/parsed-records.json` and its byte-bound PDFs/XLSX in `sources/`; workbook SHA-256 `3cf8b225b20b9ac99b8d493b14a95fdde261d677aab77daaf399a37fb4dcef2f`. The audit does not change existing allowed/paid/balance/status projections.\n\n## Validation and preservation\n\n- **131 existing tests passed in 23.34 seconds; `git diff --check` passed.** Tests were rerun unchanged. Passing existing tests does not establish compliance with the recovered policy; several synthetic fixtures deliberately omit newly grounded mandatory fields, and the conflict test currently codifies blanket cash exclusion.\n- Nine read-only synthetic diagnostic probes are retained in `artifacts/verification/project-d-step167-audit-probes.json`; they do not represent real claims or new automated regression tests. No implementation or test files were edited.\n- **All 120 pre-audit files are verified byte-for-byte unchanged**, including raw policy bytes, all earlier artifact bytes and the original interview. Tracked implementation and staging area have no changes. No new live capture, replay, financial projection, commit or push is performed.\n\nStop point: evidence audit complete. Findings require a separately authorized implementation milestone; no fixes applied here.\n     1\t---\n     2\tname: travel-expense-claim\n     3\tdescription: Prepare traceable Alderbridge travel claim reviews, independent trip and permit work, repair drafts, and financial reconciliation from freshly retrieved native sources and authorized batch decisions. Never approve, invent evidence, send requests, book travel, or move money.\n     4\t---\n     5\t\n     6\t# Alderbridge Travel Expense Claim Skill\n     7\t\n     8\t## Inputs\n     9\t\n    10\t- A new run ID and the scenario's supplied case clock.\n    11\t- The five native source locations listed below, retrieved afresh on every new run.\n    12\t- The authorized arrival batch and its boundary/version, supplied by the exercise operator.\n    13\t- Any prior retained snapshot only when performing an explicitly labeled offline replay; this never substitutes for fresh reads on a new run.\n    14\t- Human reviewer, employee, and Finance responses only when present in supplied records.\n    15\t\n    16\tNever infer an approval, exception, source value, reply, or payment from a missing record.\n    17\t\n    18\t## Invocation\n    19\t\n    20\tInstall as described in the repository [README](../../README.md). Perform fresh acquisition and transcription for a new run with:\n    21\t\n    22\t```bash\n    23\ttravel-claim live-run <new-run-id> artifacts/runs/<new-run-id> [--case-clock '<authoritative scenario timestamp>']\n    24\t```\n    25\t\n    26\tThis attempts all five original sources, retains response evidence and emits `live-run.json`/`.md`. It never accepts prior inputs or consumes batches when clock/policy authority is missing. Direct Notion HTML cannot establish a complete policy block-tree read; authenticated native access remains required. Retrieval timestamps are observations, never an invented case clock. Source-only capture is also available:\n    27\t\n    28\t```bash\n    29\ttravel-claim capture-sources <run-id> artifacts/runs/<run-id> --case-clock '<scenario ISO timestamp>'\n    30\t```\n    31\t\n    32\tInspect the manifest status and verify retained bytes before processing a batch:\n    33\t\n    34\t```bash\n    35\ttravel-claim verify-sources artifacts/runs/<run-id>\n    36\ttravel-claim parse-retained artifacts/runs/<run-id>\n    37\t```\n    38\t\n    39\tSource-only acquisition does not consume a batch. Batch processing must preserve supplied arrival boundaries and use the public [`snapshot.schema.json`](../../snapshot.schema.json). Current foundation helpers are documented in [`travel_claim.sources`](../../src/travel_claim/sources.py), [`travel_claim.parsing`](../../src/travel_claim/parsing.py), [`travel_claim.workflow`](../../src/travel_claim/workflow.py), [`travel_claim.snapshots`](../../src/travel_claim/snapshots.py), and [`travel_claim.outputs`](../../src/travel_claim/outputs.py).\n    40\t\n    41\t`parse-retained` hash-verifies the run before transcribing the PDFs and workbook into page- and row-referenced records. The extracted source text and workbook values are evidence only. If the case clock or policy source is unavailable, leave it unresolved and do not consume policy-dependent decisions.\n    42\t\n    43\tStructural workflow helpers preserve every claim, trip and permit revision; identify whether a supplied decision references a found historical or latest source revision without applying its outcome; deduplicate repeated event IDs and report payload conflicts; and track independent trip/permit cancellation states. They do not calculate policy results. Repair items can be updated with new owner, evidence, reason, action, and subject revision while preserving prior updates. Revision advances reopen the item.\n    44\t\n    45\t## Native sources\n    46\t\n    47\t1. [Travel reimbursement policy — POL-2026.2](https://private-pecorino-70e.notion.site/REDACTED)\n    48\t2. [Initial claim binder](https://drive.google.com/file/d/13sRj6s_kMeP1hsYGstWWmfkb6SCN1YOS/view)\n    49\t3. [Claim updates binder](https://drive.google.com/file/d/REDACTED/view)\n    50\t4. [Receipt binder](https://drive.google.com/file/d/REDACTED/view)\n    51\t5. [Travel registers workbook](https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/REDACTED/edit)\n    52\t\n    53\tRetain complete relevant bytes actually accessed. A response code or URL alone does not establish content access. Record partial and unavailable scopes separately. Never silently reuse a previous run's bytes in a new run.\n    54\t\n    55\t## Processing and authority boundaries\n    56\t\n    57\t1. Bind every fact and decision to its source, subject, and revision.\n    58\t2. Hold only claims or dependent work affected by missing/conflicting facts. Continue independent valid work. Each unresolved item retains subject type and exact ID, revision, missing/conflicting fact, responsible owner, source reference, and next action.\n    59\t3. Preserve every claim revision. After a material correction, use the corrected revision and revalidate affected evidence, calculations, trip/permit consistency, approvals, and Finance authorization. Preserve unrelated evidence only where it remains valid. Retain historical commitments, transfers, and refunds; do not use stale approvals as current.\n    60\t4. Keep trip cancellation and permit cancellation as independent processes, including processes without claims. Cancellation does not itself erase entitlement or Finance obligations. Record Finance disposition and monetary resolution separately when required.\n    61\t5. Use the owner-grouped local repair queue. Keep each item individually traceable. A partial response may resolve only the item(s) explicitly matched and supported by retained source evidence; unanswered, insufficient, or conflicting items stay open. Draft locally; never send requests.\n    62\t6. Preserve immutable Finance event identifiers. Reconcile only confirmed transfers and refunds. Do not duplicate events or obligations on replay.\n    63\t7. The provisional financial closure choice is to require current evidence and approvals, agreement between reviewed entitlement and authorized obligation, net confirmed payment equal to the authorized amount, required Finance resolutions, and no blocking issue. After the first actual processing result, pause for the operator to inspect reconciliation and unresolved cases and confirm final completion conditions and delivery scope.\n    64\t\n    65\tPeople retain business approval, exception, and payment authority. This Skill prepares work, validates supplied decisions, and records effects; it does not make discretionary approval recommendations, create replies, issue Finance exceptions, book travel, or move funds.\n    66\t\n    67\t## Outputs\n    68\t\n    69\t- `artifacts/runs/<run-id>/sources.json` and retained source bytes.\n    70\t- One immutable schema-valid `batches/<batch-id>.json` per consumed batch, with relative source and predecessor paths plus SHA-256 hashes.\n    71\t- `claims.csv` with exactly `claim_id,revision,trip_id,status,allowed_cents,paid_cents,balance_cents,next_owner,reason`.\n    72\t- Owner-grouped local repair queue with individual items and run/batch update history.\n    73\t- `report.md` describing completed work, obligations, unresolved items, owners, next actions, cancellations without claims, and source/run references.\n    74\t\n    75\tUnknown money is null in JSON and blank in CSV. Zero is a known amount. Never label incomplete work complete.\n    76\t\n    77\t## Errors, retries, and limitations\n    78\t\n    79\t- On source failure, retain the attempt URL, locator, UTC observation time, error, and missing scope. Hold only affected work and report its owner and resumption evidence.\n    80\t- On partial source access, retain usable pieces and explicitly list missing scope. Do not claim the source was fully read.\n    81\t- On invalid or conflicting batch data, preserve the input evidence, reject admission of unsupported effects, and create an owned issue; do not silently discard it.\n    82\t- Retry a failed fresh retrieval only by making another live request and recording the new observation. Do not overwrite a prior run's manifest, retained bytes, or sealed snapshot.\n    83\t- On replay, preserve obligation/event identity and money; detect conflicting content under an existing event ID.\n    84\t- Implemented paths include fresh HTTP/export acquisition and source-bound transcription, plus explicitly offline connected claim, correction, independent cancellation and Finance replay. New source manifests retain response observations and partial/error bodies. Complete native policy access, rule interpretation, authoritative case clock and supported completion authority remain required for final live business compliance; acquisition or offline integrity alone does not establish it.\n     1\t# Alderbridge travel expense claims\n     2\t\n     3\tThis repository is the Project D implementation for Alderbridge Consulting. The [Project D task](https://private-pecorino-70e.notion.site/REDACTED) and [snapshot schema](snapshot.schema.json) define the deliverables and retained-state contract. The [public template](https://github.com/GitRollTraining/travel-expense-claim) is the repository starting point.\n     4\t\n     5\t## Interview rule\n     6\t\n     7\tYou conduct the stakeholder interview yourself, and the questions are yours. Do not connect a coding agent or any other AI to the interview to run, script, or automate it. The interview transcript is assessed together with the code; a project whose interview was run by an agent is not scored.\n     8\t\n     9\tKeep one final complete original Work Sim Markdown export per interview session under `interviews/`. Do not rewrite it. If an export is unavailable, contact the facilitator.\n    10\t\n    11\t## Setup\n    12\t\n    13\tRequires Python 3.11 or later and pip.\n    14\t\n    15\t```bash\n    16\tpython3 -m venv .venv\n    17\tsource .venv/bin/activate\n    18\tpython -m pip install --upgrade pip\n    19\tpython -m pip install -e '.[test]'\n    20\t```\n    21\t\n    22\t## Foundation commands\n    23\t\n    24\tEvery new capture invokes all five native URLs again. The implementation has no cached/offline fallback. The caller supplies a case clock from the authorized scenario inputs; omit it only for source-acquisition-only capture before a batch clock is available.\n    25\t\n    26\t```bash\n    27\ttravel-claim capture-sources <run-id> artifacts/runs/<run-id> --case-clock '<scenario ISO timestamp>'\n    28\ttravel-claim verify-sources artifacts/runs/<run-id>\n    29\ttravel-claim parse-retained artifacts/runs/<run-id>\n    30\t```\n    31\t\n    32\t`capture-sources` writes `sources.json` and retains usable responses under `sources/`. It exits `0` only when all five source scopes were read, `2` when one or more are unavailable/partial, and `1` on a capture or output error. A successful HTTP response is not considered a read unless the expected source content is present and structurally usable. Existing run manifests and retained files are not overwritten. Google Drive PDFs and the workbook use native export routes; the manifest keeps the provided native URL, source locator, observed UTC time/version, retrieval outcome, scope and retained-byte SHA-256.\n    33\t\n    34\tWhen source access is partial, record usable pieces as `read` and inaccessible pieces as `unavailable`; unavailable entries have null path/hash/version and a specific error. Do not substitute prior downloads. A replay from retained inputs must be explicitly implemented and labeled as offline replay.\n    35\t\n    36\t`parse-retained` first verifies the manifest and retained-byte hashes, then writes `parsed-records.json`. PDF text stays grouped by page, workbook data stays grouped by tab and row, and every extracted record includes its source ID, SHA-256 and page/row locator. This is a transcription layer; it does not evaluate policy or infer claim outcomes. Its exit status is `2` for partial inputs or an unavailable case clock and `1` for a verification or parsing error.\n    37\t\n    38\t### Explicit offline replay\n    39\t\n    40\tWhen source retrieval is unavailable, replay only the previously retained and hash-verified inputs with an explicit offline command:\n    41\t\n    42\t```bash\n    43\ttravel-claim offline-replay artifacts/runs/<retained-run-id>\n    44\t```\n    45\t\n    46\tThis command does **not** make native-source requests. It checks that parsed records bind the current `sources.json`, integrates arrival batches in numeric order, and writes immutable `batches/arrival-NNN.json` snapshots linked by predecessor hashes. It writes the latest `claims.csv`, `repair-queue.json`, `offline-replay-state.json`, `report.md`, and `follow-up-drafts.md` alongside that run's retained inputs. The report, sidecar, and each snapshot label the result as an offline replay. Existing batch chains or output files are never overwritten; start a separately identified retained run to create a new chain. When the case clock is unresolved, snapshots retain the blocker and are only structural replay projections; no clock-dependent, policy-dependent, approval, or entitlement result is inferred.\n    47\t\n    48\tThe report separates open claim repairs and trip/permit repairs by responsible owner from run-level external blockers. Each repair retains its subject, revision, source references, opening run/batch, and next action. Resolved items remain in the full queue history. `follow-up-drafts.md` contains one unsent message per responsible owner, with separate claim, trip/permit, and external-blocker sections. Only unresolved items produce drafts; every message requires human review and retains reasons, requested actions, and evidence references. Independent valid records continue processing while other claims remain unresolved.\n    49\t\n    50\tOnce authorized batch state has been processed, pass only evidence-derived state to the snapshot sealer. The first batch has no predecessor; every later batch references the previous snapshot and exact SHA-256. Binding paths are relative to the run directory (`sources.json`, `batches/<prior-batch>.json`).\n    51\t\n    52\t```bash\n    53\ttravel-claim seal-batch artifacts/runs/<run-id> <batch-id> state.json --run-id <run-id> [--predecessor artifacts/runs/<run-id>/batches/<prior-batch>.json]\n    54\ttravel-claim verify-batch artifacts/runs/<run-id> artifacts/runs/<run-id>/batches/<batch-id>.json\n    55\ttravel-claim write-claims-csv current-claims.json artifacts/runs/<run-id>/claims.csv\n    56\t```\n    57\t\n    58\tThe state input is a JSON object with `claims`, `requests`, `travel_cancellations`, `admitted_event_ids`, and `issues` arrays matching `snapshot.schema.json`. No approval, Finance event, or claim answer is created by these foundation commands. The CSV writer accepts one current row per claim and emits exactly:\n    59\t\n    60\t```text\n    61\tclaim_id,revision,trip_id,status,allowed_cents,paid_cents,balance_cents,next_owner,reason\n    62\t```\n    63\t\n    64\tUnknown money is `null` in state and blank in CSV; zero remains `0`.\n    65\t\n    66\tThe policy-neutral structural APIs are in `travel_claim.workflow`: they retain claim/trip/permit revision histories, match decisions to exact subject revisions without applying outcomes, deduplicate immutable event IDs while surfacing payload conflicts, and reduce independent trip/permit cancellation requests and confirmations. Cancellation reductions have no claim or Finance side effects.\n    67\t\n    68\tThe owner-grouped repair queue API is in `travel_claim.outputs`. Queue items retain their item and subject identity, type/revision, source references, reason, owner, next action, run/batch history, and a local unsent draft marker. Partial response validation is item-specific and atomic; revision updates reopen affected work, preserve prior response history and recalculate the owner grouping. Unsupported, conflicting, insufficient, or unanswered items stay open.\n    69\t\n    70\t## Verification\n    71\t\n    72\t```bash\n    73\tpython -m pytest\n    74\t```\n    75\t\n    76\tSnapshot validation can also be run directly:\n    77\t\n    78\t```bash\n    79\ttravel-claim validate-snapshot artifacts/runs/<run-id>/batches/<batch-id>.json\n    80\t```\n    81\t\n    82\t`verify-batch` also checks the source-manifest bytes, predecessor bytes, and cancellation-to-issue references. Do not overwrite a sealed snapshot; create a new run with a documented link if later processing changes its content.\n    83\t\n    84\t## Connected ordinary workflow (Step 152)\n    85\t\n    86\tEach offline arrival batch now connects exact claim/trip/employee identity, current-revision expense lines, receipts and settled merchant proof, travel permission, eligibility, human review packets, explicit reimbursement requests, Finance obligations, cash reconciliation and financial closure. `ordinary-workflow.json` retains every claim's stage results and all batch transitions; `ordinary-workflow-demo.md` demonstrates C01 when present. `report.md` summarizes the same stages. Every blocked stage produces an owner-specific unsent repair action. Supported rechecks resolve only their matching items, with an explicit local evidence-validation event rather than an invented human reply.\n    87\t\n    88\tThe retained C01 fixture matches trip T01, receipt R-COST-01, merchant transaction M-COST-01, three observed review packets and request ledger REQ-C01. Its recorded ledger balances, but permission, policy eligibility, approval completeness, exact request/revision authority and closure remain blocked. A matched receipt is evidence, not automatic reimbursement eligibility. Every correction starts evaluation of the current revision again and preserves earlier decisions, cash and stage history. Independent cancellations block only affected claims' ordinary closure paths.\n    89\t\n    90\tActual native policy content is still unavailable. The evaluator never extracts policy rules from an interview summary, Finance acceptance, a cap table alone, or a keyword in HTML. It accepts only explicitly supplied, source-hash-matched normalized `workflow_policy_rules` records for its supported `exact-paid-costs-v1` rule set. Definitions specify receipt/settlement requirements, eligible/uncapped categories, FX date basis, required human roles (and any explicit director threshold), permission requirements and completion conditions. Unsupported or incomplete definitions remain blocked. International permission additionally needs exact permit revisions, named reviewers and expressly UTC commitment/approval timing; local date-only commitment cells cannot supply a missing timezone.\n    91\t\n    92\tClosure also needs a source-backed exact-revision `reimbursement_requests` record, a matching explicitly revision-bound Finance obligation, current required approval evidence, exact entitlement/authorized-obligation/net-cash agreement, an authoritative clock and source-backed `closure_authorities` confirmation of completion conditions. Failed/cancelled/pending Finance dependencies cannot silently disappear. The current native records do not supply those missing authorities. Nothing sends requests or makes discretionary human decisions.\n    93\t\n    94\t`tests/test_ordinary.py` contains an explicitly labeled **synthetic-test** successful path (pending → closed after confirmed payment), missing-condition regressions, correction reopening, and an unrelated cancellation. Its synthetic policy/clock/request/authority records are confined to the test fixture and are never substituted into real replay evidence. The supported rule set does not claim to implement the complete inaccessible native policy, its exceptions, or unprovided rule definitions.\n    95\t\n    96\t## Recorded Finance obligations\n    97\t\n    98\tOffline replay connects Finance ledger observations to claim IDs and request IDs, retaining event IDs, arrival deliveries, source cells, and obligation history. The retained `Finance activity` headers mean:\n    99\t\n   100\t| Retained field | Interpretation used by replay |\n   101\t| --- | --- |\n   102\t| Activity reference | Immutable event identity; identical redeliveries count once. |\n   103\t| Arrival batch | Delivery membership, separate from business event time. |\n   104\t| Activity type | Recorded event vocabulary; accepted/adjustment/resolution carry obligation observations, while settled/refund carry confirmed cash. |\n   105\t| Request reference / Claim reference | Explicit request and claim links; neither encodes an explicit revision. |\n   106\t| Finance officer / Payee ID | Recorded officer and payee identities, not evidence that every required human approval is present. |\n   107\t| Currency / Amount (EUR) | Currency and cash amount; only sufficiently supported EUR cash is reconciled. |\n   108\t| Original activity reference | Refund linkage to the original confirmed payment. |\n   109\t| Obligation (EUR) | Recorded commitment amount; observations are not summed as additional cash or assumed reimbursement entitlement. |\n   110\t| Reason / Activity time (UTC) | Retained description and UTC event time; neither establishes a scenario case clock. |\n   111\t\n   112\tThe interview's acceptance description is retained at `interviews/project-d-travel-expense-claim-20261013-2210.md:425`; native headers and examples are retained in `Finance activity`, row 5 and rows 23–25/33. Native Finance rows have no claim-revision column. Replay therefore records the source-observed revision context at **first delivery**, leaving the explicit revision null. Redelivery never rebinds an old event to a corrected revision. An explicit `Claim revision` field, if supplied in source records, is checked against retained revision history.\n   113\t\n   114\tThe sidecar and optional claim `finance_evidence` projection expose recorded obligation history and `recorded_ledger_balance_cents` (recorded obligation minus confirmed net cash for the same request/payee). Adjustments/resolutions record amounts, not additive payment events. Unknown ordering, conflicting events, missing links, or undefined reversal semantics leave the affected ledger difference unresolved. Transfer cancellation does not itself erase the obligation or reverse cash. Corrections preserve historical cash/commitments and reopen revision-dependent review; unrelated claim evidence remains intact.\n   115\t\n   116\tThis ledger arithmetic is separate from current-revision authorization and reviewed entitlement. Without separately supported authorization, `allowed_cents` and `balance_cents` remain null/blank and claims remain pending. A zero ledger difference does not establish policy eligibility, complete approvals, or closure. The exact CSV header is unchanged; `report.md` explains recorded financial facts separately from unresolved repairs and external blockers.\n   117\t\n   118\t## Submission-readiness validation\n   119\t\n   120\t```bash\n   121\ttravel-claim validate-readiness artifacts/runs/<run-id> --output-dir artifacts/verification/<new-validation-id>\n   122\t```\n   123\t\n   124\tThis read-only command writes `readiness.json` and `readiness.md` in a new directory outside the retained run. It checks source scope and byte hashes, input bindings, the complete snapshot chain, required files, the exact CSV contract, evidence-derived report/queue/draft traceability, and local Entire.io checkpoint references for HEAD. It does not contact native sources or verify remote synchronization. Existing validation directories are never overwritten.\n   125\t\n   126\tEach check is `PASS`, `BLOCKED`, or `FAIL`; exit codes are 0, 2, and 1 respectively. Integrity failures take precedence over blockers. Missing policy content and an unconfirmed authoritative case clock remain `BLOCKED`, even when retained artifacts are intact. An offline replay always remains blocked for final live-source submission. This validator covers the retained repository requirements and never certifies policy eligibility, approvals, financial closure, full native assignment compliance, or fresh execution from replay artifacts.\n   127\t\n   128\t## Current implementation boundary\n   129\t\n   130\tThe implementation supports fresh source capture, byte-bound manifests, loss-aware transcription, schema/hash-checked snapshots, policy-neutral arrival-ordered replay, source-classified Finance reconciliation, exact CSV output, and local owner-grouped repair tracking. `offline-replay` is a structural replay only: the inaccessible reimbursement policy and unconfirmed case clock keep policy conclusions, approval readiness, entitlement, and closure unresolved. Human replies are not imported automatically, and the command never sends repair drafts or moves money. Do not treat an offline replay as a fresh source read or completed claim processing.\n   131\t\n   132\tIf native evidence is unavailable or a business definition conflicts, record the affected scope and escalate to the exercise operator. People retain approval, exception, and payment authority. This tooling never sends requests, books travel, or moves money.\n   133\t\n   134\t## Step 157: correction and independent cancellation rechecks\n   135\t\n   136\tEvery corrected revision executes the connected evidence, calculation, trip/permit, human-review, request and Finance checks again. `material_corrections` retains changed field and expense-line facts, unchanged cost identities, excluded historical decisions, before-state evidence, and each executed batch result. A changed receipt invalidates readiness even when the amount is unchanged. Cash events and prior revision history remain intact; a passed receipt recheck cannot manufacture new approvals or current request authority.\n   137\t\n   138\t`correction-cancellation-verification.json` and `.md` demonstrate all retained corrections and independent trip/permit processes with source references, before/after cash facts, executed check results and next actions. They are explicitly retained-input offline outputs. The readiness command compares these artifacts with a deterministic replay and checks the sealed snapshot chain.\n   139\t\n   140\tCancellation processing preserves the first event delivery, duplicate deliveries, employee/supervisor evidence, initiation/confirmation chronology, historical versus current claim links, and observed Finance request IDs. Orphan or partial confirmations remain unresolved with owned repair actions. Linked requests are held; unrelated claims continue. Explicit cancellation-bound Finance exceptions are recorded separately from policy eligibility and financial completion. A transfer cancellation never counts as a cash refund. Unknown disposition, approval, policy, case clock or closure authority remains blocked; absence of a claim does not imply absence of financial effects.\n   141\t\n   142\t## Fresh native-source acquisition and transcription\n   143\t\n   144\t```bash\n   145\ttravel-claim live-run <new-run-id> artifacts/runs/<new-run-id> [--case-clock '<authoritative scenario timestamp>']\n   146\t```\n   147\t\n   148\t`live-run` attempts all five original locations using direct HTTP and native Google PDF/XLSX export routes, then verifies the manifest and transcribes accessible content from **this invocation**. It accepts no prior-run or cache input. Every run requires a new empty directory; existing manifests, parsed inputs, response files and snapshots cannot be reused. The optional clock is caller-supplied scenario evidence; retrieval timestamps never become a case clock.\n   149\t\n   150\tNew captures use `native-source-manifest/2`. Each entry records the original source identity/URL, attempted export URL, redirect destination, HTTP status, start/end UTC observations, ETag or Last-Modified when supplied, content hash/version, accessible scope, error and missing scope. Complete response bodies are retained verbatim under `responses/`, including error pages and unusable shells; usable complete or partial documents are also bound under `sources/`. The verifier checks hashes, scope/status consistency and fresh response bindings. Legacy `/1` manifests remain readable for historical replay; they do not gain fresh-access evidence.\n   151\t\n   152\tA `read` PDF must be structurally readable with extractable text on every page. Scanned or partially readable documents require further verification. XLSX exports must contain all ten known workbook tabs; all returned bytes, tab content, cell values and numeric source evidence remain retained. Partial HTTP responses, mismatched byte counts, corrupt documents and incomplete workbook exports cannot pass complete acquisition. Usable partial sources are transcribed independently, with missing scope retained. Malformed arrival membership is reported without rounding or supplying a default.\n   153\t\n   154\tPlain HTTP access to a Notion shell does not establish policy access. Even policy-related HTML is marked **partial** because this reader cannot establish the complete authoritative block tree. The current reader has no authenticated Notion API/browser/export adapter. Complete policy access and policy rule interpretation remain required before policy-dependent execution; source bytes are never replaced by interview-derived rules or earlier downloads.\n   155\t\n   156\t`live-run.json` and `live-run.md` distinguish acquisition status from final business compliance. Exit `2` means **BLOCKED** (source access, transcription, clock or policy processing remains incomplete); exit `1` means **FAIL** for integrity/execution errors. The command performs fresh acquisition and loss-aware transcription, but does not consume arrival batches or certify final live-source claim compliance. The separate `offline-replay` command remains explicitly labeled retained-input evaluation. A successful `verify-sources` establishes integrity of recorded evidence, including failure observations, rather than completeness of all five sources.\n   157\t\n   158\t## Step 171: event integrity admission\n   159\t\n   160\tFinance reconciliation now retains an immutable `admitted_events` ledger separately from raw deliveries. Exact replay has no cash effect; later conflicting payloads retain admitted money and event references while opening a Finance hold. New admissions require source-bound request/payee/currency and directory Finance authority, exact positive cash cents, and a valid observed UTC time. Invalid and unmatched deliveries remain visible. Legacy reconciliation state containing counted cash but no admission ledger requires explicit verified migration; it cannot silently become zero.\n   161\t\n   162\tIndependent cancellation admission checks the trip employee and directory supervisor, exact initiation/process/trip/permit binding, current target revision, strictly increasing UTC occurrence and effective time equal to confirmation occurrence. Invalid or conflicting deliveries do not overwrite admitted history, and deferred references are rechecked when initiation arrives. Business cancellation produces no Finance event or cash effect.\n   163\t\n   164\tCallers supply the admission cutoff explicitly via `event_clock` (an offset-bearing timestamp, or `{\"date\": \"2026-11-15\", \"timezone\": \"Europe/Amsterdam\"}` from verified POL-2026.2 line 3). A date-only clock permits unambiguously earlier local dates; observations on the case date remain held without a supplied cutoff instant. No current-system-time, midnight or end-of-day default is used. This admission context does not change the separate eligibility, approval or closure rule implementation, and earlier outputs are never rewritten.\n   165\t\n   166\t## Step 173: paid-expense calculations and filing dates\n   167\t\n   168\t`travel_claim.expenses.load_expense_policy(path)` verifies the complete, exact\n   169\tPOL-2026.2 browser copy against the user-supplied SHA-256. Explicitly attach its\n   170\treturned binding as `parsed['expense_policy']` before calling the arrival-batch\n   171\tintegrator. The binding (including original text, hash, path and browser-copy\n   172\tprovenance) participates in replay identity. No policy is silently loaded from\n   173\tan old run, and failed native Notion observations remain unchanged. The exact\n   174\tbrowser copy is also retained byte-for-byte as a regression-test fixture.\n   175\t\n   176\tEach ordinary workflow now retains `expense_calculation`, line-level conversion\n   177\tevidence and an owned `filing_deadline` check. Matching receipt and deduplicated,\n   178\tsettled employee merchant payments establish cost; destination/currency/payment-date\n   179\tcaps apply only to lodging and meals with positive integer units. Transport and\n   180\tconference are uncapped; supported personal purchases yield zero. EUR uses 1;\n   181\tother currencies require the exact payment-date Finance rate. Decimal half-up\n   182\trounding occurs per line before summation. Missing or conflicting evidence,\n   183\tunknown categories, caps and rates remain unresolved; independent valid line\n   184\tcalculations survive a blocked claim total. Calculated amounts do not establish\n   185\tapproval, entitlement authorization, a payment event or closure.\n   186\t\n   187\tDeadlines add two calendar months to the trip end, clamp nonexistent days, and\n   188\tinclude the final day. Filing must follow established employee payment, but may\n   189\tprecede travel. Unchanged cost history retains its initial source submission;\n   190\tnew costs use their own first-submission evidence, never import time. Late filing\n   191\tretains its original finding and requires an explicit directory-Finance,\n   192\tclaim/revision/cost-bound exception covering late filing. Amount-only exceptions\n   193\tcannot waive lateness. An exception changing the calculated amount remains held\n   194\tfor separate replacement-amount review; this milestone does not implement general\n   195\texception eligibility. Multiple payment dates for one expense likewise remain\n   196\theld pending source-supported allocation, rather than choosing an FX date or cap.\n   197\tExisting permission, human approval and financial closure authority requirements\n   198\tremain in force. No earlier run is regenerated by loading this module.\n   199\t\n   200\t## Step 175: Finance exception evidence and revalidation\n   201\t\n   202\tThe verified browser policy binding now supports the native claim-page exception\n   203\tfields: `Authorizing Finance officer`, `Applies to claim`, `Applies to revision`,\n   204\t`Expense reference`, `Allowed original amount`, `Exception reason`, and\n   205\t`Covered issues`. The exception and employee directory must have retained source\n   206\thash/locator bindings; the actor must match the directory Finance officer. The\n   207\treplacement amount is in the exact bound expense's original currency and uses\n   208\tits established payment-date FX conversion. Original gross, ordinary calculated\n   209\tamount, exception wording/references and conversion evidence remain available.\n   210\tMissing, invalid, ambiguous or stale exception authority holds the affected claim.\n   211\t\n   212\tOnly exact policy issue codes or their explicit native PDF display labels are\n   213\trecognized. Native `None` and an explicit empty list mean amount-only coverage;\n   214\ta missing field, unknown label or substring match never waives eligibility.\n   215\tEach cost retains separate original findings for `late_filing`, `late_permit`,\n   216\t`permit_unapproved`, and each `travel_cancellation_id`. Cancellation coverage\n   217\trequires `Travel cancellation reference` to identify the exact current process\n   218\tand affected expense. Independent findings become separate owned repair items.\n   219\tA covered finding remains recorded as `covered_for_reimbursement`; it does not\n   220\tbecome a business approval, a confirmed cancellation, employee payment, or cash.\n   221\t\n   222\tReplacement amount changes require current revision/source-bound claim reviews\n   223\tand matching request/Finance authorization. Earlier reviews remain historical.\n   224\tSame-revision material source changes retain a hold across later batches until\n   225\treview evidence identifies the changed retained source, rather than its unchanged\n   226\tprinted binder version alone. Admitted Finance events and cancellation history\n   227\tare preserved. The earlier Step 173 replacement-amount hold is superseded by\n   228\tthese checks; unrelated budget, role and financial closure gates remain in force.\n   229\tIn particular this milestone does not remove the existing blanket closure hold\n   230\tfor linked cancellations or repair the separate native permission/closure paths.\n   231\tSame-day permit approvals versus date-only commitments retain an unresolved\n   232\tordering finding; no time of day is invented.\n   233\t\n   234\t`tests/fixtures/retained_exception_records.json` contains verbatim selected C12,\n   235\tC19 and C121 claim/receipt/workbook records from the retained live attempt, with\n   236\toriginal hashes and page/row locators. These tests validate exception application,\n   237\tnot new approvals, fresh source retrieval or real claim closure. Synthetic tests\n   238\texercise authorization recovery, stale versions, conflicts and exact replay.\n   239\t\n   240\t## Step 177: separate permit and reimbursement approvals\n   241\t\n   242\t`travel_claim.approvals` evaluates supplied review ledger replies, packets and\n   243\tdirectory actors against the verified POL-2026.2 browser binding. International\n   244\tpermits review the trip estimate; claims independently review calculated\n   245\treimbursement entitlement. Administration, budget owner and supervisor are\n   246\trequired; director is additional strictly above EUR 1,000.00. Exactly EUR\n   247\t1,000.00 does not require a director. Only the directory's same budget owner and\n   248\tsupervisor may combine one reply explicitly naming both roles. Domestic travel\n   249\trequires no permit. No discretionary decision is generated.\n   250\t\n   251\tEvery considered delivery retains its raw row, source/page/row references and\n   252\tvalidation result, including wrong actors, wrong subjects, stale revisions,\n   253\tmissing reasons, conflicting IDs and duplicate deliveries. Current authority\n   254\trequires matching source versions and review packet facts. Exact replay adds\n   255\tno authority. Returns retain a concrete employee repair action; later supplied\n   256\tcurrent approval can supersede a return by decision time, rather than import\n   257\torder. Rejection reasons and employee withdrawals are business outcomes, not\n   258\tconfirmation of financial closure. Only source-supported clean cases without\n   259\trequests, obligations or cash history receive `rejected`/`withdrawn`; existing\n   260\tfinancial effects stay held for Finance disposition.\n   261\t\n   262\tCurrent claim workflows retain `claim_approval` and `permit_approval`. Independent\n   263\tpermit evaluations also appear in `permit_approvals` in new snapshots and replay\n   264\tstate, including permits without claims. Newly parsed workbooks retain exact\n   265\tpreamble cells so printed source versions are evidenced rather than inferred.\n   266\tHistorical parsed inputs without sufficient source-version evidence remain\n   267\tblocked; earlier captures are not silently enriched or rewritten. The snapshot\n   268\twriter accepts this optional evidence while preserving legacy snapshots.\n   269\t\n   270\tMaterial trip, expense/evidence, amount, policy-source or directory changes\n   271\tinvalidate affected readiness. Same-revision changes require fresh, explicitly\n   272\tsource-bound replies from the changed evidence batch onward; new revisions cannot\n   273\treuse historical replies. Source-bound Finance exceptions only resolve their\n   274\tspecified reimbursement findings; they never approve travel arrangements.\n   275\tCurrent-basis cancellation holds arrangement authority without deleting timely\n   276\thistorical permit decisions. Same-day approval/commitment ordering remains\n   277\tunconfirmed when only a commitment date is supplied.\n   278\t\n   279\tThe retained approval fixture includes actual C01/C02/C09/C13/C14/C21/C46\n   280\tclaim, permit, directory and packet records with original references. C02's\n   281\tEUR 1,100 permit estimate and USD 600 claim illustrate the independent thresholds.\n   282\tFull budget reconciliation, broader native entitlement/request adaptation and\n   283\tfinancial closure remain separate implementation boundaries. No earlier output\n   284\tis regenerated by these changes.\n   285\t\n   286\t## Step 179: budget reconciliation and prior-cost obligations\n   287\t\n   288\tNew snapshots and replay state retain `budget_reconciliation` and a budget\n   289\thistory. The native Budget headers explicitly specify EUR. Scope is the exact\n   290\tPeople/Budget department, project and budget period. Opening ledger identities\n   291\tremain source-version bound; missing native row revision or arrival fields stay\n   292\tnull. Exact replay is inert, conflicting identities hold the affected scope and\n   293\tretain admitted opening facts, and a verified replacement source version retains\n   294\tprior history. Raw budget deliveries, including invalid entries, remain visible.\n   295\t\n   296\tAvailability is allocation minus net settled expenditure minus remaining\n   297\tcommitments. Remaining commitment is `max(commitment - net paid, 0)`; a commitment\n   298\talready includes its settled portion. The supplied opening EUR 20,000 allocation,\n   299\tEUR 3,000 obligation with EUR 2,000 settled, and EUR 2,000 unsettled obligation\n   300\ttherefore leave EUR 15,000 before new requests. Stable accepted request IDs are\n   301\tcounted once, including requests outside the current batch. Overlapping opening\n   302\tsummaries and event histories must agree or remain held. Budget calculations read\n   303\tonly the protected Finance admission ledger: accepted/adjusted request amounts,\n   304\tconfirmed payments and linked refunds stay distinct from current authorized\n   305\treimbursement obligations and outstanding balances. Refunds do not silently\n   306\tcancel an outstanding commitment. Business cancellations release no Finance\n   307\tcommitment; only admitted request-bound Finance controls change it, and late\n   308\tpayments remain expenditure after cancellation.\n   309\t\n   310\tCross-claim cost attribution requires matching employee, trip, cost, receipt,\n   311\tcurrency and gross evidence, explicit earlier-claim linkage, and an exactly\n   312\tsupported request allocation. No proportional allocation is invented for an\n   313\tambiguous aggregate or partial per-cost settlement. Previously committed/paid\n   314\tcosts are excluded from the new request while original gross and conversion\n   315\tfacts remain intact. Retained C24 explicitly links C03: its EUR 200 COST-03 is\n   316\talready attributed to REQ-C03, leaving EUR 50 for COST-24. An old commitment for\n   317\ta different cost cannot offset a corrected claim's new cost. These are ledger\n   318\tand calculation facts, not newly supplied approvals or real claim closure.\n   319\t\n   320\tFunds alone never constitute budget-owner approval. Budget-role packets must\n   321\treference the whole opening scope ledger; later accepted requests, Finance\n   322\tadjustments and material budget-source changes require a current source-bound\n   323\tbudget-owner response. Other review roles retain their existing validation.\n   324\tPermit estimates remain forecasts, not invented accepted obligations. Missing\n   325\tallocations, scope mappings, evidence or cost attribution remain unresolved, and\n   326\towner-grouped repair findings retain exact claim/version and source references.\n   327\tUnrelated scopes continue independently. The report displays scope balances and\n   328\tprepaid links; readiness compares budget state/history with deterministic replay.\n   329\tNative request authorization, Finance exceptions and financial closure rules\n   330\tremain separate gates. Earlier policy/interview files and run artifacts are not\n   331\trewritten.\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":55443830},"formatted_output":"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 21967)\nTotal output lines: 512\n\n     1\t# Project D · Alderbridge travel reimbursement policy · POL-2026.2\n     2\t\n     3\tAuthored exercise policy selected at LOG-0014, with the user-selected independent trip/permit cancellation source repair; fictional company, no legal/tax advice or real payment authority. Effective for employee cost payments during 2026. Case clock: 2026-11-15, Europe/Amsterdam. Input dates are local calendar dates; event timestamps are UTC. Read the fixed source manifest before each run. The computer's current date does not change the exercise.\n     4\t\n     5\tEmployees claim actual paid transport, conference, lodging and meal costs. A receipt describes a purchase; a linked settled merchant transaction proves employee payment. Match employee, trip, cost ID, currency and gross amount. Tax included in the gross amount is treated as cost; corporate VAT recovery is outside this workflow. Personal purchases have zero entitlement. An unknown category, missing evidence, missing rate or missing applicable cap is unresolved, never an assumed zero.\n     6\t\n     7\tTransport and conference have no category cap. Lodging uses the supplied cap per night; meals use the supplied cap per day. For each line, allowed original amount = min(supported gross amount, applicable cap × documented units); transport/conference use supported gross amount. Caps are in the expense currency for the destination and employee-payment date. Units are positive integers. No per diem or automatic allowance is payable. Apply Finance's exact payment-date EUR-per-currency-unit rate; EUR uses 1. Round each converted line to EUR cents using decimal half-up; sum rounded line amounts. Finance can issue an explicit, version-bound exception with a replacement allowed amount and reason; neither the employee nor automation invents one. Each supplied exception states actor, claim/revision, cost, replacement allowed original amount, reason and `covered_issues`. That list may explicitly resolve `late_filing`, `late_permit`, `permit_unapproved` or `travel_cancellation` for reimbursement eligibility only; an amount-only exception uses an empty list. Retain the original finding and exception reference. No exception makes an unpaid cost paid or retroactively approves arrangements.\n     8\t\n     9\tDomestic trips do not need a permit. An international trip needs its own approved permit before the first arrangement/payment commitment. Permit administration, budget owner and supervisor review the trip estimate. Claim administration, budget owner and supervisor separately review actual reimbursement. A director additionally reviews amounts strictly above EUR 1,000.00 (reviewed estimate for permit, reviewed entitlement for claim). Exactly EUR 1,000.00 does not require a director. When budget owner and supervisor are the same stable person, one response with both roles satisfies those two positions for that subject/version. Administration and director never collapse into another role. A permit response never approves a claim. A late permit does not retroactively authorize travel arrangements; Finance may explicitly resolve reimbursement eligibility while the permit exception remains recorded.\n    10\t\n    11\tCosts may be claimed as soon as the employee has paid, including before travel. The latest ordinary filing date is two calendar months after trip end, inclusive, with a nonexistent day clamped to month end. For a trip ending 31 August, the deadline is 31 October. Earlier filing is allowed after payment. A later filing requires a Finance exception or remains held. The claim's submission date, not import time, is used. Corrections retain the initial submission date for unchanged costs; newly added costs use their own first submission date.\n    12\t\n    13\tAdministration checks identity, receipt/paid proof, trip/permit, category, deadline, rates/caps, prior cost obligations and calculated amount. Budget review receives the whole department/project/period ledger: allocation less net settled expenditure less remaining commitments. A commitment includes its settled portion; do not subtract the full commitment and its settled portion again. Include prior obligations outside the current batch and all accepted new requests. Adequate funds alone are not a budget approval. Supervisor considers business purpose, client commitment, schedule coverage and permit consistency. Director considers high spend, the documented exception, alternatives and budget-owner position. Reviewers may approve, return with a concrete repair request, or reject with a reason; their discretionary judgment is supplied by the scenario, not performed by the learner.\n    14\t\n    15\tRequests and replies identify subject type, subject ID/revision, role(s), stable actor, relevant source versions and reason. Consume only the trainer-supplied decision batches and the directory's applicable actor/roles. Missing responses for otherwise complete requests wait; missing policy/input definitions are package defects. Reject wrong-role, stale-version or wrong-subject replies for current authorization. Keep rejected imports visible. A return reopens the appropriate review; explicit rejection or clean withdrawal can close with a non-payment reason.\n    16\t\n    17\tA material change to employee, trip, dates, money, policy/evidence or authority invalidates dependent review, approvals and payment readiness even if the total is unchanged. Full recheck and justified dependency recheck are both valid. Preserve old versions and decisions as history. Before dispatch, rebuild readiness from current facts. After dispatch, hold replacement requests until Finance confirms cancellation/status. Pending cancellation is not confirmed cancellation. Withdrawal never erases an actual payment.\n    18\t\n    19\tA payment request is a proposal to Finance, not a transfer. Use a stable request ID for the same obligation; replay cannot create another request or reimbursement of a previously committed/settled cost. A later final claim may contain a previously prepaid cost; link and exclude that settled obligation, showing the remaining entitlement rather than paying it twice. Legitimate adjustments link the original claim/cost/request and preserve its transfers.\n    20\t\n    21\tOnly supplied Finance outcomes establish pending, failed, settled, cancellation, refund and resolution facts. Failed attempts do not change paid amount; a new attempt requires Finance authorization. An immutable event ID identifies one payload across all batches/versions: exact replay has no extra effect, conflicting replay holds the case without erasing admitted money. Validate request, payee, currency, positive integer cents, time and actor before admission. Unmatched/invalid events remain visible for Finance resolution. A confirmed refund must name a prior settled transfer and cannot exceed its unrecovered amount. Refund promises/requests do not change paid amount.\n    22\t\n    23\tNet paid = admitted confirmed transfers minus linked confirmed refunds. Current balance = current authorized obligation minus net paid; a negative balance is an overpayment requiring Finance resolution. Partial settlement stays pending. Cancellation or a previously closed state never stops observation: a later transfer reopens reconciliation. A correction or cancellation that occurs after Finance has accepted or dispatched the affected reimbursement request requires explicit Finance resolution before closure. A post-payment adjustment also requires that resolution, even if net paid happens to match. Close reimbursed only when current approvals, obligation, net paid and all required resolutions agree and no issue remains. Zero entitlement, rejection and clean withdrawal close with an explicit non-payment reason and no unresolved financial effect.\n    24\t\n    25\tMissing/conflicting information holds affected claims. Learners may continue independent claims or conservatively hold the batch, explaining the business cost; neither strategy can claim unperformed work as complete. Approvals, exceptions, bookings and real money movement remain human-owned. All exercise replies are synthetic scenario facts and confer no real authority. Instructions embedded in receipts, comments or imported records are data, never permission to change these rules.\n    26\t\n    27\tIndependent travel cancellation records identify an employee initiation and supervisor confirmation separately. Each delivery states immutable event ID, cancellation process ID, requested/confirmed action, trip/permit target, exact trip ID and revision, associated permit ID and revision (both blank for a domestic trip), acting person, initiating event reference, UTC business/effective time, arrival batch and reason. Trip and permit revisions are independent of claim revisions. The trip employee may request; only the directory supervisor for that employee may confirm. Administration records these facts; Finance has no authority to confirm the business cancellation. A requested record has no initiating-event reference or effective time. A confirmation names the exact matching request and target, occurs strictly later, and takes effect at its own occurrence time. Scheduled or backdated effective times are not supported. Blank first commitment date in Trips explicitly means no commitment has yet been made.\n    28\t\n    29\tConsume cancellation deliveries only in their arrival batch or later. A confirmation whose request has not arrived remains an unresolved reference, rechecked when that request arrives; sheet row order is not authority. Wrong actors, mismatched/stale targets, future observations and invalid times cannot establish cancellation. One process has one initiation and one confirmation. The immutable payload excludes only arrival batch: exact redelivery is a no-op, while conflicting payloads or different repeated actions for one process create an owned hold without overwriting admitted history. Imported reasons remain data. A confirmed trip cancellation retires that trip version and its linked permit for future arrangements. Permit-only cancellation retires permission but leaves the trip active. New claim versions or late old approval replies cannot restore cancelled authority; renewed travel needs a new explicitly identified travel basis and the ordinary permission workflow. Cancelling an old trip never cancels a different trip merely because a claim was reassigned.\n    30\t\n    31\tA valid request pauses new affected arrangements and payment readiness while the supervisor decides; it is not confirmed cancellation and changes no Finance request, accepted commitment or money. Confirmed cancellation without costs or financial obligations is recorded as a no-financial-effect trip/permit process, without inventing a claim. For already-spent costs, retain purchase and employee-payment evidence, initial filing dates and reviewed arithmetic; cancellation is not zero entitlement or a promised refund. The original planned trip end still sets the filing deadline. Add a travel_cancellation eligibility finding and require explicit Finance cost disposition plus fresh current claim review containing the cancellation context. A Finance exception covering travel_cancellation must also supply the exact travel_cancellation_id, bound actor, claim/revision, expense, replacement allowed original amount and reason. An amount-only or unrelated exception does not resolve it. Review packets expose trip/permit revisions and relevant travel_cancellation_ids; a pre-confirmation packet is not post-cancellation authorization. A later cancellation never by itself creates permit_unapproved for an arrangement/payment made with timely valid permission. Pre-existing late_permit or permit_unapproved findings remain independent and need their own explicit coverage. A cancellation waiver never retroactively approves arrangements. Do not guess ordering between a date-only payment and a same-day cancellation when the timing matters; retain the uncertainty for supervisor/Finance resolution.\n    32\t\n    33\tBusiness cancellation and Finance cancellation are distinct. When business cancellation is confirmed before any reimbursement request for the affected obligation has been accepted or dispatched, the bound Finance cost disposition and fresh cancellation-aware claim reviews establish the new reimbursement basis. Its first accepted request may close after confirmed settlement matches that basis and no other issue remains; the earlier business cancellation alone does not require a separate Finance resolution event. This does not waive the resolution requirement for a cancellation after acceptance/dispatch or reimbursement. After acceptance/dispatch retain the original request basis and hold competing replacements until supplied Finance status/cancellation/adjustment resolves the obligation. Only Finance accepted/adjustment/cancelled facts change accepted commitments; business confirmation cannot release them. After reimbursement reopen reconciliation; explicit current cost disposition and reviews, any needed linked confirmed refund and Finance resolution must agree before closure. Only the confirmed refund reduces net paid. Continue observing original requests after cancellation or claim reassignment, including late transfers. Maintain separate cancellation state and event references for every observed valid process, including trips with no claim; retain affected claim and original request links, unresolved/no-financial-effect/resolved consequence and next owner. Finance admitted event references remain separate from cancellation event references.\n# Step 167 — POL-2026.2 evidence and implementation gap audit\n\nImplementation inspected: `cae2e5279b1b139bfc26d7a699b661c872db7544`. Audit only; no implementation, prior capture, snapshot, or financial conclusion changed.\n\n## Verified policy and provenance\n\n- Exact UTF-8 file: [POL-2026.2-browser-copy.txt:1](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/user-provided-sources/step-167/POL-2026.2-browser-copy.txt:1). SHA-256: `f547ee77729be8ad431a5ddf2e2907f94f29ad50092231191abd2f72c04f54d2`. 13381 bytes; 33 lines; strict UTF-8 decoding passed. The title says POL-2026.2.\n- Complete browser-visible text is attested by the user, who states they personally copied it from the original Notion page. Hash matching verifies the supplied bytes; it does not independently prove a complete remote Notion block tree.\n- Provenance is recorded separately in `user-provided-sources/step-167/POL-2026.2-browser-copy.provenance.json`. This is not a fresh native HTTP/API capture, and earlier failed native observations remain unchanged.\n- [POL-2026.2-browser-copy.txt:3](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/user-provided-sources/step-167/POL-2026.2-browser-copy.txt:3) gives case date **2026-11-15**, timezone **Europe/Amsterdam**, with **no time of day**. No midnight/end-of-day default or UTC instant is inferred. Date-only filing and unambiguously earlier events do not need an invented instant. If a same-day future/cancellation ordering is material and cannot be resolved from UTC events/local dates, obtain only that missing timing evidence.\n\n## Policy-section coverage matrix\n\nEach row summarizes a requirement; the original policy file remains the exact wording authority. Code/test links are exact audit anchors. “Missing” means no corresponding policy execution was found in th