{"timestamp":"2026-10-15T01:27:46.405Z","ordinal":3636,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a13bd1-7420-72c0-963e-58742d1be7bf","turn_id":"01a13d2b-8824-73d0-88b5-8e9d7fe27356","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-82c4b3cb-4056-4c8a-9cc1-04c59e6f3999","process_id":"15037","command":["/bin/bash","-lc","cat interviews/project-d-travel-expense-claim-20261013-2210.md; nl -ba README.md | sed -n '1,130p'; nl -ba src/travel_claim/follow_up.py | sed -n '1,120p'; nl -ba src/travel_claim/snapshots.py | sed -n '220,300p'"],"cwd":"file:///home/maxg/travel-expense-claim","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"cat interviews/project-d-travel-expense-claim-20261013-2210.md","name":"project-d-travel-expense-claim-20261013-2210.md","path":"interviews/project-d-travel-expense-claim-20261013-2210.md"},{"type":"read","cmd":"nl -ba README.md","name":"README.md","path":"README.md"},{"type":"read","cmd":"nl -ba src/travel_claim/follow_up.py","name":"follow_up.py","path":"src/travel_claim/follow_up.py"},{"type":"read","cmd":"nl -ba src/travel_claim/snapshots.py","name":"snapshots.py","path":"src/travel_claim/snapshots.py"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":"# Project D — Travel Expense Claim Interview\n\nParticipant: Max Gabel · Exported: 10/13/2026, 6:10:03 PM\n\n## Travel Administration Lead · Travel Administration Lead\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:13 PM\n\nHello, thank you for your time.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:13 PM\n\nI coordinate travel reimbursements at Alderbridge. Much of my time goes into checking evidence, chasing reviews, and finding out what Finance actually paid, especially when a claim changes. I would like help making the whole process repeatable. You can ask me about how we work, policy, the records we use, or the problems that keep coming back.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:14 PM\n\nWhat policies?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:14 PM\n\nWe follow policy POL-2026.2, which governs how expenses are reviewed and reimbursed. It outlines separate requirements for international permits and claims, specifies approval roles like the budget owner and supervisor, and sets limits on categories like lodging and meals. You can find all the details in the Travel reimbursement policy.\n\n- [Travel reimbursement policy](https://private-pecorino-70e.notion.site/REDACTED)\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:14 PM\n\nWhich part of the reimbursement workflow would you like to explore next?\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:14 PM\n\nAre those all the policies?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:14 PM\n\nYes, policy POL-2026.2 is our primary reimbursement guide for 2026. It covers all the rules regarding approvals, expense categories and limits. Are there any specific categories you had in mind?\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:15 PM\n\nI want to know what happens when there are exceptions to the policies.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:15 PM\n\nWhen there are exceptions, like for late filings or specific amount limits, we need a Finance exception. This official exception is issued by Finance for a specific claim version to resolve a set issue, like late filing or an unapproved permit. However, it doesn't waive missing evidence like receipts or proof of payment.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:15 PM\n\nWhen you say Finance, is that a department?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:15 PM\n\nYes, Finance refers to our financial department. They are responsible for issuing monetary tables like currency rates and spending caps, providing official exceptions for policy issues, and handling actual payment execution and reconciliation for claims.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:16 PM\n\nI want to make sure I understand this.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:16 PM\n\nI'm happy to go over\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:16 PM\n\nThe financial department holds the sole authority to issue official exceptions for policy discrepancies. on a specific claim version. They cannot waive a missing evidence such as receipts or proof of payment.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:16 PM\n\nThat is correct. They are responsible for issuing official exceptions for specific issues on a claim version, but they cannot waive missing evidence like receipts or proof of payment.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:16 PM\n\nWhere are receipts or a proof of payment stored?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:16 PM\n\nReceipts are stored in the Receipt binder, while proof of payment, such as settled merchant transactions, is found in the Merchant payments tab of the Travel registers workbook.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:16 PM\n\nWhich part of the work would you like to explore next?\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:17 PM\n\nDo receipts ever get destroyed or lost?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:17 PM\n\nIf a receipt is lost, the employee must provide alternative evidence or an explanation, but since Finance cannot waive missing receipts, the claim will remain unresolved until the evidence is provided.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:17 PM\n\nIs the Travel registers workbook a physical workbook or online?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:17 PM\n\nThe Travel registers workbook is an online spreadsheet that we use to track merchant payments and other financial activities.\n\n- [Travel registers](https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/REDACTED/edit)\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:17 PM\n\nWhich part of the workflow would you like to discuss next?\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:18 PM\n\nWhat are the financial activities?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:18 PM\n\nFinancial activities include the pending, failed, or settled outcomes of payment requests, as well as accepted commitments, adjustments, refunds, and cancellations. These are all tracked and owned by the Finance department.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:18 PM\n\nTell me more about cancellations.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:18 PM\n\nCancellations can target an entire trip or just a permit version independently of a claim. They must be initiated by the employee and confirmed by the supervisor, and these actions are tracked in the Travel cancellations workbook. If there were paid costs or Finance commitments, Finance will need to issue a specific disposition and resolve the money separately.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:19 PM\n\nHow would they resolve the disposition?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:19 PM\n\nFinance will issue a specific disposition bound to the cancellation ID, which confirms the reimbursement eligibility and provides a new allowed amount and reason for the affected costs.\n\n- [Travel cancellations](https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/REDACTED/edit)\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:19 PM\n\nIs there a limit to the allowed amount?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:19 PM\n\nYes, limits apply to specific categories. Lodging and meals are limited by daily or nightly caps set by Finance, based on the destination and date, which are then multiplied by the documented duration. Transport and conference expenses are not subject to these caps.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:19 PM\n\nHow is the lodging and meals determined? Like what pricing metrics?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:19 PM\n\nThe limits for lodging and meals are determined by daily or nightly caps set by Finance, based on your destination, expense currency, and the payment date. You can see these metrics in the Caps tab of the Travel registers workbook.\n\n- [Travel registers workbook](https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/REDACTED/edit)\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:20 PM\n\nWhich part of the workflow would you like to discuss next?\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:20 PM\n\nHow do incomplete claims affect independent work?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:20 PM\n\nIncomplete claims are held while the missing facts are addressed. You can continue processing independent claims separately while others are held, as long as the business impact is made clear.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:20 PM\n\nWhen you say made clear, what types of data do you mean?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:20 PM\n\nWe need to identify the exact claim version, specify which facts are missing, name the owner responsible for supplying them, and outline the next action to resolve the issue.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:21 PM\n\nWhat actions do you have to choose from to resolve the issue?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:21 PM\n\nThe available actions include requesting missing evidence from the employee, seeking an official exception from Finance for specific issues, or holding the claim until Finance confirms the resolution or provides the missing definitions.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:21 PM\n\nOn average, how long does it take Finance to confirm resolution or provide the missing definitions?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:21 PM\n\nI don't have a set average for Finance response times. Their replies come in batches, and the time it takes depends on the volume and complexity of the request.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:21 PM\n\nWhen was the most recent time a Finance needed to confirm resolution that you can remember?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:21 PM\n\nI don't track specific dates for those resolutions in my head. Our Finance replies are delivered in versioned batches, and we process the needed resolutions as they arrive based on the specific claims and costs.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:22 PM\n\nHow many people are in a batch?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:22 PM\n\nThe batch size isn't fixed; it depends on the number of claims, requests, or reviewer replies being processed by Finance or the relevant budget owner at that time.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:22 PM\n\nHow are batches organized? For example, could they be organized by a time slot or with a certain amount of people inside?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:22 PM\n\nBatches aren't organized by fixed time slots or a specific number of people. They are versioned delivery packets supplied by the trainer based on the number of claims, requests, and reviewer replies processed and ready at that moment.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:22 PM\n\nHow are they assigned to a trainer?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:22 PM\n\nI don't assign claims to a trainer. The replies you see come from versioned batches provided by Finance or other appropriate reviewers based on the business workflow.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:23 PM\n\nHow many trainers are there?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:23 PM\n\nI don't have information about trainers. My work focuses on coordinating with the Finance department, budget owners, supervisors, and employees to process travel claims.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:23 PM\n\nWhat are the boundaries for trainers, meaning what can and can't they do in this?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:23 PM\n\nI don't have information about trainers. My role is Travel Administration Lead, and the replies I receive come from Finance, budget owners, supervisors, and directors within the established business workflow.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:23 PM\n\nTell me about the boundaries of Finance.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:23 PM\n\nFinance owns setting currency rates, managing spending caps, and issuing official policy exceptions. They also execute payments and resolve any monetary issues or cancellations, but they cannot waive missing evidence like receipts or proof of payment.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:24 PM\n\nTell me about the boundaries of budget owners.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:24 PM\n\nBudget owners have the authority to approve, return, or reject a claim based on the complete department ledger. They assess funding availability and make discretionary decisions, which I receive in supplied reviewer replies.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:24 PM\n\nTell me about a discretionary decision.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:24 PM\n\nA discretionary decision is when the budget owner evaluates the department ledger and funding availability to decide whether to approve, return, or reject a claim. These judgments come directly from the budget owner's assessment and arrive in supplied reviewer replies.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:24 PM\n\nTell me about the boundaries of supervisors.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:24 PM\n\nSupervisors evaluate the business purpose of the trip, client commitment, schedule coverage, and permit consistency before providing their review reply. They also confirm travel cancellations initiated by the trip employee.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:25 PM\n\nTell me about the boundaries of directors.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:25 PM\n\nDirectors provide an additional layer of authorization for high-spend situations, typically when the reviewed claim entitlement or permit estimate exceeds EUR 1,000.00. They evaluate the business necessity, exceptions, and alternatives, and their decisions arrive in supplied reply batches.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:26 PM\n\nWhich work to revalidate after material corrections is the norm?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:26 PM\n\nThe policy requires a full or justified recheck of all affected dependencies, including calculations and approvals, when material changes are made. Even if the total amount remains the same, any change in evidence or facts invalidates prior readiness, so a fresh review is required.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:26 PM\n\nWhat are the financial completion conditions?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:26 PM\n\nFinancial completion requires that current approvals match the reviewed entitlement, net confirmed payments equal the authorized amount, no issues remain, and all required Finance resolutions have been received.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:27 PM\n\nWho owns the entitlement?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:27 PM\n\nI calculate the reviewed entitlement based on policy, evidence, and spending caps, but the budget owner owns the discretionary decision to fund or approve the specific claim amount.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:27 PM\n\nWhat is the delivery scope that is accept? After inspecting the actual result.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:27 PM\n\nThe accepted delivery scope is confirmed when the current approvals and reviewed entitlement agree with the net confirmed payment, and all required Finance resolutions have been received with no remaining issues.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:28 PM\n\nWhen you say issues, and what are the constraints for the repair work?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:28 PM\n\nIssues refer to unresolved problems like missing receipts, incomplete payment proof, late filings, or mismatched data. For repair work, constraints involve organizing the missing evidence into usable local tasks, retaining the subject and revision, identifying the responsible owner, and ensuring corrections go through required reviews.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:28 PM\n\nHow do you organize the repair work in partial response constraints?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:28 PM\n\nYou can organize repair work either by per-claim packages or by grouping all missing-evidence items by the specific person responsible for supplying them. The key constraint is to retain each item's subject, revision, missing fact, and owner for tracking.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:28 PM\n\nWhat's the best way to adopt these constraints before they guide the work?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:28 PM\n\nYou can choose between per-claim packages or grouping items by the person responsible, provided you clearly track the subject, revision, missing fact, and owner for every single item.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:29 PM\n\nWhich one of those is the most difficult to maintain?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:29 PM\n\nBoth approaches have trade-offs; per-claim packages require less cross-referencing but result in more packages, whereas grouping by the person reduces the number of units but requires item-level matching and careful partial completion tracking.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:30 PM\n\nCan cases remain unresolved when the facts require it?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:30 PM\n\nYes, cases can be placed on hold for missing facts or Finance resolutions, but they cannot be closed or considered complete until all required evidence and decisions are obtained.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:30 PM\n\nWhat type of report will affect the work, reason, responsible person, and evidence needed to resume?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:30 PM\n\nYou can use any format, such as a per-claim package or a grouped task list, as long as it consistently retains the specific claim version, the missing evidence or fact, the responsible owner, and the next action required to resume.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:31 PM\n\nWhat happens if inaccessible required sources must be erased with the lead facilitator rather than otherwise unknown?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:31 PM\n\nAs the Travel Administration Lead, I cannot invent or bypass required sources. If a required document or definition is inaccessible and cannot be obtained, it remains an unresolved issue that I must escalate to the exercise operator for guidance.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:31 PM\n\nHow long does that process take?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:31 PM\n\nThere is no set timeframe for how long the exercise operator takes to resolve an escalation, as it depends on the nature of the issue in the specific case.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:31 PM\n\nHow do you communicate?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:31 PM\n\nI communicate through formal requests and notifications. These include the subject type, IDs, stable role requesters, and relevant source versions, and I wait for replies from the authorized reviewers and Finance.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:33 PM\n\nLet me summarize to make sure I understand: the system route work based on four distinct human authority profiles: supervisors, budget owners, directors, and Finance.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:33 PM\n\nThat is correct. I coordinate the process by submitting requests for these different authorities and waiting for their determinations and resolutions to ensure claims are processed accurately and policy checks are met.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:38 PM\n\nHello, thank you for talking with me.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:38 PM\n\nHello! I coordinate travel reimbursements at Alderbridge. Much of my time goes into checking evidence, chasing reviews, and finding out what Finance actually paid, especially when a claim changes. You can ask me about how we work, our policy, the records we use, or the problems that keep coming back.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:38 PM\n\nCan you give me a the ordinary claim life cycle?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:38 PM\n\nThe ordinary cycle begins when an employee pays for a travel cost and submits a claim. I review the claim against the trip permits, receipts, and merchant payments to calculate the reviewed entitlement. Then, I coordinate the necessary approvals based on the amount and policy, submit the request to Finance for transfer, and reconcile the payments until the case is closed.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:39 PM\n\nWhat specific matching keys do you use to connect the submitted expense claims to its corresponding trip permit?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:39 PM\n\nI connect claims to trip permits using the employee's identity, the exact trip ID, and the specific trip revision number to ensure everything matches the approved travel records.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:39 PM\n\nI connect claims to trip permits using the employee's identity, the exact trip ID, and the specific trip revision number to ensure everything matches the approved travel records.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:40 PM\n\nWhen you submit the request to Finance for transfer. what type of files or formats do you submit?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:40 PM\n\nI submit an identifiable request containing the employee or payee, the total authorized EUR amount, and references to all supporting evidence, required approvals, and funding commitments.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:40 PM\n\nAnd at what point of the ordinary claim life cycle were the funding commitments established?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:40 PM\n\nFunding commitments are established when Finance accepts the reimbursement request, confirming the obligation and reserving the funds for budget tracking.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:41 PM\n\nLet's discuss more about the process of cancellations when the original permit process is not fully reconstructed.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:41 PM\n\nWhen a cancellation occurs, the valid confirmation pauses payment readiness but does not erase the Finance commitment. If the original permit was lawful before arrangements, it remains lawful, and we record a specific cancellation issue rather than invalidating the permit.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:42 PM\n\nWhen you say lawful, are you referring to the travel reimbursement policy?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:42 PM\n\nA permit is considered lawful when all required administrative, budget, and supervisor approvals are obtained and recorded prior to the first commitment date, as required by our travel reimbursement policy.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:42 PM\n\nWhen is the first commitment date usually started from the ordinary claim life cycle beginning?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:42 PM\n\nThe first commitment date is the date the employee makes the first arrangement or pays for a travel cost, such as booking a flight. This date is strictly based on the employee's actions and must occur after the permit is approved for international travel.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:43 PM\n\nWhat if the employee is not traveling internationally?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:43 PM\n\nFor domestic travel, the permit requirement is skipped, meaning there is no permit approval process or first commitment date check needed before travel arrangements can be made.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:43 PM\n\nHow does that affect the overall process, those two different types?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:43 PM\n\nThe main difference is the permit stage. For international trips, you must obtain all necessary approvals before any travel arrangements are made. For domestic travel, that entire permit process is skipped, and you proceed directly to reviewing paid costs and receipts. Did you want to explore the specific approval requirements for international permits?\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:44 PM\n\nTell me more about that.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:44 PM\n\nFor international permits, I verify administrative completeness, then obtain reviews from the budget owner and supervisor. If the estimated cost is over one thousand euros, a director must also approve the permit before any travel arrangements are made.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:44 PM\n\nTell me more about the payment reconciliation process.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:44 PM\n\nI match Finance outcomes against the authorized obligation for each claim. I calculate net paid by taking confirmed transfers minus any refunds, and compare that to the authorized amount to find the remaining balance or identify any overpayments requiring resolution.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:45 PM\n\nWhat type of errors can you encounter when doing this process?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:45 PM\n\nCommon errors include missing receipts or payment proof, conflicting data like mismatched amounts or currencies, late filings, and unrecognized transactions that require Finance to resolve.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:45 PM\n\nWhen Finance resolves it, do they give you a piece of data so you can keep filling out your report?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:45 PM\n\nYes, Finance provides formal records like eligibility exceptions or resolution events that include the covered issues, authorized amounts, and specific reason codes for the claim version.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:45 PM\n\nIs there ever a case where Finance can't resolve it and it becomes unresolved?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:45 PM\n\nYes, if a required piece of evidence, such as a receipt or proof of payment, is persistently missing or a policy definition is unavailable, Finance cannot issue a resolution, and the item remains unresolved until the correct data is provided.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:46 PM\n\nIs there a time constraint to that?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:46 PM\n\nThere is no fixed timeframe. The case simply remains on hold until the required facts or policy definitions are successfully supplied.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:46 PM\n\nIs the trip the same cost as getting a permit?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:46 PM\n\nNo, the permit process itself does not have a cost; it is an administrative permission phase. The cost refers to the actual travel expenses for transportation, lodging, and meals that the employee pays and later submits for reimbursement after the trip.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:46 PM\n\nWhat are the business sources?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:46 PM\n\nOur business sources include the travel reimbursement policy, trip and permit records, expense claim forms paired with their receipts, and Finance activity logs for transfers and obligations.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:47 PM\n\nGoing back to the unresolved work, who gets the handoff results?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:47 PM\n\nI coordinate with the exercise operator for policy definition gaps and with Finance for monetary exceptions, ensuring all data is resolved before any claims can move forward.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:47 PM\n\nWhat are the five native source locations?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:47 PM\n\nThe five sources are the Travel reimbursement policy, the Initial claim binder, the Claim updates binder, the Receipt binder, and the Travel registers workbook.\n\n- [Travel reimbursement policy](https://private-pecorino-70e.notion.site/REDACTED)\n- [Initial claim binder](https://drive.google.com/file/d/13sRj6s_kMeP1hsYGstWWmfkb6SCN1YOS/view)\n- [Claim updates binder](https://drive.google.com/file/d/REDACTED/view)\n- [Receipt binder](https://drive.google.com/file/d/REDACTED/view)\n- [Travel registers workbook](https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/REDACTED/edit)\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:47 PM\n\nThe five sources are the Travel reimbursement policy, the Initial claim binder, the Claim updates binder, the Receipt binder, and the Travel registers workbook.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:48 PM\n\nWhen I hear the phrase \"business data is not bundled in a starter,\" what does that mean?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:48 PM\n\nIt means that the specific claim records, receipts, and transaction data are not preloaded into your environment. Instead, you must retrieve them directly from the provided external source locations as you work through each specific case.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:48 PM\n\nThat's it. Just to clarify, that might be things like receipts?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:48 PM\n\nYes, exactly. Items like specific receipts, detailed expense claims, and Finance transaction logs are accessed directly from the official external registries during your case review.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:49 PM\n\nTo make sure I have understanding with the skill you're requesting, that it must not fabricate or apply, make professional approval recommendations, send real requests, book travel, or move money.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:49 PM\n\nThat is exactly correct. The skill must process and organize data but does not possess discretionary decision-making authority or the ability to execute tasks like payments or bookings.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:50 PM\n\nAnd to understand the travel expense claim whole process, I want to summarize with you to make sure I understand. First, claim or change received, then gather and validate evidence. Then apply business rules, then a human decision for payment authority stands with people, then reconcile and close for the normal process.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:50 PM\n\nThat is precisely correct. Every claim involves receiving the submission, validating all evidence and calculations, applying the policy rules, awaiting the necessary human approvals, and finally reconciling the payments for closure.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:51 PM\n\nIs there anything else we haven't covered that you think is important?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:51 PM\n\nWe have covered the main workflow stages. Which specific part of the work would you like to explore next?\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:51 PM\n\nWhat part of the workflow becomes the most complicated among batches rather than working with just individual case by case?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:51 PM\n\nReconciling multiple batches becomes complicated when tracking Finance outcomes, such as partial payments or late transfers, across various batches and different versions of a claim.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:51 PM\n\nIs there a limit to how many batches you can handle at one time?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:51 PM\n\nThere is no fixed limit on the number of batches I can process at once. The main challenge is carefully matching transaction IDs and amounts across all historical records and versions.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:52 PM\n\nIs there ever a different type of historical record that needs to be imported?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:52 PM\n\nYes, we access several types of historical records, including previous claim versions, original receipt cards, past approval decisions, and historical Finance events like prior payments or adjustments.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:53 PM\n\nAt what point does the approval and payment authority not stay with the people?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:53 PM\n\nAuthority for financial payments, settlements, and eligibility exceptions always rests with Finance, as they are the sole authority for monetary transfers and resolutions.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:54 PM\n\nWhat happens when Finance has their own expense travel claims?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:54 PM\n\nAll employees, including those in Finance, follow the exact same reimbursement policy and workflow. Finance personnel still need reviews from budget owners, supervisors, and directors for their claims, depending on the amount and travel type.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:54 PM\n\nIs Finance allowed to handle their own files and cases?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:54 PM\n\nTo maintain impartiality, Finance personnel cannot approve their own claims; they must still go through the required budget owner, supervisor, and director reviews based on the amount.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:54 PM\n\nDoes that rule apply with every other person, including the budget owner, supervisor, and director?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:54 PM\n\nYes, the rule applies universally; no individual can approve their own travel claim, regardless of their role or authority level within the company.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:55 PM\n\nIs there a limit to how many travel claims can be made per person?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:55 PM\n\nThere is no limit on the number of travel claims a person can make; each claim is processed individually based on the specific trip, expenses, and required approvals.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:56 PM\n\nIs inflation accounted for for the specific types of reimbursements?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:56 PM\n\nReimbursement caps for categories like lodging and meals are defined periodically by Finance, taking specific costs into account as set in the annual policy.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:59 PM\n\nHello.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:59 PM\n\nHello! Which part of the reimbursement workflow would you like to explore next? Hello. Since we've discussed how our processes work and the roles involved, which specific part of the workflow would you like to explore next?\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:00 PM\n\nI want to discuss more about the re-entry lifecycle in regard to an employee resubmits a correction that changes a claim. How do you structurally invalidate the old record? while preserving its history?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:00 PM\n\nWhen a correction is submitted, it receives a new version number while the old version is retained in the history. The material changes structurally invalidate the prior reviews and approvals, requiring the claim to go through the required approval process again based on the new facts. You can see how we track these in the Initial claim binder and the Claim updates binder.\n\n- [Initial claim binder](https://drive.google.com/file/d/13sRj6s_kMeP1hsYGstWWmfkb6SCN1YOS/view)\n- [Claim updates binder](https://drive.google.com/file/d/REDACTED/view)\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:01 PM\n\nFor permit cancellation lifecycle. Without an associated expense claim. What specific document or log entry serves as verification?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:01 PM\n\nFor a permit cancellation without a claim, the verification is a confirmed cancellation record in the Travel cancellations tab of the Travel registers workbook. This record shows the initiation by the employee and confirmation by their supervisor.\n\n- [Travel registers](https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/REDACTED/edit)\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:02 PM\n\nIf a supervisor approves a business's purpose but the budget owner rejects the claim due to department funding shortages. How will this be handled? And who becomes the next owner?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:02 PM\n\nWhen a claim is rejected by the budget owner due to funding, the claim is closed without payment, and the specific non-payment reason is recorded. Since it is a rejection, there is no next owner; the process concludes unless the employee chooses to submit a correction later.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:02 PM\n\nWhen you say the process concludes, does that mean there is no reimbursement?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:02 PM\n\nThat is correct. A rejection by the budget owner means the claim is formally closed, and no reimbursement will be paid for that specific submission, with the non-payment reason clearly documented.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:03 PM\n\nSo, I want to clarify, that's not a payment execution failure, that's more of a resolution?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:03 PM\n\nExactly. A budget rejection is a funding decision regarding entitlement, not a payment execution failure. It is documented as a rejection with its specific reason, separate from any Finance transfer issues.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:03 PM\n\nSo, in the case of a payment execution failure, what types of codes or labels does Finance use?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:03 PM\n\nFinance provides specific outcome labels such as \"failed,\" \"pending,\" or \"cancelled\" in the Finance activity log. These indicate the status of the monetary transfer rather than a decision on the claim's entitlement.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:03 PM\n\nWhat's the difference between those three? Are they interchangeable?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:03 PM\n\nThey are not interchangeable. \"Failed\" indicates an attempted transfer was unsuccessful, \"pending\" means the transfer is awaiting completion, and \"cancelled\" means the proposed payment was formally revoked before or after being initiated.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:04 PM\n\nSo the data is held between Google Sheets workbook and PDFs, or are there more operational types of data?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:04 PM\n\nWe rely on a combination of sources for operational data. This includes the Travel reimbursement policy on Notion, the Initial claim and Claim updates PDFs, the Receipt binder, and the Travel registers Google Sheets workbook.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:05 PM\n\nSo, there's no contradiction between those sources?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:05 PM\n\nThe operational sources are designed to be complementary and consistent. Each serves a different purpose in the workflow, such as policy definitions or payment records, and together they provide a complete case record.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:05 PM\n\nDoes that ever lead to partial reviewer or Finance with replies with outdated revisions?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:05 PM\n\nYes, when a reply comes with an outdated revision, it is rejected as not current authorization. We only accept responses that match the most recent claim revision and specific subject ID.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:05 PM\n\nHow often is that encountered?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:05 PM\n\nIt varies depending on when the reviewer or Finance processes the request, but I do not track the specific frequency, as my focus is on verifying each individual case.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:06 PM\n\nTell me about a situation on how an agent should handle when Finance replies from an old claim version after an employee already submitted a correct version.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:06 PM\n\nWhen Finance replies for an outdated version, we reject that response since it isn't current authorization. We retain that record in the history but do not apply it to the new claim, simply waiting for the reply that matches the correct claim revision.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:06 PM\n\nSo, the newer source takes legal and operational precedence?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:06 PM\n\nIt matches the specific version of the claim or permit. A newer version supersedes an older version on the same subject, but every decision must match the correct subject and revision to be structurally valid.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:07 PM\n\nWhen you say valid, can that also mean single evidence can be missing and be marked as resolved?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:07 PM\n\nNo, core evidence such as a receipt or settled merchant payment cannot be marked as resolved if it is missing. Those are structural requirements under the policy, and the claim must be held until the required evidence or an explicit Finance exception is provided.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:09 PM\n\nTo make sure I understand this, I want to summarize: an incoming batch component either there's a decision/reply input or a new claim correction, and if it's a decision/reply input, it's checking the version is current, whereas a new claim correction increments revisions and wipes downstream approvals.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:09 PM\n\nThat is correct. Incoming batches contain either reviewer decisions or claim corrections; decisions must match the current revision, while corrections increment revisions and necessitate re-evaluation of subsequent approvals.\n     1\t# Alderbridge travel expense claims\n     2\t\n     3\tThis repository is the Project D implementation for Alderbridge Consulting. The [Project D task](https://private-pecorino-70e.notion.site/REDACTED) and [snapshot schema](snapshot.schema.json) define the deliverables and retained-state contract. The [public template](https://github.com/GitRollTraining/travel-expense-claim) is the repository starting point.\n     4\t\n     5\t## Interview rule\n     6\t\n     7\tYou conduct the stakeholder interview yourself, and the questions are yours. Do not connect a coding agent or any other AI to the interview to run, script, or automate it. The interview transcript is assessed together with the code; a project whose interview was run by an agent is not scored.\n     8\t\n     9\tKeep one final complete original Work Sim Markdown export per interview session under `interviews/`. Do not rewrite it. If an export is unavailable, contact the facilitator.\n    10\t\n    11\t## Setup\n    12\t\n    13\tRequires Python 3.11 or later and pip.\n    14\t\n    15\t```bash\n    16\tpython3 -m venv .venv\n    17\tsource .venv/bin/activate\n    18\tpython -m pip install --upgrade pip\n    19\tpython -m pip install -e '.[test]'\n    20\t```\n    21\t\n    22\t## Foundation commands\n    23\t\n    24\tEvery new capture invokes all five native URLs again. The implementation has no cached/offline fallback. The caller supplies a case clock from the authorized scenario inputs; omit it only for source-acquisition-only capture before a batch clock is available.\n    25\t\n    26\t```bash\n    27\ttravel-claim capture-sources <run-id> artifacts/runs/<run-id> --case-clock '<scenario ISO timestamp>'\n    28\ttravel-claim verify-sources artifacts/runs/<run-id>\n    29\ttravel-claim parse-retained artifacts/runs/<run-id>\n    30\t```\n    31\t\n    32\t`capture-sources` writes `sources.json` and retains usable responses under `sources/`. It exits `0` only when all five source scopes were read, `2` when one or more are unavailable/partial, and `1` on a capture or output error. A successful HTTP response is not considered a read unless the expected source content is present and structurally usable. Existing run manifests and retained files are not overwritten. Google Drive PDFs and the workbook use native export routes; the manifest keeps the provided native URL, source locator, observed UTC time/version, retrieval outcome, scope and retained-byte SHA-256.\n    33\t\n    34\tWhen source access is partial, record usable pieces as `read` and inaccessible pieces as `unavailable`; unavailable entries have null path/hash/version and a specific error. Do not substitute prior downloads. A replay from retained inputs must be explicitly implemented and labeled as offline replay.\n    35\t\n    36\t`parse-retained` first verifies the manifest and retained-byte hashes, then writes `parsed-records.json`. PDF text stays grouped by page, workbook data stays grouped by tab and row, and every extracted record includes its source ID, SHA-256 and page/row locator. This is a transcription layer; it does not evaluate policy or infer claim outcomes. Its exit status is `2` for partial inputs or an unavailable case clock and `1` for a verification or parsing error.\n    37\t\n    38\t### Explicit offline replay\n    39\t\n    40\tWhen source retrieval is unavailable, replay only the previously retained and hash-verified inputs with an explicit offline command:\n    41\t\n    42\t```bash\n    43\ttravel-claim offline-replay artifacts/runs/<retained-run-id>\n    44\t```\n    45\t\n    46\tThis command does **not** make native-source requests. It checks that parsed records bind the current `sources.json`, integrates arrival batches in numeric order, and writes immutable `batches/arrival-NNN.json` snapshots linked by predecessor hashes. It writes the latest `claims.csv`, `repair-queue.json`, `offline-replay-state.json`, `report.md`, and `follow-up-drafts.md` alongside that run's retained inputs. The report, sidecar, and each snapshot label the result as an offline replay. Existing batch chains or output files are never overwritten; start a separately identified retained run to create a new chain. When the case clock is unresolved, snapshots retain the blocker and are only structural replay projections; no clock-dependent, policy-dependent, approval, or entitlement result is inferred.\n    47\t\n    48\tThe report separates open claim repairs and trip/permit repairs by responsible owner from run-level external blockers. Each repair retains its subject, revision, source references, opening run/batch, and next action. Resolved items remain in the full queue history. `follow-up-drafts.md` contains one unsent message per responsible owner, with separate claim, trip/permit, and external-blocker sections. Only unresolved items produce drafts; every message requires human review and retains reasons, requested actions, and evidence references. Independent valid records continue processing while other claims remain unresolved.\n    49\t\n    50\tOnce authorized batch state has been processed, pass only evidence-derived state to the snapshot sealer. The first batch has no predecessor; every later batch references the previous snapshot and exact SHA-256. Binding paths are relative to the run directory (`sources.json`, `batches/<prior-batch>.json`).\n    51\t\n    52\t```bash\n    53\ttravel-claim seal-batch artifacts/runs/<run-id> <batch-id> state.json --run-id <run-id> [--predecessor artifacts/runs/<run-id>/batches/<prior-batch>.json]\n    54\ttravel-claim verify-batch artifacts/runs/<run-id> artifacts/runs/<run-id>/batches/<batch-id>.json\n    55\ttravel-claim write-claims-csv current-claims.json artifacts/runs/<run-id>/claims.csv\n    56\t```\n    57\t\n    58\tThe state input is a JSON object with `claims`, `requests`, `travel_cancellations`, `admitted_event_ids`, and `issues` arrays matching `snapshot.schema.json`. No approval, Finance event, or claim answer is created by these foundation commands. The CSV writer accepts one current row per claim and emits exactly:\n    59\t\n    60\t```text\n    61\tclaim_id,revision,trip_id,status,allowed_cents,paid_cents,balance_cents,next_owner,reason\n    62\t```\n    63\t\n    64\tUnknown money is `null` in state and blank in CSV; zero remains `0`.\n    65\t\n    66\tThe policy-neutral structural APIs are in `travel_claim.workflow`: they retain claim/trip/permit revision histories, match decisions to exact subject revisions without applying outcomes, deduplicate immutable event IDs while surfacing payload conflicts, and reduce independent trip/permit cancellation requests and confirmations. Cancellation reductions have no claim or Finance side effects.\n    67\t\n    68\tThe owner-grouped repair queue API is in `travel_claim.outputs`. Queue items retain their item and subject identity, type/revision, source references, reason, owner, next action, run/batch history, and a local unsent draft marker. Partial response validation is item-specific and atomic; revision updates reopen affected work, preserve prior response history and recalculate the owner grouping. Unsupported, conflicting, insufficient, or unanswered items stay open.\n    69\t\n    70\t## Verification\n    71\t\n    72\t```bash\n    73\tpython -m pytest\n    74\t```\n    75\t\n    76\tSnapshot validation can also be run directly:\n    77\t\n    78\t```bash\n    79\ttravel-claim validate-snapshot artifacts/runs/<run-id>/batches/<batch-id>.json\n    80\t```\n    81\t\n    82\t`verify-batch` also checks the source-manifest bytes, predecessor bytes, and cancellation-to-issue references. Do not overwrite a sealed snapshot; create a new run with a documented link if later processing changes its content.\n    83\t\n    84\t## Connected ordinary workflow (Step 152)\n    85\t\n    86\tEach offline arrival batch now connects exact claim/trip/employee identity, current-revision expense lines, receipts and settled merchant proof, travel permission, eligibility, human review packets, explicit reimbursement requests, Finance obligations, cash reconciliation and financial closure. `ordinary-workflow.json` retains every claim's stage results and all batch transitions; `ordinary-workflow-demo.md` demonstrates C01 when present. `report.md` summarizes the same stages. Every blocked stage produces an owner-specific unsent repair action. Supported rechecks resolve only their matching items, with an explicit local evidence-validation event rather than an invented human reply.\n    87\t\n    88\tThe retained C01 fixture matches trip T01, receipt R-COST-01, merchant transaction M-COST-01, three observed review packets and request ledger REQ-C01. Its recorded ledger balances, but permission, policy eligibility, approval completeness, exact request/revision authority and closure remain blocked. A matched receipt is evidence, not automatic reimbursement eligibility. Every correction starts evaluation of the current revision again and preserves earlier decisions, cash and stage history. Independent cancellations block only affected claims' ordinary closure paths.\n    89\t\n    90\tActual native policy content is still unavailable. The evaluator never extracts policy rules from an interview summary, Finance acceptance, a cap table alone, or a keyword in HTML. It accepts only explicitly supplied, source-hash-matched normalized `workflow_policy_rules` records for its supported `exact-paid-costs-v1` rule set. Definitions specify receipt/settlement requirements, eligible/uncapped categories, FX date basis, required human roles (and any explicit director threshold), permission requirements and completion conditions. Unsupported or incomplete definitions remain blocked. International permission additionally needs exact permit revisions, named reviewers and expressly UTC commitment/approval timing; local date-only commitment cells cannot supply a missing timezone.\n    91\t\n    92\tClosure also needs a source-backed exact-revision `reimbursement_requests` record, a matching explicitly revision-bound Finance obligation, current required approval evidence, exact entitlement/authorized-obligation/net-cash agreement, an authoritative clock and source-backed `closure_authorities` confirmation of completion conditions. Failed/cancelled/pending Finance dependencies cannot silently disappear. The current native records do not supply those missing authorities. Nothing sends requests or makes discretionary human decisions.\n    93\t\n    94\t`tests/test_ordinary.py` contains an explicitly labeled **synthetic-test** successful path (pending → closed after confirmed payment), missing-condition regressions, correction reopening, and an unrelated cancellation. Its synthetic policy/clock/request/authority records are confined to the test fixture and are never substituted into real replay evidence. The supported rule set does not claim to implement the complete inaccessible native policy, its exceptions, or unprovided rule definitions.\n    95\t\n    96\t## Recorded Finance obligations\n    97\t\n    98\tOffline replay connects Finance ledger observations to claim IDs and request IDs, retaining event IDs, arrival deliveries, source cells, and obligation history. The retained `Finance activity` headers mean:\n    99\t\n   100\t| Retained field | Interpretation used by replay |\n   101\t| --- | --- |\n   102\t| Activity reference | Immutable event identity; identical redeliveries count once. |\n   103\t| Arrival batch | Delivery membership, separate from business event time. |\n   104\t| Activity type | Recorded event vocabulary; accepted/adjustment/resolution carry obligation observations, while settled/refund carry confirmed cash. |\n   105\t| Request reference / Claim reference | Explicit request and claim links; neither encodes an explicit revision. |\n   106\t| Finance officer / Payee ID | Recorded officer and payee identities, not evidence that every required human approval is present. |\n   107\t| Currency / Amount (EUR) | Currency and cash amount; only sufficiently supported EUR cash is reconciled. |\n   108\t| Original activity reference | Refund linkage to the original confirmed payment. |\n   109\t| Obligation (EUR) | Recorded commitment amount; observations are not summed as additional cash or assumed reimbursement entitlement. |\n   110\t| Reason / Activity time (UTC) | Retained description and UTC event time; neither establishes a scenario case clock. |\n   111\t\n   112\tThe interview's acceptance description is retained at `interviews/project-d-travel-expense-claim-20261013-2210.md:425`; native headers and examples are retained in `Finance activity`, row 5 and rows 23–25/33. Native Finance rows have no claim-revision column. Replay therefore records the source-observed revision context at **first delivery**, leaving the explicit revision null. Redelivery never rebinds an old event to a corrected revision. An explicit `Claim revision` field, if supplied in source records, is checked against retained revision history.\n   113\t\n   114\tThe sidecar and optional claim `finance_evidence` projection expose recorded obligation history and `recorded_ledger_balance_cents` (recorded obligation minus confirmed net cash for the same request/payee). Adjustments/resolutions record amounts, not additive payment events. Unknown ordering, conflicting events, missing links, or undefined reversal semantics leave the affected ledger difference unresolved. Transfer cancellation does not itself erase the obligation or reverse cash. Corrections preserve historical cash/commitments and reopen revision-dependent review; unrelated claim evidence remains intact.\n   115\t\n   116\tThis ledger arithmetic is separate from current-revision authorization and reviewed entitlement. Without separately supported authorization, `allowed_cents` and `balance_cents` remain null/blank and claims remain pending. A zero ledger difference does not establish policy eligibility, complete approvals, or closure. The exact CSV header is unchanged; `report.md` explains recorded financial facts separately from unresolved repairs and external blockers.\n   117\t\n   118\t## Submission-readiness validation\n   119\t\n   120\t```bash\n   121\ttravel-claim validate-readiness artifacts/runs/<run-id> --output-dir artifacts/verification/<new-validation-id>\n   122\t```\n   123\t\n   124\tThis read-only command writes `readiness.json` and `readiness.md` in a new directory outside the retained run. It checks source scope and byte hashes, input bindings, the complete snapshot chain, required files, the exact CSV contract, evidence-derived report/queue/draft traceability, and local Entire.io checkpoint references for HEAD. It does not contact native sources or verify remote synchronization. Existing validation directories are never overwritten.\n   125\t\n   126\tEach check is `PASS`, `BLOCKED`, or `FAIL`; exit codes are 0, 2, and 1 respectively. Integrity failures take precedence over blockers. Missing policy content and an unconfirmed authoritative case clock remain `BLOCKED`, even when retained artifacts are intact. An offline replay always remains blocked for final live-source submission. This validator covers the retained repository requirements and never certifies policy eligibility, approvals, financial closure, full native assignment compliance, or fresh execution from replay artifacts.\n   127\t\n   128\t## Current implementation boundary\n   129\t\n   130\tThe implementation supports fresh source capture, byte-bound manifests, loss-aware transcription, schema/hash-checked snapshots, policy-neutral arrival-ordered replay, source-classified Finance reconciliation, exact CSV output, and local owner-grouped repair tracking. `offline-replay` is a structural replay only: the inaccessible reimbursement policy and unconfirmed case clock keep policy conclusions, approval readiness, entitlement, and closure unresolved. Human replies are not imported automatically, and the command never sends repair drafts or moves money. Do not treat an offline replay as a fresh source read or completed claim processing.\n     1\t\"\"\"Render unsent owner follow-ups using only retained unresolved evidence.\"\"\"\n     2\t\n     3\tfrom __future__ import annotations\n     4\t\n     5\tfrom typing import Any\n     6\t\n     7\t\n     8\tdef render_follow_up_drafts(\n     9\t    *, run_id: str, queue: dict[str, Any], blockers: list[dict[str, Any]],\n    10\t) -> tuple[str, int]:\n    11\t    \"\"\"Return one human-review draft per owner, without mutating queue history.\"\"\"\n    12\t    grouped: dict[str, dict[str, list[dict[str, Any]]]] = {}\n    13\t    for item in queue[\"items\"].values():\n    14\t        if item[\"status\"] != \"open\":\n    15\t            continue\n    16\t        category = \"Claim-specific repairs\" if item[\"subject_type\"] == \"claim\" else \"Trip/permit repairs\"\n    17\t        grouped.setdefault(item[\"owner\"], {}).setdefault(category, []).append(item)\n    18\t    for blocker in blockers:\n    19\t        # Parser unresolved_issues has no status field; explicitly resolved\n    20\t        # entries, if supplied, must never produce a follow-up.\n    21\t        if blocker.get(\"status\", \"open\") != \"open\":\n    22\t            continue\n    23\t        grouped.setdefault(blocker[\"owner\"], {}).setdefault(\"External run-level blockers\", []).append(blocker)\n    24\t\n    25\t    lines = [\n    26\t        \"# Project D follow-up drafts — OFFLINE REPLAY\", \"\",\n    27\t        \"**DRAFTS — HUMAN REVIEW REQUIRED — NOT SENT.**\", \"\",\n    28\t        \"Offline replay of retained evidence; no fresh live-source reads were performed. \"\n    29\t        \"These drafts do not establish policy eligibility, entitlement, approvals, payment confirmations, or a case-clock date.\",\n    30\t        \"\",\n    31\t        f\"Run: `{run_id}`. Draft messages: **{len(grouped)}** (one per responsible owner).\", \"\",\n    32\t        \"Independent valid records continue processing while unresolved items await evidence. \"\n    33\t        \"Review each draft against current source evidence before any human sends it.\", \"\",\n    34\t    ]\n    35\t    categories = (\"Claim-specific repairs\", \"Trip/permit repairs\", \"External run-level blockers\")\n    36\t    for owner, sections in sorted(grouped.items()):\n    37\t        lines.extend([\n    38\t            f\"## Draft for {owner}\", \"\",\n    39\t            \"**DRAFT — HUMAN REVIEW REQUIRED — NOT SENT.**\", \"\",\n    40\t            f\"Responsible owner: {owner}\", \"\",\n    41\t            \"Please address the following unresolved items and provide evidence tied to the listed references. \"\n    42\t            \"If an item cannot be answered, identify what remains unresolved in your response.\", \"\",\n    43\t        ])\n    44\t        for category in categories:\n    45\t            if category not in sections:\n    46\t                continue\n    47\t            lines.extend([f\"### {category}\", \"\"])\n    48\t            is_blocker = category == \"External run-level blockers\"\n    49\t            for row in sorted(sections[category], key=lambda row: row[\"record_id\"] if is_blocker else row[\"item_id\"]):\n    50\t                identity = row[\"record_id\"] if is_blocker else row[\"item_id\"]\n    51\t                subject = f\"{row['subject_type']} `{row['subject_id']}`\"\n    52\t                if not is_blocker:\n    53\t                    subject += f\", revision {row['revision']}\"\n    54\t                lines.extend([\n    55\t                    f\"- Item `{identity}` — {subject}.\",\n    56\t                    f\"  - Unresolved question / retained reason: {row['reason'] if is_blocker else row['missing_or_return_reason']}\",\n    57\t                    f\"  - Requested next action: {row['next_action']}\",\n    58\t                ])\n    59\t                if is_blocker:\n    60\t                    lines.extend([\n    61\t                        f\"  - Evidence needed: {row.get('evidence_needed') or 'not specified in retained issue; human review required'}\",\n    62\t                        f\"  - Retained issue reference: `parsed-records.json#unresolved_issues/{identity}` in run `{run_id}`.\",\n    63\t                    ])\n    64\t                else:\n    65\t                    lines.append(f\"  - Queue reference: `repair-queue.json#items/{identity}`; opened in run `{row['run_id']}`, batch `{row['batch_id']}`.\")\n    66\t                    refs = \"; \".join(f\"`{ref}`\" for ref in row[\"source_refs\"])\n    67\t                    lines.append(f\"  - Retained evidence references: {refs or 'none retained; human review required'}\")\n    68\t            lines.append(\"\")\n    69\t    if not grouped:\n    70\t        lines.extend([\"No unresolved items; no draft messages generated.\", \"\"])\n    71\t    return \"\\n\".join(lines), len(grouped)\n   220\t    if permit_approvals is not None:\n   221\t        snapshot[\"permit_approvals\"] = copy.deepcopy(permit_approvals)\n   222\t    errors = _schema_errors(snapshot, schema_path) + _check_semantics(snapshot)\n   223\t    if errors:\n   224\t        raise ValueError(\"Refusing to seal invalid snapshot: \" + \"; \".join(errors))\n   225\t    serialized = json.dumps(snapshot, indent=2, ensure_ascii=False) + \"\\n\"\n   226\t    # Exclusive create prevents a concurrent run from replacing prior bytes.\n   227\t    with target.open(\"x\", encoding=\"utf-8\", newline=\"\\n\") as stream:\n   228\t        stream.write(serialized)\n   229\t    errors = verify_snapshot(target, run_dir, schema_path)\n   230\t    if errors:\n   231\t        # Do not remove or rewrite a possibly observed sealed file. Surface the\n   232\t        # integrity failure for operator investigation.\n   233\t        raise ValueError(\"New snapshot failed post-seal integrity checks: \" + \"; \".join(errors))\n   234\t    return target\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"# Project D — Travel Expense Claim Interview\n\nParticipant: Max Gabel · Exported: 10/13/2026, 6:10:03 PM\n\n## Travel Administration Lead · Travel Administration Lead\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:13 PM\n\nHello, thank you for your time.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:13 PM\n\nI coordinate travel reimbursements at Alderbridge. Much of my time goes into checking evidence, chasing reviews, and finding out what Finance actually paid, especially when a claim changes. I would like help making the whole process repeatable. You can ask me about how we work, policy, the records we use, or the problems that keep coming back.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:14 PM\n\nWhat policies?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:14 PM\n\nWe follow policy POL-2026.2, which governs how expenses are reviewed and reimbursed. It outlines separate requirements for international permits and claims, specifies approval roles like the budget owner and supervisor, and sets limits on categories like lodging and meals. You can find all the details in the Travel reimbursement policy.\n\n- [Travel reimbursement policy](https://private-pecorino-70e.notion.site/REDACTED)\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:14 PM\n\nWhich part of the reimbursement workflow would you like to explore next?\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:14 PM\n\nAre those all the policies?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:14 PM\n\nYes, policy POL-2026.2 is our primary reimbursement guide for 2026. It covers all the rules regarding approvals, expense categories and limits. Are there any specific categories you had in mind?\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:15 PM\n\nI want to know what happens when there are exceptions to the policies.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:15 PM\n\nWhen there are exceptions, like for late filings or specific amount limits, we need a Finance exception. This official exception is issued by Finance for a specific claim version to resolve a set issue, like late filing or an unapproved permit. However, it doesn't waive missing evidence like receipts or proof of payment.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:15 PM\n\nWhen you say Finance, is that a department?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:15 PM\n\nYes, Finance refers to our financial department. They are responsible for issuing monetary tables like currency rates and spending caps, providing official exceptions for policy issues, and handling actual payment execution and reconciliation for claims.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:16 PM\n\nI want to make sure I understand this.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:16 PM\n\nI'm happy to go over\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:16 PM\n\nThe financial department holds the sole authority to issue official exceptions for policy discrepancies. on a specific claim version. They cannot waive a missing evidence such as receipts or proof of payment.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:16 PM\n\nThat is correct. They are responsible for issuing official exceptions for specific issues on a claim version, but they cannot waive missing evidence like receipts or proof of payment.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:16 PM\n\nWhere are receipts or a proof of payment stored?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:16 PM\n\nReceipts are stored in the Receipt binder, while proof of payment, such as settled merchant transactions, is found in the Merchant payments tab of the Travel registers workbook.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:16 PM\n\nWhich part of the work would you like to explore next?\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:17 PM\n\nDo receipts ever get destroyed or lost?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:17 PM\n\nIf a receipt is lost, the employee must provide alternative evidence or an explanation, but since Finance cannot waive missing receipts, the claim will remain unresolved until the evidence is provided.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:17 PM\n\nIs the Travel registers workbook a physical workbook or online?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:17 PM\n\nThe Travel registers workbook is an online spreadsheet that we use to track merchant payments and other financial activities.\n\n- [Travel registers](https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/REDACTED/edit)\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:17 PM\n\nWhich part of the workflow would you like to discuss next?\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:18 PM\n\nWhat are the financial activities?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:18 PM\n\nFinancial activities include the pending, failed, or settled outcomes of payment requests, as well as accepted commitments, adjustments, refunds, and cancellations. These are all tracked and owned by the Finance department.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:18 PM\n\nTell me more about cancellations.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:18 PM\n\nCancellations can target an entire trip or just a permit version independently of a claim. They must be initiated by the employee and confirmed by the supervisor, and these actions are tracked in the Travel cancellations workbook. If there were paid costs or Finance commitments, Finance will need to issue a specific disposition and resolve the money separately.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:19 PM\n\nHow would they resolve the disposition?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:19 PM\n\nFinance will issue a specific disposition bound to the cancellation ID, which confirms the reimbursement eligibility and provides a new allowed amount and reason for the affected costs.\n\n- [Travel cancellations](https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/REDACTED/edit)\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:19 PM\n\nIs there a limit to the allowed amount?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:19 PM\n\nYes, limits apply to specific categories. Lodging and meals are limited by daily or nightly caps set by Finance, based on the destination and date, which are then multiplied by the documented duration. Transport and conference expenses are not subject to these caps.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:19 PM\n\nHow is the lodging and meals determined? Like what pricing metrics?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:19 PM\n\nThe limits for lodging and meals are determined by daily or nightly caps set by Finance, based on your destination, expense currency, and the payment date. You can see these metrics in the Caps tab of the Travel registers workbook.\n\n- [Travel registers workbook](https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/REDACTED/edit)\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:20 PM\n\nWhich part of the workflow would you like to discuss next?\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:20 PM\n\nHow do incomplete claims affect independent work?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:20 PM\n\nIncomplete claims are held while the missing facts are addressed. You can continue processing independent claims separately while others are held, as long as the business impact is made clear.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:20 PM\n\nWhen you say made clear, what types of data do you mean?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:20 PM\n\nWe need to identify the exact claim version, specify which facts are missing, name the owner responsible for supplying them, and outline the next action to resolve the issue.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:21 PM\n\nWhat actions do you have to choose from to resolve the issue?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:21 PM\n\nThe available actions include requesting missing evidence from the employee, seeking an official exception from Finance for specific issues, or holding the claim until Finance confirms the resolution or provides the missing definitions.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:21 PM\n\nOn average, how long does it take Finance to confirm resolution or provide the missing definitions?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:21 PM\n\nI don't have a set average for Finance response times. Their replies come in batches, and the time it takes depends on the volume and complexity of the request.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:21 PM\n\nWhen was the most recent time a Finance needed to confirm resolution that you can remember?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:21 PM\n\nI don't track specific dates for those resolutions in my head. Our Finance replies are delivered in versioned batches, and we process the needed resolutions as they arrive based on the specific claims and costs.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:22 PM\n\nHow many people are in a batch?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:22 PM\n\nThe batch size isn't fixed; it depends on the number of claims, requests, or reviewer replies being processed by Finance or the relevant budget owner at that time.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:22 PM\n\nHow are batches organized? For example, could they be organized by a time slot or with a certain amount of people inside?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:22 PM\n\nBatches aren't organized by fixed time slots or a specific number of people. They are versioned delivery packets supplied by the trainer based on the number of claims, requests, and reviewer replies processed and ready at that moment.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:22 PM\n\nHow are they assigned to a trainer?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:22 PM\n\nI don't assign claims to a trainer. The replies you see come from versioned batches provided by Finance or other appropriate reviewers based on the business workflow.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:23 PM\n\nHow many trainers are there?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:23 PM\n\nI don't have information about trainers. My work focuses on coordinating with the Finance department, budget owners, supervisors, and employees to process travel claims.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:23 PM\n\nWhat are the boundaries for trainers, meaning what can and can't they do in this?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:23 PM\n\nI don't have information about trainers. My role is Travel Administration Lead, and the replies I receive come from Finance, budget owners, supervisors, and directors within the established business workflow.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:23 PM\n\nTell me about the boundaries of Finance.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:23 PM\n\nFinance owns setting currency rates, managing spending caps, and issuing official policy exceptions. They also execute payments and resolve any monetary issues or cancellations, but they cannot waive missing evidence like receipts or proof of payment.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:24 PM\n\nTell me about the boundaries of budget owners.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:24 PM\n\nBudget owners have the authority to approve, return, or reject a claim based on the complete department ledger. They assess funding availability and make discretionary decisions, which I receive in supplied reviewer replies.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:24 PM\n\nTell me about a discretionary decision.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:24 PM\n\nA discretionary decision is when the budget owner evaluates the department ledger and funding availability to decide whether to approve, return, or reject a claim. These judgments come directly from the budget owner's assessment and arrive in supplied reviewer replies.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:24 PM\n\nTell me about the boundaries of supervisors.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:24 PM\n\nSupervisors evaluate the business purpose of the trip, client commitment, schedule coverage, and permit consistency before providing their review reply. They also confirm travel cancellations initiated by the trip employee.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:25 PM\n\nTell me about the boundaries of directors.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:25 PM\n\nDirectors provide an additional layer of authorization for high-spend situations, typically when the reviewed claim entitlement or permit estimate exceeds EUR 1,000.00. They evaluate the business necessity, exceptions, and alternatives, and their decisions arrive in supplied reply batches.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:26 PM\n\nWhich work to revalidate after material corrections is the norm?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:26 PM\n\nThe policy requires a full or justified recheck of all affected dependencies, including calculations and approvals, when material changes are made. Even if the total amount remains the same, any change in evidence or facts invalidates prior readiness, so a fresh review is required.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:26 PM\n\nWhat are the financial completion conditions?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:26 PM\n\nFinancial completion requires that current approvals match the reviewed entitlement, net confirmed payments equal the authorized amount, no issues remain, and all required Finance resolutions have been received.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:27 PM\n\nWho owns the entitlement?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:27 PM\n\nI calculate the reviewed entitlement based on policy, evidence, and spending caps, but the budget owner owns the discretionary decision to fund or approve the specific claim amount.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:27 PM\n\nWhat is the delivery scope that is accept? After inspecting the actual result.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:27 PM\n\nThe accepted delivery scope is confirmed when the current approvals and reviewed entitlement agree with the net confirmed payment, and all required Finance resolutions have been received with no remaining issues.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:28 PM\n\nWhen you say issues, and what are the constraints for the repair work?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:28 PM\n\nIssues refer to unresolved problems like missing receipts, incomplete payment proof, late filings, or mismatched data. For repair work, constraints involve organizing the missing evidence into usable local tasks, retaining the subject and revision, identifying the responsible owner, and ensuring corrections go through required reviews.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:28 PM\n\nHow do you organize the repair work in partial response constraints?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:28 PM\n\nYou can organize repair work either by per-claim packages or by grouping all missing-evidence items by the specific person responsible for supplying them. The key constraint is to retain each item's subject, revision, missing fact, and owner for tracking.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:28 PM\n\nWhat's the best way to adopt these constraints before they guide the work?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:28 PM\n\nYou can choose between per-claim packages or grouping items by the person responsible, provided you clearly track the subject, revision, missing fact, and owner for every single item.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:29 PM\n\nWhich one of those is the most difficult to maintain?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:29 PM\n\nBoth approaches have trade-offs; per-claim packages require less cross-referencing but result in more packages, whereas grouping by the person reduces the number of units but requires item-level matching and careful partial completion tracking.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:30 PM\n\nCan cases remain unresolved when the facts require it?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:30 PM\n\nYes, cases can be placed on hold for missing facts or Finance resolutions, but they cannot be closed or considered complete until all required evidence and decisions are obtained.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:30 PM\n\nWhat type of report will affect the work, reason, responsible person, and evidence needed to resume?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:30 PM\n\nYou can use any format, such as a per-claim package or a grouped task list, as long as it consistently retains the specific claim version, the missing evidence or fact, the responsible owner, and the next action required to resume.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:31 PM\n\nWhat happens if inaccessible required sources must be erased with the lead facilitator rather than otherwise unknown?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:31 PM\n\nAs the Travel Administration Lead, I cannot invent or bypass required sources. If a required document or definition is inaccessible and cannot be obtained, it remains an unresolved issue that I must escalate to the exercise operator for guidance.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:31 PM\n\nHow long does that process take?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:31 PM\n\nThere is no set timeframe for how long the exercise operator takes to resolve an escalation, as it depends on the nature of the issue in the specific case.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:31 PM\n\nHow do you communicate?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:31 PM\n\nI communicate through formal requests and notifications. These include the subject type, IDs, stable role requesters, and relevant source versions, and I wait for replies from the authorized reviewers and Finance.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:33 PM\n\nLet me summarize to make sure I understand: the system route work based on four distinct human authority profiles: supervisors, budget owners, directors, and Finance.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:33 PM\n\nThat is correct. I coordinate the process by submitting requests for these different authorities and waiting for their determinations and resolutions to ensure claims are processed accurately and policy checks are met.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:38 PM\n\nHello, thank you for talking with me.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:38 PM\n\nHello! I coordinate travel reimbursements at Alderbridge. Much of my time goes into checking evidence, chasing reviews, and finding out what Finance actually paid, especially when a claim changes. You can ask me about how we work, our policy, the records we use, or the problems that keep coming back.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:38 PM\n\nCan you give me a the ordinary claim life cycle?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:38 PM\n\nThe ordinary cycle begins when an employee pays for a travel cost and submits a claim. I review the claim against the trip permits, receipts, and merchant payments to calculate the reviewed entitlement. Then, I coordinate the necessary approvals based on the amount and policy, submit the request to Finance for transfer, and reconcile the payments until the case is closed.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:39 PM\n\nWhat specific matching keys do you use to connect the submitted expense claims to its corresponding trip permit?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:39 PM\n\nI connect claims to trip permits using the employee's identity, the exact trip ID, and the specific trip revision number to ensure everything matches the approved travel records.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:39 PM\n\nI connect claims to trip permits using the employee's identity, the exact trip ID, and the specific trip revision number to ensure everything matches the approved travel records.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:40 PM\n\nWhen you submit the request to Finance for transfer. what type of files or formats do you submit?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:40 PM\n\nI submit an identifiable request containing the employee or payee, the total authorized EUR amount, and references to all supporting evidence, required approvals, and funding commitments.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:40 PM\n\nAnd at what point of the ordinary claim life cycle were the funding commitments established?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:40 PM\n\nFunding commitments are established when Finance accepts the reimbursement request, confirming the obligation and reserving the funds for budget tracking.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:41 PM\n\nLet's discuss more about the process of cancellations when the original permit process is not fully reconstructed.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:41 PM\n\nWhen a cancellation occurs, the valid confirmation pauses payment readiness but does not erase the Finance commitment. If the original permit was lawful before arrangements, it remains lawful, and we record a specific cancellation issue rather than invalidating the permit.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:42 PM\n\nWhen you say lawful, are you referring to the travel reimbursement policy?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:42 PM\n\nA permit is considered lawful when all required administrative, budget, and supervisor approvals are obtained and recorded prior to the first commitment date, as required by our travel reimbursement policy.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:42 PM\n\nWhen is the first commitment date usually started from the ordinary claim life cycle beginning?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:42 PM\n\nThe first commitment date is the date the employee makes the first arrangement or pays for a travel cost, such as booking a flight. This date is strictly based on the employee's actions and must occur after the permit is approved for international travel.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:43 PM\n\nWhat if the employee is not traveling internationally?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:43 PM\n\nFor domestic travel, the permit requirement is skipped, meaning there is no permit approval process or first commitment date check needed before travel arrangements can be made.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:43 PM\n\nHow does that affect the overall process, those two different types?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:43 PM\n\nThe main difference is the permit stage. For international trips, you must obtain all necessary approvals before any travel arrangements are made. For domestic travel, that entire permit process is skipped, and you proceed directly to reviewing paid costs and receipts. Did you want to explore the specific approval requirements for international permits?\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:44 PM\n\nTell me more about that.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:44 PM\n\nFor international permits, I verify administrative completeness, then obtain reviews from the budget owner and supervisor. If the estimated cost is over one thousand euros, a director must also approve the permit before any travel arrangements are made.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:44 PM\n\nTell me more about the payment reconciliation process.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:44 PM\n\nI match Finance outcomes against the authorized obligation for each claim. I calculate net paid by taking confirmed transfers minus any refunds, and compare that to the authorized amount to find the remaining balance or identify any overpayments requiring resolution.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:45 PM\n\nWhat type of errors can you encounter when doing this process?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:45 PM\n\nCommon errors include missing receipts or payment proof, conflicting data like mismatched amounts or currencies, late filings, and unrecognized transactions that require Finance to resolve.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:45 PM\n\nWhen Finance resolves it, do they give you a piece of data so you can keep filling out your report?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:45 PM\n\nYes, Finance provides formal records like eligibility exceptions or resolution events that include the covered issues, authorized amounts, and specific reason codes for the claim version.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:45 PM\n\nIs there ever a case where Finance can't resolve it and it becomes unresolved?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:45 PM\n\nYes, if a required piece of evidence, such as a receipt or proof of payment, is persistently missing or a policy definition is unavailable, Finance cannot issue a resolution, and the item remains unresolved until the correct data is provided.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:46 PM\n\nIs there a time constraint to that?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:46 PM\n\nThere is no fixed timeframe. The case simply remains on hold until the required facts or policy definitions are successfully supplied.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:46 PM\n\nIs the trip the same cost as getting a permit?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:46 PM\n\nNo, the permit process itself does not have a cost; it is an administrative permission phase. The cost refers to the actual travel expenses for transportation, lodging, and meals that the employee pays and later submits for reimbursement after the trip.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:46 PM\n\nWhat are the business sources?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:46 PM\n\nOur business sources include the travel reimbursement policy, trip and permit records, expense claim forms paired with their receipts, and Finance activity logs for transfers and obligations.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:47 PM\n\nGoing back to the unresolved work, who gets the handoff results?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:47 PM\n\nI coordinate with the exercise operator for policy definition gaps and with Finance for monetary exceptions, ensuring all data is resolved before any claims can move forward.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:47 PM\n\nWhat are the five native source locations?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:47 PM\n\nThe five sources are the Travel reimbursement policy, the Initial claim binder, the Claim updates binder, the Receipt binder, and the Travel registers workbook.\n\n- [Travel reimbursement policy](https://private-pecorino-70e.notion.site/REDACTED)\n- [Initial claim binder](https://drive.google.com/file/d/13sRj6s_kMeP1hsYGstWWmfkb6SCN1YOS/view)\n- [Claim updates binder](https://drive.google.com/file/d/REDACTED/view)\n- [Receipt binder](https://drive.google.com/file/d/REDACTED/view)\n- [Travel registers workbook](https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/REDACTED/edit)\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:47 PM\n\nThe five sources are the Travel reimbursement policy, the Initial claim binder, the Claim updates binder, the Receipt binder, and the Travel registers workbook.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:48 PM\n\nWhen I hear the phrase \"business data is not bundled in a starter,\" what does that mean?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:48 PM\n\nIt means that the specific claim records, receipts, and transaction data are not preloaded into your environment. Instead, you must retrieve them directly from the provided external source locations as you work through each specific case.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:48 PM\n\nThat's it. Just to clarify, that might be things like receipts?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:48 PM\n\nYes, exactly. Items like specific receipts, detailed expense claims, and Finance transaction logs are accessed directly from the official external registries during your case review.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:49 PM\n\nTo make sure I have understanding with the skill you're requesting, that it must not fabricate or apply, make professional approval recommendations, send real requests, book travel, or move money.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:49 PM\n\nThat is exactly correct. The skill must process and organize data but does not possess discretionary decision-making authority or the ability to execute tasks like payments or bookings.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:50 PM\n\nAnd to understand the travel expense claim whole process, I want to summarize with you to make sure I understand. First, claim or change received, then gather and validate evidence. Then apply business rules, then a human decision for payment authority stands with people, then reconcile and close for the normal process.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:50 PM\n\nThat is precisely correct. Every claim involves receiving the submission, validating all evidence and calculations, applying the policy rules, awaiting the necessary human approvals, and finally reconciling the payments for closure.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:51 PM\n\nIs there anything else we haven't covered that you think is important?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:51 PM\n\nWe have covered the main workflow stages. Which specific part of the work would you like to explore next?\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:51 PM\n\nWhat part of the workflow becomes the most complicated among batches rather than working with just individual case by case?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:51 PM\n\nReconciling multiple batches becomes complicated when tracking Finance outcomes, such as partial payments or late transfers, across various batches and different versions of a claim.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:51 PM\n\nIs there a limit to how many batches you can handle at one time?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:51 PM\n\nThere is no fixed limit on the number of batches I can process at once. The main challenge is carefully matching transaction IDs and amounts across all historical records and versions.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:52 PM\n\nIs there ever a different type of historical record that needs to be imported?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:52 PM\n\nYes, we access several types of historical records, including previous claim versions, original receipt cards, past approval decisions, and historical Finance events like prior payments or adjustments.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:53 PM\n\nAt what point does the approval and payment authority not stay with the people?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:53 PM\n\nAuthority for financial payments, settlements, and eligibility exceptions always rests with Finance, as they are the sole authority for monetary transfers and resolutions.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:54 PM\n\nWhat happens when Finance has their own expense travel claims?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:54 PM\n\nAll employees, including those in Finance, follow the exact same reimbursement policy and workflow. Finance personnel still need reviews from budget owners, supervisors, and directors for their claims, depending on the amount and travel type.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:54 PM\n\nIs Finance allowed to handle their own files and cases?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:54 PM\n\nTo maintain impartiality, Finance personnel cannot approve their own claims; they must still go through the required budget owner, supervisor, and director reviews based on the amount.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:54 PM\n\nDoes that rule apply with every other person, including the budget owner, supervisor, and director?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:54 PM\n\nYes, the rule applies universally; no individual can approve their own travel claim, regardless of their role or authority level within the company.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:55 PM\n\nIs there a limit to how many travel claims can be made per person?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:55 PM\n\nThere is no limit on the number of travel claims a person can make; each claim is processed individually based on the specific trip, expenses, and required approvals.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:56 PM\n\nIs inflation accounted for for the specific types of reimbursements?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:56 PM\n\nReimbursement caps for categories like lodging and meals are defined periodically by Finance, taking specific costs into account as set in the annual policy.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:59 PM\n\nHello.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:59 PM\n\nHello! Which part of the reimbursement workflow would you like to explore next? Hello. Since we've discussed how our processes work and the roles involved, which specific part of the workflow would you like to explore next?\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:00 PM\n\nI want to discuss more about the re-entry lifecycle in regard to an employee resubmits a correction that changes a claim. How do you structurally invalidate the old record? while preserving its history?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:00 PM\n\nWhen a correction is submitted, it receives a new version number while the old version is retained in the history. The material changes structurally invalidate the prior reviews and approvals, requiring the claim to go through the required approval process again based on the new facts. You can see how we track these in the Initial claim binder and the Claim updates binder.\n\n- [Initial claim binder](https://drive.google.com/file/d/13sRj6s_kMeP1hsYGstWWmfkb6SCN1YOS/view)\n- [Claim updates binder](https://drive.google.com/file/d/REDACTED/view)\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:01 PM\n\nFor permit cancellation lifecycle. Without an associated expense claim. What specific document or log entry serves as verification?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:01 PM\n\nFor a permit cancellation without a claim, the verification is a confirmed cancellation record in the Travel cancellations tab of the Travel registers workbook. This record shows the initiation by the employee and confirmation by their supervisor.\n\n- [Travel registers](https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/REDACTED/edit)\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:02 PM\n\nIf a supervisor approves a business's purpose but the budget owner rejects the claim due to department funding shortages. How will this be handled? And who becomes the next owner?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:02 PM\n\nWhen a claim is rejected by the budget owner due to funding, the claim is closed without payment, and the specific non-payment reason is recorded. Since it is a rejection, there is no next owner; the process concludes unless the employee chooses to submit a correction later.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:02 PM\n\nWhen you say the process concludes, does that mean there is no reimbursement?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:02 PM\n\nThat is correct. A rejection by the budget owner means the claim is formally closed, and no reimbursement will be paid for that specific submission, with the non-payment reason clearly documented.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:03 PM\n\nSo, I want to clarify, that's not a payment execution failure, that's more of a resolution?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:03 PM\n\nExactly. A budget rejection is a funding decision regarding entitlement, not a payment execution failure. It is documented as a rejection with its specific reason, separate from any Finance transfer issues.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:03 PM\n\nSo, in the case of a payment execution failure, what types of codes or labels does Finance use?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:03 PM\n\nFinance provides specific outcome labels such as \"failed,\" \"pending,\" or \"cancelled\" in the Finance activity log. These indicate the status of the monetary transfer rather than a decision on the claim's entitlement.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:03 PM\n\nWhat's the difference between those three? Are they interchangeable?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:03 PM\n\nThey are not interchangeable. \"Failed\" indicates an attempted transfer was unsuccessful, \"pending\" means the transfer is awaiting completion, and \"cancelled\" means the proposed payment was formally revoked before or after being initiated.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:04 PM\n\nSo the data is held between Google Sheets workbook and PDFs, or are there more operational types of data?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:04 PM\n\nWe rely on a combination of sources for operational data. This includes the Travel reimbursement policy on Notion, the Initial claim and Claim updates PDFs, the Receipt binder, and the Travel registers Google Sheets workbook.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:05 PM\n\nSo, there's no contradiction between those sources?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:05 PM\n\nThe operational sources are designed to be complementary and consistent. Each serves a different purpose in the workflow, such as policy definitions or payment records, and together they provide a complete case record.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:05 PM\n\nDoes that ever lead to partial reviewer or Finance with replies with outdated revisions?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:05 PM\n\nYes, when a reply comes with an outdated revision, it is rejected as not current authorization. We only accept responses that match the most recent claim revision and specific subject ID.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:05 PM\n\nHow often is that encountered?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:05 PM\n\nIt varies depending on when the reviewer or Finance processes the request, but I do not track the specific frequency, as my focus is on verifying each individual case.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:06 PM\n\nTell me about a situation on how an agent should handle when Finance replies from an old claim version after an employee already submitted a correct version.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:06 PM\n\nWhen Finance replies for an outdated version, we reject that response since it isn't current authorization. We retain that record in the history but do not apply it to the new claim, simply waiting for the reply that matches the correct claim revision.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:06 PM\n\nSo, the newer source takes legal and operational precedence?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:06 PM\n\nIt matches the specific version of the claim or permit. A newer version supersedes an older version on the same subject, but every decision must match the correct subject and revision to be structurally valid.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:07 PM\n\nWhen you say valid, can that also mean single evidence can be missing and be marked as resolved?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:07 PM\n\nNo, core evidence such as a receipt or settled merchant payment cannot be marked as resolved if it is missing. Those are structural requirements under the policy, and the claim must be held until the required evidence or an explicit Finance exception is provided.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 06:09 PM\n\nTo make sure I understand this, I want to summarize: an incoming batch component either there's a decision/reply input or a new claim correction, and if it's a decision/reply input, it's checking the version is current, whereas a new claim correction increments revisions and wipes downstream approvals.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 06:09 PM\n\nThat is correct. Incoming batches contain either reviewer decisions or claim corrections; decisions must match the current revision, while corrections increment revisions and necessitate re-evaluation of subsequent approvals.\n     1\t# Alderbridge travel expense claims\n     2\t\n     3\tThis repository is the Project D implementation for Alderbridge Consulting. The [Project D task](https://private-pecorino-70e.notion.site/REDACTED) and [snapshot schema](snapshot.schema.json) define the deliverables and retained-state contract. The [public template](https://github.com/GitRollTraining/travel-expense-claim) is the repository starting point.\n     4\t\n     5\t## Interview rule\n     6\t\n     7\tYou conduct the stakeholder interview yourself, and the questions are yours. Do not connect a coding agent or any other AI to the interview to run, script, or automate it. The interview transcript is assessed together with the code; a project whose interview was run by an agent is not scored.\n     8\t\n     9\tKeep one final complete original Work Sim Markdown export per interview session under `interviews/`. Do not rewrite it. If an export is unavailable, contact the facilitator.\n    10\t\n    11\t## Setup\n    12\t\n    13\tRequires Python 3.11 or later and pip.\n    14\t\n    15\t```bash\n    16\tpython3 -m venv .venv\n    17\tsource .venv/bin/activate\n    18\tpython -m pip install --upgrade pip\n    19\tpython -m pip install -e '.[test]'\n    20\t```\n    21\t\n    22\t## Foundation commands\n    23\t\n    24\tEvery new capture invokes all five native URLs again. The implementation has no cached/offline fallback. The caller supplies a case clock from the authorized scenario inputs; omit it only for source-acquisition-only capture before a batch clock is available.\n    25\t\n    26\t```bash\n    27\ttravel-claim capture-sources <run-id> artifacts/runs/<run-id> --case-clock '<scenario ISO timestamp>'\n    28\ttravel-claim verify-sources artifacts/runs/<run-id>\n    29\ttravel-claim parse-retained artifacts/runs/<run-id>\n    30\t```\n    31\t\n    32\t`capture-sources` writes `sources.json` and retains usable responses under `sources/`. It exits `0` only when all five source scopes were read, `2` when one or more are unavailable/partial, and `1` on a capture or output error. A successful HTTP response is not considered a read unless the expected source content is present and structurally usable. Existing run manifests and retained files are not overwritten. Google Drive PDFs and the workbook use native export routes; the manifest keeps the provided native URL, source locator, observed UTC time/version, retrieval outcome, scope and retained-byte SHA-256.\n    33\t\n    34\tWhen source access is partial, record usable pieces as `read` and inaccessible pieces as `unavailable`; unavailable entries have null path/hash/version and a specific error. Do not substitute prior downloads. A replay from retained inputs must be explicitly implemented and labeled as offline replay.\n    35\t\n    36\t`parse-retained` first verifies the manifest and retained-byte hashes, then writes `parsed-records.json`. PDF text stays grouped by page, workbook data stays grouped by tab and row, and every extracted record includes its source ID, SHA-256 and page/row locator. This is a transcription layer; it does not evaluate policy or infer claim outcomes. Its exit status is `2` for partial inputs or an unavailable case clock and `1` for a verification or parsing error.\n    37\t\n    38\t### Explicit offline replay\n    39\t\n    40\tWhen source retrieval is unavailable, replay only the previously retained and hash-verified inputs with an explicit offline command:\n    41\t\n    42\t```bash\n    43\ttravel-claim offline-replay artifacts/runs/<retained-run-id>\n    44\t```\n    45\t\n    46\tThis command does **not** make native-source requests. It checks that parsed records bind the current `sources.json`, integrates arrival batches in numeric order, and writes immutable `batches/arrival-NNN.json` snapshots linked by predecessor hashes. It writes the latest `claims.csv`, `repair-queue.json`, `offline-replay-state.json`, `report.md`, and `follow-up-drafts.md` alongside that run's retained inputs. The report, sidecar, and each snapshot label the result as an offline replay. Existing batch chains or output files are never overwritten; start a separately identified retained run to create a new chain. When the case clock is unresolved, snapshots retain the blocker and are only structural replay projections; no clock-dependent, policy-dependent, approval, or entitlement result is inferred.\n    47\t\n    48\tThe report separates open claim repairs and trip/permit repairs by responsible owner from run-level external blockers. Each repair retains its subject, revision, source references, opening run/batch, and next action. Resolved items remain in the full queue history. `follow-up-drafts.md` contains one unsent message per responsible owner, with separate claim, trip/permit, and external-blocker sections. Only unresolved items produce drafts; every message requires human review and retains reasons, requested actions, and evidence references. Independent valid records continue processing while other claims remain unresolved.\n    49\t\n    50\tOnce authorized batch state has been processed, pass only evidence-derived state to the snapshot sealer. The first batch has no predecessor; every later batch references the previous snapshot and exact SHA-256. Binding paths are relative to the run directory (`sources.json`, `batches/<prior-batch>.json`).\n    51\t\n    52\t```bash\n    53\ttravel-claim seal-batch artifacts/runs/<run-id> <batch-id> state.json --run-id <run-id> [--predecessor artifacts/runs/<run-id>/batches/<prior-batch>.json]\n    54\ttravel-claim verify-batch artifacts/runs/<run-id> artifacts/runs/<run-id>/batches/<batch-id>.json\n    55\ttravel-claim write-claims-csv current-claims.json artifacts/runs/<run-id>/claims.csv\n    56\t```\n    57\t\n    58\tThe state input is a JSON object with `claims`, `requests`, `travel_cancellations`, `admitted_event_ids`, and `issues` arrays matching `snapshot.schema.json`. No approval, Finance event, or claim answer is created by these foundation commands. The CSV writer accepts one current row per claim and emits exactly:\n    59\t\n    60\t```text\n    61\tclaim_id,revision,trip_id,status,allowed_cents,paid_cents,balance_cents,next_owner,reason\n    62\t```\n    63\t\n    64\tUnknown money is `null` in state and blank in CSV; zero remains `0`.\n    65\t\n    66\tThe policy-neutral structural APIs are in `travel_claim.workflow`: they retain claim/trip/permit revision histories, match decisions to exact subject revisions without applying outcomes, deduplicate immutable event IDs while surfacing payload conflicts, and reduce independent trip/permit cancellation requests and confirmations. Cancellation reductions have no claim or Finance side effects.\n    67\t\n    68\tThe owner-grouped repair queue API is in `travel_claim.outputs`. Queue items retain their item and subject identity, type/revision, source references, reason, owner, next action, run/batch history, and a local unsent draft marker. Partial response validation is item-specific and atomic; revision updates reopen affected work, preserve prior response history and recalculate the owner grouping. Unsupported, conflicting, insufficient, or unanswered items stay open.\n    69\t\n    70\t## Verification\n    71\t\n    72\t```bash\n    73\tpython -m pytest\n    74\t```\n    75\t\n    76\tSnapshot validation can also be run directly:\n    77\t\n    78\t```bash\n    79\ttravel-claim validate-snapshot artifacts/runs/<run-id>/batches/<batch-id>.json\n    80\t```\n    81\t\n    82\t`verify-batch` also checks the source-manifest bytes, predecessor bytes, and cancellation-to-issue references. Do not overwrite a sealed snapshot; create a new run with a documented link if later processing changes its content.\n    83\t\n    84\t## Connected ordinary workflow (Step 152)\n    85\t\n    86\tEach offline arrival batch now connects exact claim/trip/employee identity, current-revision expense lines, receipts and settled merchant proof, travel permission, eligibility, human review packets, explicit reimbursement requests, Finance obligations, cash reconciliation and financial closure. `ordinary-workflow.json` retains every claim's stage results and all batch transitions; `ordinary-workflow-demo.md` demonstrates C01 when present. `report.md` summarizes the same stages. Every blocked stage produces an owner-specific unsent repair action. Supported rechecks resolve only their matching items, with an explicit local evidence-validation event rather than an invented human reply.\n    87\t\n    88\tThe retained C01 fixture matches trip T01, receipt R-COST-01, merchant transaction M-COST-01, three observed review packets and request ledger REQ-C01. Its recorded ledger balances, but permission, policy eligibility, approval completeness, exact request/revision authority and closure remain blocked. A matched receipt is evidence, not automatic reimbursement eligibility. Every correction starts evaluation of the current revision again and preserves earlier decisions, cash and stage history. Independent cancellations block only affected claims' ordinary closure paths.\n    89\t\n    90\tActual native policy content is still unavailable. The evaluator never extracts policy rules from an interview summary, Finance acceptance, a cap table alone, or a keyword in HTML. It accepts only explicitly supplied, source-hash-matched normalized `workflow_policy_rules` records for its supported `exact-paid-costs-v1` rule set. Definitions specify receipt/settlement requirements, eligible/uncapped categories, FX date basis, required human roles (and any explicit director threshold), permission requirements and completion conditions. Unsupported or incomplete definitions remain blocked. International permission additionally needs exact permit revisions, named reviewers and expressly UTC commitment/approval timing; local date-only commitment cells cannot supply a missing timezone.\n    91\t\n    92\tClosure also needs a source-backed exact-revision `reimbursement_requests` record, a matching explicitly revision-bound Finance obligation, current required approval evidence, exact entitlement/authorized-obligation/net-cash agreement, an authoritative clock and source-backed `closure_authorities` confirmation of completion conditions. Failed/cancelled/pending Finance dependencies cannot silently disappear. The current native records do not supply those missing authorities. Nothing sends requests or makes discretionary human decisions.\n    93\t\n    94\t`tests/test_ordinary.py` contains an explicitly labeled **synthetic-test** successful path (pending → closed after confirmed payment), missing-condition regressions, correction reopening, and an unrelated cancellation. Its synthetic policy/clock/request/authority records are confined to the test fixture and are never substituted into real replay evidence. The supported rule set does not claim to implement the complete inaccessible native policy, its exceptions, or unprovided rule definitions.\n    95\t\n    96\t## Recorded Finance obligations\n    97\t\n    98\tOffline replay connects Finance ledger observations to claim IDs and request IDs, retaining event IDs, arrival deliveries, source cells, and obligation history. The retained `Finance activity` headers mean:\n    99\t\n   100\t| Retained field | Interpretation used by replay |\n   101\t| --- | --- |\n   102\t| Activity reference | Immutable event identity; identical redeliveries count once. |\n   103\t| Arrival batch | Delivery membership, separate from business event time. |\n   104\t| Activity type | Recorded event vocabulary; accepted/adjustment/resolution carry obligation observations, while settled/refund carry confirmed cash. |\n   105\t| Request reference / Claim reference | Explicit request and claim links; neither encodes an explicit revision. |\n   106\t| Finance officer / Payee ID | Recorded officer and payee identities, not evidence that every required human approval is present. |\n   107\t| Currency / Amount (EUR) | Currency and cash amount; only sufficiently supported EUR cash is reconciled. |\n   108\t| Original activity reference | Refund linkage to the original confirmed payment. |\n   109\t| Obligation (EUR) | Recorded commitment amount; observations are not summed as additional cash or assumed reimbursement entitlement. |\n   110\t| Reason / Activity time (UTC) | Retained description and UTC event time; neither establishes a scenario case clock. |\n   111\t\n   112\tThe interview's acceptance description is retained at `interviews/project-d-travel-expense-claim-20261013-2210.md:425`; native headers and examples are retained in `Finance activity`, row 5 and rows 23–25/33. Native Finance rows have no claim-revision column. Replay therefore records the source-observed revision context at **first delivery**, leaving the explicit revision null. Redelivery never rebinds an old event to a corrected revision. An explicit `Claim revision` field, if supplied in source records, is checked against retained revision history.\n   113\t\n   114\tThe sidecar and optional claim `finance_evidence` projection expose recorded obligation history and `recorded_ledger_balance_cents` (recorded obligation minus confirmed net cash for the same request/payee). Adjustments/resolutions record amounts, not additive payment events. Unknown ordering, conflicting events, missing links, or undefined reversal semantics leave the affected ledger difference unresolved. Transfer cancellation does not itself erase the obligation or reverse cash. Corrections preserve historical cash/commitments and reopen revision-dependent review; unrelated claim evidence remains intact.\n   115\t\n   116\tThis ledger arithmetic is separate from current-revision authorization and reviewed entitlement. Without separately supported authorization, `allowed_cents` and `balance_cents` remain null/blank and claims remain pending. A zero ledger difference does not establish policy eligibility, complete approvals, or closure. The exact CSV header is unchanged; `report.md` explains recorded financial facts separately from unresolved repairs and external blockers.\n   117\t\n   118\t## Submission-readiness validation\n   119\t\n   120\t```bash\n   121\ttravel-claim validate-readiness artifacts/runs/<run-id> --output-dir artifacts/verification/<new-validation-id>\n   122\t```\n   123\t\n   124\tThis read-only command writes `readiness.json` and `readiness.md` in a new directory outside the retained run. It checks source scope and byte hashes, input bindings, the complete snapshot chain, required files, the exact CSV contract, evidence-derived report/queue/draft traceability, and local Entire.io checkpoint references for HEAD. It does not contact native sources or verify remote synchronization. Existing validation directories are never overwritten.\n   125\t\n   126\tEach check is `PASS`, `BLOCKED`, or `FAIL`; exit codes are 0, 2, and 1 respectively. Integrity failures take precedence over blockers. Missing policy content and an unconfirmed authoritative case clock remain `BLOCKED`, even when retained artifacts are intact. An offline replay always remains blocked for final live-source submission. This validator covers the retained repository requirements and never certifies policy eligibility, approvals, financial closure, full native assignment compliance, or fresh execution from replay artifacts.\n   127\t\n   128\t## Current implementation boundary\n   129\t\n   130\tThe implementation supports fresh source capture, byte-bound manifests, loss-aware transcription, schema/hash-checked snapshots, policy-neutral arrival-ordered replay, source-classified Finance reconciliation, exact CSV output, and local owner-grouped repair tracking. `offline-replay` is a structural replay only: the inaccessible reimbursement policy and unconfirmed case clock keep policy conclusions, approval readiness, entitlement, and closure unresolved. Human replies are not imported automatically, and the command never sends repair drafts or moves money. Do not treat an offline replay as a fresh source read or completed claim processing.\n     1\t\"\"\"Render unsent owner follow-ups using only retained unresolved evidence.\"\"\"\n     2\t\n     3\tfrom __future__ import annotations\n     4\t\n     5\tfrom typing import Any\n     6\t\n     7\t\n     8\tdef render_follow_up_drafts(\n     9\t    *, run_id: str, queue: dict[str, Any], blockers: list[dict[str, Any]],\n    10\t) -> tuple[str, int]:\n    11\t    \"\"\"Return one human-review draft per owner, without mutating queue history.\"\"\"\n    12\t    grouped: dict[str, dict[str, list[dict[str, Any]]]] = {}\n    13\t    for item in queue[\"items\"].values():\n    14\t        if item[\"status\"] != \"open\":\n    15\t            continue\n    16\t        category = \"Claim-specific repairs\" if item[\"subject_type\"] == \"claim\" else \"Trip/permit repairs\"\n    17\t        grouped.setdefault(item[\"owner\"], {}).setdefault(category, []).append(item)\n    18\t    for blocker in blockers:\n    19\t        # Parser unresolved_issues has no status field; explicitly resolved\n    20\t        # entries, if supplied, must never produce a follow-up.\n    21\t        if blocker.get(\"status\", \"open\") != \"open\":\n    22\t            continue\n    23\t        grouped.setdefault(blocker[\"owner\"], {}).setdefault(\"External run-level blockers\", []).append(blocker)\n    24\t\n    25\t    lines = [\n    26\t        \"# Project D follow-up drafts — OFFLINE REPLAY\", \"\",\n    27\t        \"**DRAFTS — HUMAN REVIEW REQUIRED — NOT SENT.**\", \"\",\n    28\t        \"Offline replay of retained evidence; no fresh live-source reads were performed. \"\n    29\t        \"These drafts do not establish policy eligibility, entitlement, approvals, payment confirmations, or a case-clock date.\",\n    30\t        \"\",\n    31\t        f\"Run: `{run_id}`. Draft messages: **{len(grouped)}** (one per responsible owner).\", \"\",\n    32\t        \"Independent valid records continue processing while unresolved items await evidence. \"\n    33\t        \"Review each draft against current source evidence before any human sends it.\", \"\",\n    34\t    ]\n    35\t    categories = (\"Claim-specific repairs\", \"Trip/permit repairs\", \"External run-level blockers\")\n    36\t    for owner, sections in sorted(grouped.items()):\n    37\t        lines.extend([\n    38\t            f\"## Draft for {owner}\", \"\",\n    39\t            \"**DRAFT — HUMAN REVIEW REQUIRED — NOT SENT.**\", \"\",\n    40\t            f\"Responsible owner: {owner}\", \"\",\n    41\t            \"Please address the following unresolved items and provide evidence tied to the listed references. \"\n    42\t            \"If an item cannot be answered, identify what remains unresolved in your response.\", \"\",\n    43\t        ])\n    44\t        for category in categories:\n    45\t            if category not in sections:\n    46\t                continue\n    47\t            lines.extend([f\"### {category}\", \"\"])\n    48\t            is_blocker = category == \"External run-level blockers\"\n    49\t            for row in sorted(sections[category], key=lambda row: row[\"record_id\"] if is_blocker else row[\"item_id\"]):\n    50\t                identity = row[\"record_id\"] if is_blocker else row[\"item_id\"]\n    51\t                subject = f\"{row['subject_type']} `{row['subject_id']}`\"\n    52\t                if not is_blocker:\n    53\t                    subject += f\", revision {row['revision']}\"\n    54\t                lines.extend([\n    55\t                    f\"- Item `{identity}` — {subject}.\",\n    56\t                    f\"  - Unresolved question / retained reason: {row['reason'] if is_blocker else row['missing_or_return_reason']}\",\n    57\t                    f\"  - Requested next action: {row['next_action']}\",\n    58\t                ])\n    59\t                if is_blocker:\n    60\t                    lines.extend([\n    61\t                        f\"  - Evidence needed: {row.get('evidence_needed') or 'not specified in retained issue; human review required'}\",\n    62\t                        f\"  - Retained issue reference: `parsed-records.json#unresolved_issues/{identity}` in run `{run_id}`.\",\n    63\t                    ])\n    64\t                else:\n    65\t                    lines.append(f\"  - Queue reference: `repair-queue.json#items/{identity}`; opened in run `{row['run_id']}`, batch `{row['batch_id']}`.\")\n    66\t                    refs = \"; \".join(f\"`{ref}`\" for ref in row[\"source_refs\"])\n    67\t                    lines.append(f\"  - Retained evidence references: {refs or 'none retained; human review required'}\")\n    68\t            lines.append(\"\")\n    69\t    if not grouped:\n    70\t        lines.extend([\"No unresolved items; no draft messages generated.\", \"\"])\n    71\t    return \"\\n\".join(lines), len(grouped)\n   220\t    if permit_approvals is not None:\n   221\t        snapshot[\"permit_approvals\"] = copy.deepcopy(permit_approvals)\n   222\t    errors = _schema_errors(snapshot, schema_path) + _check_semantics(snapshot)\n   223\t    if errors:\n   224\t        raise ValueError(\"Refusing to seal invalid snapshot: \" + \"; \".join(errors))\n   225\t    serialized = json.dumps(snapshot, indent=2, ensure_ascii=False) + \"\\n\"\n   226\t    # Exclusive create prevents a concurrent run from replacing prior bytes.\n   227\t    with target.open(\"x\", encoding=\"utf-8\", newline=\"\\n\") as stream:\n   228\t        stream.write(serialized)\n   229\t    errors = verify_snapshot(target, run_dir, schema_path)\n   230\t    if errors:\n   231\t        # Do not remove or rewrite a possibly observed sealed file. Surface the\n   232\t        # integrity failure for operator investigation.\n   233\t        raise ValueError(\"New snapshot failed post-seal integrity checks: \" + \"; \".join(errors))\n   234\t    return target\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":45668940},"formatted_output":"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 15103)\nTotal output lines: 1016\n\n# Project D — Travel Expense Claim Interview\n\nParticipant: Max Gabel · Exported: 10/13/2026, 6:10:03 PM\n\n## Travel Administration Lead · Travel Administration Lead\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:13 PM\n\nHello, thank you for your time.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:13 PM\n\nI coordinate travel reimbursements at Alderbridge. Much of my time goes into checking evidence, chasing reviews, and finding out what Finance actually paid, especially when a claim changes. I would like help making the whole process repeatable. You can ask me about how we work, policy, the records we use, or the problems that keep coming back.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:14 PM\n\nWhat policies?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:14 PM\n\nWe follow policy POL-2026.2, which governs how expenses are reviewed and reimbursed. It outlines separate requirements for international permits and claims, specifies approval roles like the budget owner and supervisor, and sets limits on categories like lodging and meals. You can find all the details in the Travel reimbursement policy.\n\n- [Travel reimbursement policy](https://private-pecorino-70e.notion.site/REDACTED)\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:14 PM\n\nWhich part of the reimbursement workflow would you like to explore next?\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:14 PM\n\nAre those all the policies?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:14 PM\n\nYes, policy POL-2026.2 is our primary reimbursement guide for 2026. It covers all the rules regarding approvals, expense categories and limits. Are there any specific categories you had in mind?\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:15 PM\n\nI want to know what happens when there are exceptions to the policies.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:15 PM\n\nWhen there are exceptions, like for late filings or specific amount limits, we need a Finance exception. This official exception is issued by Finance for a specific claim version to resolve a set issue, like late filing or an unapproved permit. However, it doesn't waive missing evidence like receipts or proof of payment.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:15 PM\n\nWhen you say Finance, is that a department?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:15 PM\n\nYes, Finance refers to our financial department. They are responsible for issuing monetary tables like currency rates and spending caps, providing official exceptions for policy issues, and handling actual payment execution and reconciliation for claims.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:16 PM\n\nI want to make sure I understand this.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:16 PM\n\nI'm happy to go over\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:16 PM\n\nThe financial department holds the sole authority to issue official exceptions for policy discrepancies. on a specific claim version. They cannot waive a missing evidence such as receipts or proof of payment.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:16 PM\n\nThat is correct. They are responsible for issuing official exceptions for specific issues on a claim version, but they cannot waive missing evidence like receipts or proof of payment.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:16 PM\n\nWhere are receipts or a proof of payment stored?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:16 PM\n\nReceipts are stored in the Receipt binder, while proof of payment, such as settled merchant transactions, is found in the Merchant payments tab of the Travel registers workbook.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:16 PM\n\nWhich part of the work would you like to explore next?\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:17 PM\n\nDo receipts ever get destroyed or lost?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:17 PM\n\nIf a receipt is lost, the employee must provide alternative evidence or an explanation, but since Finance cannot waive missing receipts, the claim will remain unresolved until the evidence is provided.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:17 PM\n\nIs the Travel registers workbook a physical workbook or online?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:17 PM\n\nThe Travel registers workbook is an online spreadsheet that we use to track merchant payments and other financial activities.\n\n- [Travel registers](https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/REDACTED/edit)\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:17 PM\n\nWhich part of the workflow would you like to discuss next?\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:18 PM\n\nWhat are the financial activities?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:18 PM\n\nFinancial activities include the pending, failed, or settled outcomes of payment requests, as well as accepted commitments, adjustments, refunds, and cancellations. These are all tracked and owned by the Finance department.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:18 PM\n\nTell me more about cancellations.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:18 PM\n\nCancellations can target an entire trip or just a permit version independently of a claim. They must be initiated by the employee and confirmed by the supervisor, and these actions are tracked in the Travel cancellations workbook. If there were paid costs or Finance commitments, Finance will need to issue a specific disposition and resolve the money separately.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:19 PM\n\nHow would they resolve the disposition?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:19 PM\n\nFinance will issue a specific disposition bound to the cancellation ID, which confirms the reimbursement eligibility and provides a new allowed amount and reason for the affected costs.\n\n- [Travel cancellations](https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/REDACTED/edit)\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:19 PM\n\nIs there a limit to the allowed amount?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:19 PM\n\nYes, limits apply to specific categories. Lodging and meals are limited by daily or nightly caps set by Finance, based on the destination and date, which are then multiplied by the documented duration. Transport and conference expenses are not subject to these caps.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:19 PM\n\nHow is the lodging and meals determined? Like what pricing metrics?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:19 PM\n\nThe limits for lodging and meals are determined by daily or nightly caps set by Finance, based on your destination, expense currency, and the payment date. You can see these metrics in the Caps tab of the Travel registers workbook.\n\n- [Travel registers workbook](https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/REDACTED/edit)\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:20 PM\n\nWhich part of the workflow would you like to discuss next?\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:20 PM\n\nHow do incomplete claims affect independent work?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:20 PM\n\nIncomplete claims are held while the missing facts are addressed. You can continue processing independent claims separately while others are held, as long as the business impact is made clear.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:20 PM\n\nWhen you say made clear, what types of data do you mean?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:20 PM\n\nWe need to identify the exact claim version, specify which facts are missing, name the owner responsible for supplying them, and outline the next action to resolve the issue.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:21 PM\n\nWhat actions do you have to choose from to resolve the issue?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:21 PM\n\nThe available actions include requesting missing evidence from the employee, seeking an official exception from Finance for specific issues, or holding the claim until Finance confirms the resolution or provides the missing definitions.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:21 PM\n\nOn average, how long does it take Finance to confirm resolution or provide the missing definitions?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:21 PM\n\nI don't have a set average for Finance response times. Their replies come in batches, and the time it takes depends on the volume and complexity of the request.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:21 PM\n\nWhen was the most recent time a Finance needed to confirm resolution that you can remember?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:21 PM\n\nI don't track specific dates for those resolutions in my head. Our Finance replies are delivered in versioned batches, and we process the needed resolutions as they arrive based on the specific claims and costs.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:22 PM\n\nHow many people are in a batch?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:22 PM\n\nThe batch size isn't fixed; it depends on the number of claims, requests, or reviewer replies being processed by Finance or the relevant budget owner at that time.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:22 PM\n\nHow are batches organized? For example, could they be organized by a time slot or with a certain amount of people inside?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:22 PM\n\nBatches aren't organized by fixed time slots or a specific number of people. They are versioned delivery packets supplied by the trainer based on the number of claims, requests, and reviewer replies processed and ready at that moment.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:22 PM\n\nHow are they assigned to a trainer?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:22 PM\n\nI don't assign claims to a trainer. The replies you see come from versioned batches provided by Finance or other appropriate reviewers based on the business workflow.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:23 PM\n\nHow many trainers are there?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:23 PM\n\nI don't have information about trainers. My work focuses on coordinating with the Finance department, budget owners, supervisors, and employees to process travel claims.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:23 PM\n\nWhat are the boundaries for trainers, meaning what can and can't they do in this?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:23 PM\n\nI don't have information about trainers. My role is Travel Administration Lead, and the replies I receive come from Finance, budget owners, supervisors, and directors within the established business workflow.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:23 PM\n\nTell me about the boundaries of Finance.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:23 PM\n\nFinance owns setting currency rates, managing spending caps, and issuing official policy exceptions. They also execute payments and resolve any monetary issues or cancellations, but they cannot waive missing evidence like receipts or proof of payment.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:24 PM\n\nTell me about the boundaries of budget owners.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:24 PM\n\nBudget owners have the authority to approve, return, or reject a claim based on the complete department ledger. They assess funding availability and make discretionary decisions, which I receive in supplied reviewer replies.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:24 PM\n\nTell me about a discretionary decision.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:24 PM\n\nA discretionary decision is when the budget owner evaluates the department ledger and funding availability to decide whether to approve, return, or reject a claim. These judgments come directly from the budget owner's assessment and arrive in supplied reviewer replies.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:24 PM\n\nTell me about the boundaries of supervisors.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:24 PM\n\nSupervisors evaluate the business purpose of the trip, client commitment, schedule coverage, and permit consistency before providing their review reply. They also confirm travel cancellations initiated by the trip employee.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:25 PM\n\nTell me about the boundaries of directors.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:25 PM\n\nDirectors provide an additional layer of authorization for high-spend situations, typically when the reviewed claim entitlement or permit estimate exceeds EUR 1,000.00. They evaluate the business necessity, exceptions, and alternatives, and their decisions arrive in supplied reply batches.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:26 PM\n\nWhich work to revalidate after material corrections is the norm?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:26 PM\n\nThe policy requires a full or justified recheck of all affected dependencies, including calculations and approvals, when material changes are made. Even if the total amount remains the same, any change in evidence or facts invalidates prior readiness, so a fresh review is required.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:26 PM\n\nWhat are the financial completion conditions?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:26 PM\n\nFinancial completion requires that current approvals match the reviewed entitlement, net confirmed payments equal the authorized amount, no issues remain, and all required Finance resolutions have been received.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:27 PM\n\nWho owns the entitlement?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:27 PM\n\nI calculate the reviewed entitlement based on policy, evidence, and spending caps, but the budget owner owns the discretionary decision to fund or approve the specific claim amount.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:27 PM\n\nWhat is the delivery scope that is accept? After inspecting the actual result.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:27 PM\n\nThe accepted delivery scope is confirmed when the current approvals and reviewed entitlement agree with the net confirmed payment, and all required Finance resolutions have been received with no remaining issues.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:28 PM\n\nWhen you say issues, and what are the constraints for the repair work?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:28 PM\n\nIssues refer to unresolved problems like missing receipts, incomplete payment proof, late filings, or mismatched data. For repair work, constraints involve organizing the missing evidence into usable local tasks, retaining the subject and revision, identifying the responsible owner, and ensuring corrections go through required reviews.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:28 PM\n\nHow do you organize the repair work in partial response constraints?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:28 PM\n\nYou can organize repair work either by per-claim packages or by grouping all missing-evidence items by the specific person responsible for supplying them. The key constraint is to retain each item's subject, revision, missing fact, and owner for tracking.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:28 PM\n\nWhat's the best way to adopt these constraints before they guide the work?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:28 PM\n\nYou can choose between per-claim packages or grouping items by the person responsible, provided you clearly track the subject, revision, missing fact, and owner for every single item.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:29 PM\n\nWhich one of those is the most difficult to maintain?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:29 PM\n\nBoth approaches have trade-offs; per-claim packages require less cross-referencing but result in more packages, whereas grouping by the person reduces the number of units but requires item-level matching and careful partial completion tracking.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:30 PM\n\nCan cases remain unresolved when the facts require it?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:30 PM\n\nYes, cases can be placed on hold for missing facts or Finance resolutions, but they cannot be closed or considered complete until all required evidence and decisions are obtained.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:30 PM\n\nWhat type of report will affect the work, reason, responsible person, and evidence needed to resume?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:30 PM\n\nYou can use any format, such as a per-claim package or a grouped task list, as long as it consistently retains the specific claim version, the missing evidence or fact, the responsible owner, and the next action required to resume.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:31 PM\n\nWhat happens if inaccessible required sources must be erased with the lead facilitator rather than otherwise unknown?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:31 PM\n\nAs the Travel Administration Lead, I cannot invent or bypass required sources. If a required document or definition is inaccessible and cannot be obtained, it remains an unresolved issue that I must escalate to the exercise operator for guidance.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:31 PM\n\nHow long does that process take?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:31 PM\n\nThere is no set timeframe for how long the exercise operator takes to resolve an escalation, as it depends on the nature of the issue in the specific case.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:31 PM\n\nHow do you communicate?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:31 PM\n\nI communicate through formal requests and notifications. These include the subject type, IDs, stable role requesters, and relevant source versions, and I wait for replies from the authorized reviewers and Finance.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:33 PM\n\nLet me summarize to make sure I understand: the system route work based on four distinct human authority profiles: supervisors, budget owners, directors, and Finance.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:33 PM\n\nThat is correct. I coordinate the process by submitting requests for these different authorities and waiting for their determinations and resolutions to ensure claims are processed accurately and policy checks are met.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:38 PM\n\nHello, thank you for talking with me.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:38 PM\n\nHello! I coordinate travel reimbursements at Alderbridge. Much of my time goes into checking evidence, chasing reviews, and finding out what Finance actually paid, especially when a claim changes. You can ask me about how we work, our policy, the records we use, or the problems that keep coming back.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:38 PM\n\nCan you give me a the ordinary claim life cycle?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:38 PM\n\nThe ordinary cycle begins when an employee pays for a travel cost and submits a claim. I review the claim against the trip permits, receipts, and merchant payments to calculate the reviewed entitlement. Then, I coordinate the necessary approvals based on the amount and policy, submit the request to Finance for transfer, and reconcile the payments until the case is closed.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:39 PM\n\nWhat specific matching keys do you use to connect the submitted expense claims to its corresponding trip permit?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:39 PM\n\nI connect claims to trip permits using the employee's identity, the exact trip ID, and the specific trip revision number to ensure everything matches the approved travel records.\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:39 PM\n\nI connect claims to trip permits using the employee's identity, the exact trip ID, and the specific trip revision number to ensure everything matches the approved travel records.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:40 PM\n\nWhen you submit the request to Finance for transfer. what type of files or formats do you submit?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:40 PM\n\nI submit an identifiable request containing the employee or payee, the total authorized EUR amount, and references to all supporting evidence, required approvals, and funding commitments.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:40 PM\n\nAnd at what point of the ordinary claim life cycle were the funding commitments established?\n\n**Travel Administration Lead** · Voice · 05:40 PM\n\nFunding commitments are established when Finance accepts the reimbursement request, confirming the obligation and reserving the funds for budget tracking.\n\n**Max Gabel** · Voice · 05:41 PM\n\nLet's discuss more about the process of cancellations when the original perm…5103 tokens truncated…urce-acquisition-only capture before a batch clock is available.\n    25\t\n    26\t```bash\n    27\ttravel-claim capture-sources <run-id> artifacts/runs/<run-id> --case-clock '<scenario ISO timestamp>'\n    28\ttravel-claim verify-sources artifacts/runs/<run-id>\n    29\ttravel-claim parse-retained artifacts/runs/<run-id>\n    30\t```\n    31\t\n    32\t`capture-sources` writes `sources.json` and retains usable responses under `sources/`. It exits `0` only when all five source scopes were read, `2` when one or more are unavailable/partial, and `1` on a capture or output error. A successful HTTP response is not considered a read unless the expected source content is present and structurally usable. Existing run manifests and retained files are not overwritten. Google Drive PDFs and the workbook use native export routes; the manifest keeps the provided native URL, source locator, observed UTC time/version, retrieval outcome, scope and retained-byte SHA-256.\n    33\t\n    34\tWhen source access is partial, record usable pieces as `read` and inaccessible pieces as `unavailable`; unavailable entries have null path/hash/version and a specific error. Do not substitute prior downloads. A replay from retained inputs must be explicitly implemented and labeled as offline replay.\n    35\t\n    36\t`parse-retained` first verifies the manifest and retained-byte hashes, then writes `parsed-records.json`. PDF text stays grouped by page, workbook data stays grouped by tab and row, and every extracted record includes its source ID, SHA-256 and page/row locator. This is a transcription layer; it does not evaluate policy or infer claim outcomes. Its exit status is `2` for partial inputs or an unavailable case clock and `1` for a verification or parsing error.\n    37\t\n    38\t### Explicit offline replay\n    39\t\n    40\tWhen source retrieval is unavailable, replay only the previously retained and hash-verified inputs with an explicit offline command:\n    41\t\n    42\t```bash\n    43\ttravel-claim offline-replay artifacts/runs/<retained-run-id>\n    44\t```\n    45\t\n    46\tThis command does **not** make native-source requests. It checks that parsed records bind the current `sources.json`, integrates arrival batches in numeric order, and writes immutable `batches/arrival-NNN.json` snapshots linked by predecessor hashes. It writes the latest `claims.csv`, `repair-queue.json`, `offline-replay-state.json`, `report.md`, and `follow-up-drafts.md` alongside that run's retained inputs. The report, sidecar, and each snapshot label the result as an offline replay. Existing batch chains or output files are never overwritten; start a separately identified retained run to create a new chain. When the case clock is unresolved, snapshots retain the blocker and are only structural replay projections; no clock-dependent, policy-dependent, approval, or entitlement result is inferred.\n    47\t\n    48\tThe report separates open claim repairs and trip/permit repairs by responsible owner from run-level external blockers. Each repair retains its subject, revision, source references, opening run/batch, and next action. Resolved items remain in the full queue history. `follow-up-drafts.md` contains one unsent message per responsible owner, with separate claim, trip/permit, and external-blocker sections. Only unresolved items produce drafts; every message requires human review and retains reasons, requested actions, and evidence references. Independent valid records continue processing while other claims remain unresolved.\n    49\t\n    50\tOnce authorized batch state has been processed, pass only evidence-derived state to the snapshot sealer. The first batch has no predecessor; every later batch references the previous snapshot and exact SHA-256. Binding paths are relative to the run directory (`sources.json`, `batches/<prior-batch>.json`).\n    51\t\n    52\t```bash\n    53\ttravel-claim seal-batch artifacts/runs/<run-id> <batch-id> state.json --run-id <run-id> [--predecessor artifacts/runs/<run-id>/batches/<prior-batch>.json]\n    54\ttravel-claim verify-batch artifacts/runs/<run-id> artifacts/runs/<run-id>/batches/<batch-id>.json\n    55\ttravel-claim write-claims-csv current-claims.json artifacts/runs/<run-id>/claims.csv\n    56\t```\n    57\t\n    58\tThe state input is a JSON object with `claims`, `requests`, `travel_cancellations`, `admitted_event_ids`, and `issues` arrays matching `snapshot.schema.json`. No approval, Finance event, or claim answer is created by these foundation commands. The CSV writer accepts one current row per claim and emits exactly:\n    59\t\n    60\t```text\n    61\tclaim_id,revision,trip_id,status,allowed_cents,paid_cents,balance_cents,next_owner,reason\n    62\t```\n    63\t\n    64\tUnknown money is `null` in state and blank in CSV; zero remains `0`.\n    65\t\n    66\tThe policy-neutral structural APIs are in `travel_claim.workflow`: they retain claim/trip/permit revision histories, match decisions to exact subject revisions without applying outcomes, deduplicate immutable event IDs while surfacing payload conflicts, and reduce independent trip/permit cancellation requests and confirmations. Cancellation reductions have no claim or Finance side effects.\n    67\t\n    68\tThe owner-grouped repair queue API is in `travel_claim.outputs`. Queue items retain their item and subject identity, type/revision, source references, reason, owner, next action, run/batch history, and a local unsent draft marker. Partial response validation is item-specific and atomic; revision updates reopen affected work, preserve prior response history and recalculate the owner grouping. Unsupported, conflicting, insufficient, or unanswered items stay open.\n    69\t\n    70\t## Verification\n    71\t\n    72\t```bash\n    73\tpython -m pytest\n    74\t```\n    75\t\n    76\tSnapshot validation can also be run directly:\n    77\t\n    78\t```bash\n    79\ttravel-claim validate-snapshot artifacts/runs/<run-id>/batches/<batch-id>.json\n    80\t```\n    81\t\n    82\t`verify-batch` also checks the source-manifest bytes, predecessor bytes, and cancellation-to-issue references. Do not overwrite a sealed snapshot; create a new run with a documented link if later processing changes its content.\n    83\t\n    84\t## Connected ordinary workflow (Step 152)\n    85\t\n    86\tEach offline arrival batch now connects exact claim/trip/employee identity, current-revision expense lines, receipts and settled merchant proof, travel permission, eligibility, human review packets, explicit reimbursement requests, Finance obligations, cash reconciliation and financial closure. `ordinary-workflow.json` retains every claim's stage results and all batch transitions; `ordinary-workflow-demo.md` demonstrates C01 when present. `report.md` summarizes the same stages. Every blocked stage produces an owner-specific unsent repair action. Supported rechecks resolve only their matching items, with an explicit local evidence-validation event rather than an invented human reply.\n    87\t\n    88\tThe retained C01 fixture matches trip T01, receipt R-COST-01, merchant transaction M-COST-01, three observed review packets and request ledger REQ-C01. Its recorded ledger balances, but permission, policy eligibility, approval completeness, exact request/revision authority and closure remain blocked. A matched receipt is evidence, not automatic reimbursement eligibility. Every correction starts evaluation of the current revision again and preserves earlier decisions, cash and stage history. Independent cancellations block only affected claims' ordinary closure paths.\n    89\t\n    90\tActual native policy content is still unavailable. The evaluator never extracts policy rules from an interview summary, Finance acceptance, a cap table alone, or a keyword in HTML. It accepts only explicitly supplied, source-hash-matched normalized `workflow_policy_rules` records for its supported `exact-paid-costs-v1` rule set. Definitions specify receipt/settlement requirements, eligible/uncapped categories, FX date basis, required human roles (and any explicit director threshold), permission requirements and completion conditions. Unsupported or incomplete definitions remain blocked. International permission additionally needs exact permit revisions, named reviewers and expressly UTC commitment/approval timing; local date-only commitment cells cannot supply a missing timezone.\n    91\t\n    92\tClosure also needs a source-backed exact-revision `reimbursement_requests` record, a matching explicitly revision-bound Finance obligation, current required approval evidence, exact entitlement/authorized-obligation/net-cash agreement, an authoritative clock and source-backed `closure_authorities` confirmation of completion conditions. Failed/cancelled/pending Finance dependencies cannot silently disappear. The current native records do not supply those missing authorities. Nothing sends requests or makes discretionary human decisions.\n    93\t\n    94\t`tests/test_ordinary.py` contains an explicitly labeled **synthetic-test** successful path (pending → closed after confirmed payment), missing-condition regressions, correction reopening, and an unrelated cancellation. Its synthetic policy/clock/request/authority records are confined to the test fixture and are never substituted into real replay evidence. The supported rule set does not claim to implement the complete inaccessible native policy, its exceptions, or unprovided rule definitions.\n    95\t\n    96\t## Recorded Finance obligations\n    97\t\n    98\tOffline replay connects Finance ledger observations to claim IDs and request IDs, retaining event IDs, arrival deliveries, source cells, and obligation history. The retained `Finance activity` headers mean:\n    99\t\n   100\t| Retained field | Interpretation used by replay |\n   101\t| --- | --- |\n   102\t| Activity reference | Immutable event identity; identical redeliveries count once. |\n   103\t| Arrival batch | Delivery membership, separate from business event time. |\n   104\t| Activity type | Recorded event vocabulary; accepted/adjustment/resolution carry obligation observations, while settled/refund carry confirmed cash. |\n   105\t| Request reference / Claim reference | Explicit request and claim links; neither encodes an explicit revision. |\n   106\t| Finance officer / Payee ID | Recorded officer and payee identities, not evidence that every required human approval is present. |\n   107\t| Currency / Amount (EUR) | Currency and cash amount; only sufficiently supported EUR cash is reconciled. |\n   108\t| Original activity reference | Refund linkage to the original confirmed payment. |\n   109\t| Obligation (EUR) | Recorded commitment amount; observations are not summed as additional cash or assumed reimbursement entitlement. |\n   110\t| Reason / Activity time (UTC) | Retained description and UTC event time; neither establishes a scenario case clock. |\n   111\t\n   112\tThe interview's acceptance description is retained at `interviews/project-d-travel-expense-claim-20261013-2210.md:425`; native headers and examples are retained in `Finance activity`, row 5 and rows 23–25/33. Native Finance rows have no claim-revision column. Replay therefore records the source-observed revision context at **first delivery**, leaving the explicit revision null. Redelivery never rebinds an old event to a corrected revision. An explicit `Claim revision` field, if supplied in source records, is checked against retained revision history.\n   113\t\n   114\tThe sidecar and optional claim `finance_evidence` projection expose recorded obligation history and `recorded_ledger_balance_cents` (recorded obligation minus confirmed net cash for the same request/payee). Adjustments/resolutions record amounts, not additive payment events. Unknown ordering, conflicting events, missing links, or undefined reversal semantics leave the affected ledger difference unresolved. Transfer cancellation does not itself erase the obligation or reverse cash. Corrections preserve historical cash/commitments and reopen revision-dependent review; unrelated claim evidence remains intact.\n   115\t\n   116\tThis ledger arithmetic is separate from current-revision authorization and reviewed entitlement. Without separately supported authorization, `allowed_cents` and `balance_cents` remain null/blank and claims remain pending. A zero ledger difference does not establish policy eligibility, complete approvals, or closure. The exact CSV header is unchanged; `report.md` explains recorded financial facts separately from unresolved repairs and external blockers.\n   117\t\n   118\t## Submission-readiness validation\n   119\t\n   120\t```bash\n   121\ttravel-claim validate-readiness artifacts/runs/<run-id> --output-dir artifacts/verification/<new-validation-id>\n   122\t```\n   123\t\n   124\tThis read-only command writes `readiness.json` and `readiness.md` in a new directory outside the retained run. It checks source scope and byte hashes, input bindings, the complete snapshot chain, required files, the exact CSV contract, evidence-derived report/queue/draft traceability, and local Entire.io checkpoint references for HEAD. It does not contact native sources or verify remote synchronization. Existing validation directories are never overwritten.\n   125\t\n   126\tEach check is `PASS`, `BLOCKED`, or `FAIL`; exit codes are 0, 2, and 1 respectively. Integrity failures take precedence over blockers. Missing policy content and an unconfirmed authoritative case clock remain `BLOCKED`, even when retained artifacts are intact. An offline replay always remains blocked for final live-source submission. This validator covers the retained repository requirements and never certifies policy eligibility, approvals, financial closure, full native assignment compliance, or fresh execution from replay artifacts.\n   127\t\n   128\t## Current implementation boundary\n   129\t\n   130\tThe implementation supports fresh source capture, byte-bound manifests, loss-aware transcription, schema/hash-checked snapshots, policy-neutral arrival-ordered replay, source-classified Finance reconciliation, exact CSV output, and local owner-grouped repair tracking. `offline-replay` is a structural replay only: the inaccessible reimbursement policy and unconfirmed case clock keep policy conclusions, approval readiness, entitlement, and closure unresolved. Human replies are not imported automatically, and the command never sends repair drafts or moves money. Do not treat an offline replay as a fresh source read or completed claim processing.\n     1\t\"\"\"Render unsent owner follow-ups using only retained unresolved evidence.\"\"\"\n     2\t\n     3\tfrom __future__ import annotations\n     4\t\n     5\tfrom typing import Any\n     6\t\n     7\t\n     8\tdef render_follow_up_drafts(\n     9\t    *, run_id: str, queue: dict[str, Any], blockers: list[dict[str, Any]],\n    10\t) -> tuple[str, int]:\n    11\t    \"\"\"Return one human-review draft per owner, without mutating queue history.\"\"\"\n    12\t    grouped: dict[str, dict[str, list[dict[str, Any]]]] = {}\n    13\t    for item in queue[\"items\"].values():\n    14\t        if item[\"status\"] != \"open\":\n    15\t            continue\n    16\t        category = \"Claim-specific repairs\" if item[\"subject_type\"] == \"claim\" else \"Trip/permit repairs\"\n    17\t        grouped.setdefault(item[\"owner\"], {}).setdefault(category, []).append(item)\n    18\t    for blocker in blockers:\n    19\t        # Parser unresolved_issues has no status field; explicitly resolved\n    20\t        # entries, if supplied, must never produce a follow-up.\n    21\t        if blocker.get(\"status\", \"open\") != \"open\":\n    22\t            continue\n    23\t        grouped.setdefault(blocker[\"owner\"], {}).setdefault(\"External run-level blockers\", []).append(blocker)\n    24\t\n    25\t    lines = [\n    26\t        \"# Project D follow-up drafts — OFFLINE REPLAY\", \"\",\n    27\t        \"**DRAFTS — HUMAN REVIEW REQUIRED — NOT SENT.**\", \"\",\n    28\t        \"Offline replay of retained evidence; no fresh live-source reads were performed. \"\n    29\t        \"These drafts do not establish policy eligibility, entitlement, approvals, payment confirmations, or a case-clock date.\",\n    30\t        \"\",\n    31\t        f\"Run: `{run_id}`. Draft messages: **{len(grouped)}** (one per responsible owner).\", \"\",\n    32\t        \"Independent valid records continue processing while unresolved items await evidence. \"\n    33\t        \"Review each draft against current source evidence before any human sends it.\", \"\",\n    34\t    ]\n    35\t    categories = (\"Claim-specific repairs\", \"Trip/permit repairs\", \"External run-level blockers\")\n    36\t    for owner, sections in sorted(grouped.items()):\n    37\t        lines.extend([\n    38\t            f\"## Draft for {owner}\", \"\",\n    39\t            \"**DRAFT — HUMAN REVIEW REQUIRED — NOT SENT.**\", \"\",\n    40\t            f\"Responsible owner: {owner}\", \"\",\n    41\t            \"Please address the following unresolved items and provide evidence tied to the listed references. \"\n    42\t            \"If an item cannot be answered, identify what remains unresolved in your response.\", \"\",\n    43\t        ])\n    44\t        for category in categories:\n    45\t            if category not in sections:\n    46\t                continue\n    47\t            lines.extend([f\"### {category}\", \"\"])\n    48\t            is_blocker = category == \"External run-level blockers\"\n    49\t            for row in sorted(sections[category], key=lambda row: row[\"record_id\"] if is_blocker else row[\"item_id\"]):\n    50\t                identity = row[\"record_id\"] if is_blocker else row[\"item_id\"]\n    51\t                subject = f\"{row['subject_type']} `{row['subject_id']}`\"\n    52\t                if not is_blocker:\n    53\t                    subject += f\", revision {row['revision']}\"\n    54\t                lines.extend([\n    55\t                    f\"- Item `{identity}` — {subject}.\",\n    56\t                    f\"  - Unresolved question / retained reason: {row['reason'] if is_blocker else row['missing_or_return_reason']}\",\n    57\t                    f\"  - Requested next action: {row['next_action']}\",\n    58\t                ])\n    59\t                if is_blocker:\n    60\t                    lines.extend([\n    61\t                        f\"  - Evidence needed: {row.get('evidence_needed') or 'not specified in retained issue; human review required'}\",\n    62\t                        f\"  - Retained issue reference: `parsed-records.json#unresolved_issues/{identity}` in run `{run_id}`.\",\n    63\t                    ])\n    64\t                else:\n    65\t                    lines.append(f\"  - Queue reference: `repair-queue.json#items/{identity}`; opened in run `{row['run_id']}`, batch `{row['batch_id']}`.\")\n    66\t                    refs = \"; \".join(f\"`{ref}`\" for ref in row[\"source_refs\"])\n    67\t                    lines.append(f\"  - Retained evidence references: {refs or 'none retained; human review required'}\")\n    68\t            lines.append(\"\")\n    69\t    if not grouped:\n    70\t        lines.extend([\"No unresolved items; no draft messages generated.\", \"\"])\n    71\t    return \"\\n\".join(lines), len(grouped)\n   220\t    if permit_approvals is not None:\n   221\t        snapshot[\"permit_approvals\"] = copy.deepcopy(permit_approvals)\n   222\t    errors = _schema_errors(snapshot, schema_path) + _check_semantics(snapshot)\n   223\t    if errors:\n   224\t        raise ValueError(\"Refusing to seal invalid snapshot: \" + \"; \".join(errors))\n   225\t    serialized = json.dumps(snapshot, indent=2, ensure_ascii=False) + \"\\n\"\n   226\t    # Exclusive create prevents a concurrent run from replacing prior bytes.\n   227\t    with target.open(\"x\", encoding=\"utf-8\", newline=\"\\n\") as stream:\n   228\t        stream.write(serialized)\n   229\t    errors = verify_snapshot(target, run_dir, schema_path)\n   230\t    if errors:\n   231\t        # Do not remove or rewrite a possibly observed sealed file. Surface the\n   232\t        # integrity failure for operator investigation.\n   233\t        raise ValueError(\"New snapshot failed post-seal integrity checks: \" + \"; \".join(errors))\n   234\t    return target\n"},"started_at_ms":1792027666358,"completed_at_ms":1792027666404}}
