{"timestamp":"2026-10-15T01:28:58.237Z","ordinal":3660,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a13bd1-7420-72c0-963e-58742d1be7bf","turn_id":"01a13d2b-8824-73d0-88b5-8e9d7fe27356","item":{"type":"AgentMessage","id":"msg_02f4b2bd528e1b90016ad02c2f39c48190b3b0081ee9d1e370","content":[{"type":"Text","text":"Project D has substantial policy-aware components, but **current HEAD is not yet a reproducible, policy-compliant final submission**. The main gaps are run integration, financial completion logic, and current end-to-end evidence.\n\nThis was read-only. HEAD remains `d494252163d4082dd9f086c5045496a73d53b237`; all **129 preserved-file hashes match**. I verified both retained source manifests and the four Step 157 snapshot bindings without errors. I did not regenerate outputs or rerun tests; Step 180’s verified result remains **376 passing tests**, which does not establish assignment compliance.\n\n**Evidence scope and policy provenance**\n\nThe original assignment is linked in [README.md:3](README.md:3), but I found no retained assignment text to inspect. The earlier evidence audit records the same limitation at [project-d-evidence-audit-f98e044.md:5](artifacts/verification/project-d-evidence-audit-f98e044.md:5). Consequently, this comparison covers the retained interview, Skill, schema, policy, and implementation; it cannot certify every original assignment requirement.\n\nBoth policy copies match SHA-256:\n\n`f547ee77729be8ad431a5ddf2e2907f94f29ad50092231191abd2f72c04f54d2`\n\nThe policy is a **user-provided browser-visible copy**, not a successful native Notion retrieval. The loader correctly preserves that distinction at [expenses.py:18](src/travel_claim/expenses.py:18). [Policy line 3](user-provided-sources/step-167/POL-2026.2-browser-copy.txt:3) supplies **2026-11-15, Europe/Amsterdam**, without a time of day. Missing policy wording and a wholly unknown case date are therefore resolved knowledge blockers; native retrieval and materially ambiguous event ordering remain separate issues.\n\n**Implementation findings**\n\n1. **Financial obligation and closure remain the highest business-correctness gap.**\n\n   Recorded Finance obligations are appropriately distinguished from current reimbursement authorization. However, the ordinary workflow requires a separate exact-revision reimbursement-request record at [ordinary.py:238](src/travel_claim/ordinary.py:238). The retained workbook has Finance activity and review packets, but no `Reimbursement requests` sheet. A source-grounded adapter or explicit unresolved mapping is still needed; an accepted event alone must not become entitlement approval.\n\n   Closure additionally requires an offset-bearing timestamp and a per-claim `closure_authorities` record at [ordinary.py:265](src/travel_claim/ordinary.py:265). These are legacy implementation gates, not requirements established by the recovered policy. The interview and policy already state the general completion conditions; a generic extra facilitator approval must not be invented.\n\n   Zero-entitlement completion also remains constrained by `request_ok`, which requires an accepted obligation. The policy allows explicit no-payment completion when no unresolved financial effect remains.\n\n2. **Cancellation admission is stronger than cancellation financial completion.**\n\n   Finance replay preserves admitted events and treats linked refunds separately from transfers: [finance.py:255](src/travel_claim/finance.py:255), [finance.py:271](src/travel_claim/finance.py:271). Refund validation checks the original transfer, matching identities, timing, and unrecovered amount.\n\n   Nevertheless, cancellation projections always emit `financial_status: \"unresolved\"` at [integration.py:392](src/travel_claim/integration.py:392). Ordinary closure blanket-blocks every affected cancellation at [ordinary.py:298](src/travel_claim/ordinary.py:298). This cannot implement the distinct pre-acceptance and post-acceptance/payment branches required by [policy line 33](user-provided-sources/step-167/POL-2026.2-browser-copy.txt:33).\n\n   Resolution checking also iterates `event_history`, rather than exclusively admitted events, at [ordinary.py:282](src/travel_claim/ordinary.py:282). Other gates currently prevent many unsafe closures, but a resolution validator must explicitly exclude invalid/conflicting imports.\n\n3. **Budgets, prepaid attribution, approvals, and correction rechecks are implemented, with remaining integration limits.**\n\n   Budget arithmetic separates net settled expenditure from remaining commitments at [budget.py:196](src/travel_claim/budget.py:196). Cost attribution preserves original evidence and refuses unsupported allocation. Tests cover retained C03/C24 linkage, corrections, cancellations, refunds, exhaustion, and replay at [test_budget.py:123](tests/test_budget.py:123).\n\n   Separate permit/claim reviews, the strictly-above-€1,000 threshold, role authority, and material dependency tracking are present at [approvals.py:52](src/travel_claim/approvals.py:52). Budget changes specifically revalidate budget-owner authority.\n\n   These are component capabilities. They have not yet been demonstrated together in a new retained, policy-bound four-batch run.\n\n4. **Fresh acquisition exists; fresh business execution does not.**\n\n   `live-run` attempts all five locations without cached fallback, but always adds a blocked policy-processing check and consumes no batches: [live_run.py:39](src/travel_claim/live_run.py:39).\n\n   The retained live attempt reports four successful exports and an inaccessible Notion policy body: [live-run.md](artifacts/runs/project-d-live-20261014T204849Z/live-run.md). No new access attempt was performed during this audit.\n\n   The public CLI has no browser-policy binding option, and parsing obtains its case clock only from the native manifest: [cli.py:67](src/travel_claim/cli.py:67), [parsing.py:248](src/travel_claim/parsing.py:248). Acquisition still demands a timestamp rather than supporting the supplied date-and-zone authority.\n\n5. **Outputs and submission validation lag behind the current implementation.**\n\n   The latest retained complete replay is:\n\n   `artifacts/runs/source-capture-20261014T100034Z-step157-revalidation/`\n\n   It contains four valid linked snapshots, **121 pending claims**, and **834 open repairs**. Its state predates recovered-policy and budget bindings. These figures describe historical outputs, not current financial conclusions.\n\n   The exact CSV contract and unsent, unresolved-only owner drafts are implemented at [outputs.py:13](src/travel_claim/outputs.py:13) and [follow_up.py:13](src/travel_claim/follow_up.py:13).\n\n   Readiness remains explicitly offline-only, requires timestamp consistency, and validates repair references against native manifest sources: [readiness.py:85](src/travel_claim/readiness.py:85), [readiness.py:181](src/travel_claim/readiness.py:181). It needs a separate evidence path for browser-policy references. Its Entire.io verification is correctly local-only at [readiness.py:296](src/travel_claim/readiness.py:296).\n\n   The Skill also retains obsolete “provisional closure” and missing-policy descriptions: [SKILL.md:63](skills/travel-expense-claim/SKILL.md:63), [SKILL.md:84](skills/travel-expense-claim/SKILL.md:84). Its documented invocation cannot reproduce the later policy-aware tests.\n\n**External evidence and human authority**\n\nStill external: the retained original assignment text, complete fresh native Notion access, and any genuinely missing claim-specific receipts, current reviews, explicit request/version links, Finance exceptions, or resolutions. Exact time evidence is needed only where ordering materially affects a decision.\n\nStill human-owned: discretionary approvals, exceptions and replacement amounts, funding decisions, Finance cancellation/status/resolution, and actual payment/refund execution. Neither balanced arithmetic nor passing tests supplies these decisions.\n\n**Prioritized gap table**\n\n| Order | Remaining milestone | Category | Dependency / importance |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Bind browser policy and date-only clock into reproducible runs and readiness | Implementation | Enables honest current-policy execution and evidence lineage |\n| 2 | Implement policy-grounded obligation authorization and financial completion | Implementation | Replace legacy gates; validate admitted resolutions, zero entitlement, overpayment and both cancellation branches |\n| 3 | Demonstrate full retained-record processing in a new immutable four-batch run | Verification evidence | Depends on 1–2; preserve unresolved claims independently |\n| 4 | Complete live business orchestration and final submission checks | Implementation + access | Requires supported policy acquisition and explicit execution scope |\n| Parallel | Obtain assignment export and missing claim-specific authority | External / human | Must remain visible; cannot be synthesized |\n\n**Recommended Step 182: provenance-bound policy and case-date run integration**\n\nImplement one bounded milestone: make the existing policy-aware engine reproducible through the CLI, without changing financial closure decisions.\n\nAffected files: `cli.py`, `expenses.py`, `parsing.py`, `offline_replay.py`, `readiness.py`, snapshot/schema binding support, `README.md`, and the Skill.\n\nScope:\n\n- Explicitly accept and retain the verified browser policy as supplemental evidence, separately from native captures.\n- Bind exact bytes, hash, provenance, policy identifier, date, and timezone into a new run.\n- Represent date-only authority without inventing an instant.\n- Preserve native Notion failure status and offline execution labels.\n- Validate supplemental policy references in reports, repairs, and readiness.\n- Update the documented invocation to reproduce that path.\n\nTests should cover exact-copy acceptance, tampering, missing binding, date-only authority, same-day uncertainty, unchanged failed native observations, no cached native fallback, supplemental-reference validation, and immutable prior-run preservation.\n\nFinancial closure and cancellation-resolution changes should follow as a separate milestone with their own focused regressions."}],"phase":"final_answer"},"started_at_ms":1792027705461,"completed_at_ms":1792027738237}}
