{"timestamp":"2026-10-15T10:51:11.297Z","ordinal":4283,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a13bd1-7420-72c0-963e-58742d1be7bf","turn_id":"01a13f29-85cd-7370-837c-72fe912c85f2","item":{"type":"AgentMessage","id":"msg_02f4b2bd528e1b90016ad0affbcfa4819080936e62e55485d4","content":[{"type":"Text","text":"**Step 183 audit complete. The project is not submission-ready: demonstrated defects remain despite 396 passing tests.** No repository files were changed, staged, committed, or pushed.\n\nBranch remains `main`, HEAD `791c8c787bbd617a7b7d3114f13c000249169151`. Working and staged tracked-file diffs are empty.\n\nThe interrupted replay was unavailable, so I executed a new replay in a fresh temporary directory. Its [readiness report](/tmp/project-d-step183-resumed-dcxsswiv/readiness.json) reports **BLOCKED**, **integrity PASS**, and **zero failed validator checks**. Those checks establish deterministic consistency, not complete business correctness.\n\nThe replay produced:\n\n- Four linked snapshots and 121 claims: **119 pending, one withdrawn, one rejected**.\n- 1,001 open repair items and eight owner-specific, unsent drafts.\n- Unchanged native acquisition observations and an exact-byte supplemental policy copy.\n- No reimbursed financial closure.\n\n**Verified capabilities**\n\n| Area | Classification | Finding |\n|---|---|---|\n| Policy provenance and clock | **PASS** | Browser copy hash matches; separately bound as user-provided evidence. Case date is **2026-11-15, Europe/Amsterdam**, without an invented time. |\n| Snapshots and revisions | **PASS** | Four-batch chain, predecessor hashes, deterministic replay, and revision histories validate. C18 advances to r2/T18B while retaining historical money. |\n| Expense arithmetic | **PASS, with admission defect below** | Category rules, integer units, payment-date caps/FX, half-up rounding, and calendar deadlines have passing regression coverage. |\n| Budgets and prepayments | **PASS for inspected ledger arithmetic** | Allocation €20,000; net settled €14,034.15; remaining commitments €3,000; available €2,965.85. C24 retains €200 prior commitment and a €50 remaining request candidate. |\n| Finance events and refunds | **PASS for inspected reconciliation** | C19 retains €100 transferred and €20 linked refund, yielding €80 net paid. Ledger agreement remains separate from approved entitlement. |\n| Repairs and drafts | **PASS structurally** | Owner grouping, partial-response handling, traceability, and unsent labeling work; terminal-claim consistency fails below. |\n| Fresh native completion | **BLOCKED** | Retained observations show four readable exports and unavailable Notion content. No fresh accessibility check was performed during this audit. |\n\n**Prioritized demonstrated defects**\n\nThe diagnostic probes were synthetic and in memory; they did not alter real claim evidence.\n\n| Priority | Classification | Defect and evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **FAIL** | **Unverified expense evidence can establish completion.** With receipt and merchant hashes replaced by unobserved hashes, calculation still returned `PASS`/10,000 cents; the synthetic connected workflow reached `closed-reimbursed`. Matching values are checked without requiring verified source bindings at [expenses.py:88](src/travel_claim/expenses.py:88). |\n| 2 | **FAIL** | **Reviewer authority ignores the case-clock cutoff.** Reviews dated November 16 were accepted under the November 15 policy clock. Same-day reviews also remained approved despite unknown cutoff precision. [approvals.py:128](src/travel_claim/approvals.py:128) parses timestamps but does not apply the cutoff already implemented at [event_validation.py:20](src/travel_claim/event_validation.py:20). |\n| 3 | **FAIL** | **Legacy closure gates conflict with recovered policy.** Closure requires a timestamp and extra per-claim completion-authority record; it blanket-blocks affected cancellations. A clean synthetic personal-zero claim remained pending because it lacked a Finance request. See [ordinary.py:243](src/travel_claim/ordinary.py:243) and [ordinary.py:265](src/travel_claim/ordinary.py:265), compared with policy completion and cancellation rules at [policy line 23](tests/fixtures/POL-2026.2-browser-copy.txt:23) and [line 33](tests/fixtures/POL-2026.2-browser-copy.txt:33). |\n| 4 | **FAIL** | **Cancellation consequences cannot reach resolved/no-financial-effect states.** Projections always emit `financial_status=\"unresolved\"` at [integration.py:392](src/travel_claim/integration.py:392). All four retained processes, including no-claim TC122, consequently remain unresolved. |\n| 5 | **FAIL** | **Blank commitment evidence produces contradictory permit findings.** A synthetic approved permit with no first commitment was authorized by the permit evaluator, but eligibility generated `late_permit` by parsing `None` as a date: [exceptions.py:153](src/travel_claim/exceptions.py:153). [Policy line 27](tests/fixtures/POL-2026.2-browser-copy.txt:27) explicitly defines blank as no commitment yet. |\n| 6 | **FAIL** | **Terminal claims receive misleading follow-ups and CSV reasons.** C13 is withdrawn and C14 rejected, yet each has eight open repairs requesting further authorization. Their CSV reason is “no supported authorized obligation,” replacing the supplied withdrawal/rejection reason. Sources: initial binder pages 13–14 and Review ledger row 40. See [integration.py:171](src/travel_claim/integration.py:171) and [offline_replay.py:268](src/travel_claim/offline_replay.py:268). |\n| 7 | **FAIL** | **The correction report contradicts the verified clock.** It still states that the authoritative case clock is unconfirmed, despite readiness passing the supplemental date binding. The wording is hardcoded at [revalidation.py:91](src/travel_claim/revalidation.py:91). |\n\nReadiness currently reproduces these outputs faithfully; therefore its structural checks can pass while these semantic defects remain.\n\n**External blockers and verification limits**\n\n- **BLOCKED:** Complete native Notion acquisition has not been established. The browser copy must remain distinctly labeled.\n- **BLOCKED:** Current claim-specific reviews, exceptions, request/version authority, and required Finance resolutions remain unresolved where unsupported. They must not be generated automatically.\n- **NOT VERIFIED:** Complete original assignment compliance—the repository links the assignment but lacks retained assignment text.\n- **Preservation:** All 129 hashes matched before interruption. The original preservation manifest is unavailable after resumption, so a fresh complete 129-file comparison is **NOT VERIFIED**. Policy and interview hashes match their known values; native retained-byte verification passes; tracked files remain unchanged.\n\n**Recommended Step 184**\n\nImplement **expense-source and reviewer-clock admission hardening** before broader closure work.\n\nScope: `expenses.py`, `approvals.py`, supporting integration/readiness checks where necessary, and focused regression tests.\n\nRequire verified bindings for calculation evidence and enforce the existing clock semantics before reviewer replies become current authority. Preserve rejected evidence and historical decisions. Tests should reproduce both confirmed defects, including future and same-day reviews, missing/mismatched hashes, unaffected valid claims, and prevention of closure from unbound evidence.\n\nAfterward: correct policy-grounded closure/cancellation transitions, unify permit findings, repair terminal-claim handoffs, rerun end-to-end validation, recover the original preservation manifest, and complete authoritative assignment/source-access verification."}],"phase":"final_answer"},"started_at_ms":1792061438146,"completed_at_ms":1792061471297}}
