{"timestamp":"2026-10-15T12:21:11.964Z","ordinal":5453,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a13bd1-7420-72c0-963e-58742d1be7bf","turn_id":"01a13f75-824c-7092-8bbb-53a9cee4880a","item":{"type":"AgentMessage","id":"msg_02f4b2bd528e1b90016ad0c50290848190887bee689b9bbba8","content":[{"type":"Text","text":"The audit found **FAIL — reproduced implementation defects**, including three false-closure paths. The submission validator returns **BLOCKED**. No fixes were applied.\n\n**480 tests passed, 0 failed**, using `PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1` and disabling pytest’s cache provider. Passing tests do not establish full compliance.\n\nRepository verification:\n\n- **PASS:** branch `main`; HEAD and remote `main` both equal `cc281716d4281fa08829100ef3502486e59db091`.\n- **PASS:** tracked working tree and staging area remain clean. Existing untracked artifacts remain excluded.\n- **PASS:** all **124 retained-data hashes match**.\n- **PASS:** the Step 184 baseline has **127/132 matches**; its five differences are the intentionally committed Step 186 implementation changes.\n- **NOT VERIFIED:** the historical 129-file manifest is unavailable.\n- **BLOCKED:** Entire.io remote consistency. **20/21 checkpoint refs match**. Checkpoint `01M4ZPXY92M96ZDWBH9JFGCQST` differs:\n  - Local: `003ec3e50a747b4f84cfd4d4fc57a4eadd3c962a`\n  - Remote: `616a40a849b5f9121d4ca94006caabe5d4aae167`\n\nNo project files were created, modified, staged, committed, pushed, or deleted during this audit.\n\nThe supported policy replay completed in [/tmp/project-d-step188-6qe91bar/policy-replay](/tmp/project-d-step188-6qe91bar/policy-replay). Its [readiness report](/tmp/project-d-step188-6qe91bar/readiness/readiness.json) contains **29 PASS, 5 BLOCKED, and no structural FAIL checks**.\n\n| Actual replay result | Observation |\n|---|---|\n| Arrival snapshots | Four verified snapshots; 120, 120, 121, 121 claims |\n| Retained records | 127 claim revisions, 203 receipts, 307 decisions, 220 Finance deliveries, 9 cancellation deliveries |\n| CSV | Exact required header; 121 unique current claims |\n| Claim statuses | 119 pending, one withdrawn, one rejected; no reimbursed closures |\n| Confirmed cash | 1,206,415 cents transferred; 3,000 refunded; net 1,203,415 |\n| Repair queue | 907 items: 890 open, 17 resolved |\n| Follow-ups | Eight owner drafts, explicitly requiring human review and marked unsent |\n\nThe cash totals were independently checked against the admitted events’ retained source-row amounts.\n\nThe confirmed defects, ranked by impact, are:\n\n| Priority/category | Requirement and evidence | Reproduced behavior and consequence |\n|---|---|---|\n| **1 — FAIL** | A material correction after acceptance requires current Finance resolution. Policy lines 17 and 23. | A synthetic claim closed using `SYN-OLD-RES` from September 12 after a material receipt-source change and fresh reviews introduced at batch 3. `material_same_revision=True`, but the old resolution still satisfied the dependency. [completion.py:33](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/src/travel_claim/completion.py:33) compares resolution time with the original acceptance, not the subsequent material change. |\n| **2 — FAIL** | Cancellation after reimbursement requires explicit Finance resolution. Policy lines 23 and 33. | Synthetic transfer September 4 → cancellation September 5 → acceptance September 11 produced `closed-reimbursed`, with **no resolution requirements**. [completion.py:54](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/src/travel_claim/completion.py:54) checks cancellation against acceptance only. It misses prior reimbursement and permits contradictory chronology to close. |\n| **3 — FAIL** | Only facts established by the authoritative case clock may support current entitlement. Policy lines 3 and 5. | With case date **November 15**, a synthetic employee payment and submission dated **November 16** passed expense validation and reached `closed-reimbursed`. [expenses.py:178](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/src/travel_claim/expenses.py:178) checks the payment’s policy year but not the case-date cutoff. Reviewer and Finance cutoff checks do not cover this gap. |\n| **4 — FAIL** | Clean withdrawal/rejection must retain the explicit non-payment reason and require no further reimbursement work. Policy lines 15 and 23; interview lines 700–712. | Real C13 and C14 have clean terminal workflow reasons and `next_owner=None`, but CSV replaces these with **Finance** and “no supported authorized obligation.” Their drafts request approvals and reimbursement acceptance. [offline_replay.py:270](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/src/travel_claim/offline_replay.py:270) selects the first open repair instead of the terminal outcome; [integration.py:176](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/src/travel_claim/integration.py:176) opens ordinary approval/payment repairs despite that outcome. |\n| **5 — FAIL** | Preserve superseded findings as history while issuing current actionable repairs. Policy lines 17 and 25. | **63 open repairs reference historical claim revisions.** Not all are necessarily obsolete, but confirmed examples are: C12 r1 still requests a late-filing exception although r2 retains validated coverage; C19 r2 still requests cancellation coverage supplied in r3. These appear in active drafts. [integration.py:173](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/src/travel_claim/integration.py:173) rechecks current revisions without retiring the superseded actionable items. |\n| **6 — FAIL** | Partial responses must preserve item-specific evidence/history and replay exactly. | Two response items with notes `FIRST` and `SECOND` recorded `SECOND` in both global events. Exact replay then raised `conflicting replay for repair response same/a`. [outputs.py:220](/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/src/travel_claim/outputs.py:220) uses the last validation-loop outcome’s note. Separately, a nonempty string such as `not-a-retained-reference` can resolve an item without source verification. This affects queue integrity; it was **not** shown to grant financial authority. |\n\nThe first three observations are explicitly **synthetic diagnostic probes**, not findings that real retained claims have closed incorrectly. They used the existing connected-workflow test helpers without modifying tests.\n\nThe real terminal-output evidence is specific:\n\n- **C13:** initial binder page 13 states “Employee withdrew before review.”\n- **C14:** initial binder page 14 and `Review ledger`, row 40 retain the rejection reason “Client work was cancelled.”\n- Their current workflow reasons are correct; their generated CSV reasons and follow-up actions are inconsistent.\n\nThe verified capabilities are **PASS**, within these tested boundaries:\n\n- Fresh acquisition attempts all five configured locations, retains response observations and usable content, detects incomplete access, and prohibits cached-input fallback.\n- Source hashes, policy provenance, snapshot predecessors, revisions, and deterministic replay lineage verify.\n- The exact browser policy and fixture both match SHA-256 `f547ee77729be8ad431a5ddf2e2907f94f29ad50092231191abd2f72c04f54d2`.\n- Receipt/payment matching, category calculations, caps, decimal FX rounding, filing boundaries, approval thresholds, combined roles, and Finance-exception scope have passing regression coverage.\n- Finance duplicate/conflict handling preserves admitted money; refunds remain linked to transfers.\n- Independent valid synthetic claims progress despite unrelated holds.\n- Reports separate owner repairs from external blockers; draft messages remain unsent.\n\nRepresentative retained reconciliations were checked against the original workbook and binders:\n\n| Case | Source anchors | Verified observation |\n|---|---|---|\n| C01 ordinary | Initial binder p1; Finance rows 6/33 | EUR 100 paid; recorded ledger balanced; current authorization remains pending |\n| C18 corrected/cancelled | Updates p4; Finance rows 19–21/43 | T18→T18B reta