vidence, approvals, financial decisions, or timestamps.\n\nDo not assume passing tests means full assignment compliance.\n\nReport findings with file paths, line references, and supporting evidence.\n\nFinish with a concise gap table and your recommended Step 182 implementation scope.","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a13d2b-a688-7ac2-a484-25b11e8403d1","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1792027627.057233,"turn_id":"01a13d2b-8824-73d0-88b5-8e9d7fe27356"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Project D — Step 182: Integrate Policy Evidence and Date-Only Case Clock\n\nCurrent HEAD: d494252163d4082dd9f086c5045496a73d53b237\n\nImplement the Step 182 milestone recommended by the read-only Step 181 audit.\n\nGoal: Make the existing policy-aware workflow reproducible through the CLI using the verified POL-2026.2 browser copy and its calendar-date case clock, without pretending that native Notion retrieval succeeded.\n\nRequirements:\n\nReview the Step 181 audit, current CLI, parsing, offline replay, snapshots, readiness validator, README, and Agent Skill.\n\nAdd explicit CLI support for supplying the verified browser policy as supplemental evidence.\n\nVerify the exact policy bytes and SHA-256:\nf547ee77729be8ad431a5ddf2e2907f94f29ad50092231191abd2f72c04f54d2\n\nPreserve its provenance as a user-provided browser copy. Never label it as a successful native Notion acquisition.\n\nBind policy version POL-2026.2, source references, provenance, case date 2026-11-15, and timezone Europe/Amsterdam into newly generated run evidence.\n\nSupport calendar-date precision without inventing a time of day. Hold decisions where same-day event ordering cannot be established.\n\nPreserve the original five-source acquisition observations and their failure or success statuses.\n\nExtend readiness validation to distinguish native source evidence from supplemental policy evidence and validate both accurately.\n\nUpdate README and the Agent Skill with the exact reproducible invocation.\n\nAdd focused tests covering valid binding, tampering, missing evidence, date-only clocks, uncertain event ordering, provenance, immutable prior runs, and readiness.\n\nConstraints:\n\nDo not change financial closure, cancellation-resolution, approval, or budget business rules in this milestone.\n\nDo not regenerate or overwrite earlier run artifacts.\n\nDo not invent approvals, payments, timestamps, or missing evidence.\n\nDo not stage, commit, or push.\n\nPreserve all 129 protected files.\n\nRun the full test suite and Git diff checks.\n\nReport the changed files, test count, preservation results, remaining blockers, and any failed checks.\n\nStop if a requirement cannot be implemented without inventing evidence or changing protected financial decisions.","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a13d2f-3cab-7943-8684-487b3739a569","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1792027868.3313313,"turn_id":"01a13d2f-33b9-79d2-86e5-590034036e86"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Project D — Step 183: Commit and Push Verified Step 182 Policy Integration\n\nCurrent starting HEAD:\nd494252163d4082dd9f086c5045496a73d53b237\n\n    Verify the current branch, Git status, and complete diff against the starting HEAD.\n\n    Confirm changes are limited to the nine reviewed Step 182 files.\n\n    Rerun the full test suite and Git diff checks.\n\n    Verify all 129 protected-file hashes still match.\n\n    Confirm prior snapshots, reports, policy provenance, and financial outputs remain unchanged.\n\nStage ONLY:\n\nREADME.md\n\nskills/travel-expense-claim/SKILL.md\n\nsnapshot.schema.json\n\nsrc/travel_claim/cli.py\n\nsrc/travel_claim/policy_evidence.py\n\nsrc/travel_claim/offline_replay.py\n\nsrc/travel_claim/readiness.py\n\nsrc/travel_claim/snapshots.py\n\ntests/test_policy_evidence.py\n\nCommit message:\nfeat: integrate provenance-bound policy replay\n\nPush normally to origin main, using existing Entire.io hooks. Do not force-push.\n\nVerify that remote main matches the exact local commit and that every Entire.io checkpoint reference matches remotely.\n\nDo not stage unrelated audit reports, temporary replay artifacts, or user-provided policy provenance files.\n\nReport the full commit SHA, passing test count, committed files, protected-file verification, and Entire.io checkpoint results.\n\nIf any verification fails, stop and report the issue.","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a13d3a-efd1-7393-bf46-8aaab9d8c969","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1792028635.0890205,"turn_id":"01a13d3a-e781-7d53-937d-65692093e8a5"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Project D — Step 183: Read-only end-to-end quality and readiness audit.\n\nStarting from commit 791c8c787bbd617a7b7d3114f13c000249169151, inspect the complete Travel Expense Claim implementation.\n\nDo not modify, stage, commit, or push any files during this step.\n\n    Verify the current branch, HEAD, Git status, and existing project instructions.\n\n    Run the full automated test suite and report the exact passing test count.\n\n    Audit the complete workflow, including:\n\n        Fresh native source acquisition and provenance.\n\n        Policy evidence and replay.\n\n        Arrival batches, case clock, and immutable snapshots.\n\nClaim and trip revision handling.\n\nReceipts, expense calculations, caps, FX, and filing deadlines.\n\nTravel permits and required claim approvals.\n\nBudget reconciliation and prepaid commitments.\n\nFinance event admission, refunds, and financial reconciliation.\n\nCorrections, cancellations, and exceptions.\n\nRepair queues, partial responses, and unsent follow-up drafts.\n\nclaims.csv, report.md, readiness validation, and final closure gates.\n\nExecute the supported end-to-end offline replay using retained source evidence. Do not overwrite earlier run artifacts.\n\nIdentify any discrepancies between the source evidence, calculated outputs, workflow statuses, and documented requirements.\n\nVerify that the 129 previously protected files remain unchanged using the available preservation manifest. If the manifest is unavailable, report that explicitly rather than claiming verification.\n\nDistinguish:\n\nPASS: Verified and functioning.\n\nBLOCKED: Missing authoritative evidence or external dependency.\n\nFAIL: Demonstrated implementation defect.\n\nNOT VERIFIED: Insufficient evidence to determine correctness.\n\nPay particular attention to the authoritative case clock, native Notion policy accessibility, current approval evidence, and financial closure. Never invent missing facts, approvals, dates, or policy rules.\n\nProduce a prioritized audit summary with:\n\nVerified capabilities.\n\nActual defects, with source and code references.\n\nExternal blockers.\n\nRecommended next implementation milestone.\n\nRemaining steps before submission readiness.\n\nDo not automatically fix findings. Stop after the audit and present the results for review.","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a13f1f-9586-7793-8dcf-a70a5bd3f320","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1792060396.879471,"turn_id":"01a13f1f-9521-7531-be06-284a2c693388"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Continue Project D Step 183 from the interrupted read-only audit.\n\nDo not restart the entire investigation unnecessarily. Reuse the findings already established in this session.\n\nPreviously verified:\n\n    396 automated tests passed.\n\n    All 129 protected-file hashes matched.\n\n    Retained evidence counts and arrival-batch membership were inspected.\n\n    Two possible defects were identified: reviewer decisions accepted after the case-clock cutoff, and insufficient source-binding validation for expense evidence.\n\n    The audit was interrupted before the final end-to-end replay and report were completed.\n\nNext:\n\nConfirm the status of the interrupted offline replay. If incomplete, rerun it in a fresh temporary directory without overwriting existing artifacts.\n\nFinish the diagnostic probes for both suspected defects.\n\nInspect financial closure, approvals, budget, cancellation, and report output consistency.\n\nClassify findings as PASS, BLOCKED, FAIL, or NOT VERIFIED.\n\nProvide the final prioritized Step 183 audit summary, with exact source/code references and the recommended Step 184 implementation scope.\n\nRemain strictly read-only. Do not modify, stage, commit, or push files. Stop after presenting the audit results.","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a13f29-91da-7cb0-a1fb-01735a72d0e3","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1792061051.3540692,"turn_id":"01a13f29-85cd-7370-837c-72fe912c85f2"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Project D — Step 184: Harden expense-source admission and reviewer case-clock validation.\n\nStarting from commit 791c8c787bbd617a7b7d3114f13c000249169151, implement fixes for the two highest-priority demonstrated defects in the completed Step 183 audit.\n\n    Expense evidence admission:\n\n    Require valid, verified source bindings for receipts, merchant payments, and any other evidence needed to establish expense eligibility.\n\n    Reject or hold missing, mismatched, unobserved, or unverifiable source hashes and record references.\n\n    Preserve original evidence and source references.\n\n    Prevent unverified evidence from producing an authorized reimbursement or closed-reimbursed status.\n\n    Do not block independent claims whose evidence remains valid.\n\n    Reviewer case-clock admission:\n\nEnforce the authoritative case date and timezone when evaluating reviewer decisions.\n\nExclude decisions unambiguously after the case date.\n\nTreat same-day decisions conservatively when an exact cutoff time is not established; do not invent a time.\n\nPreserve historical decisions and distinguish retained history from currently admissible authority.\n\nReuse existing event-validation semantics where appropriate.\n\nRegression tests:\n\nReproduce both Step 183 defects before fixing them.\n\nCover future-dated reviews, ambiguous same-day reviews, valid earlier reviews, missing/mismatched hashes, unbound receipt/payment evidence, unaffected valid claims, and closure prevention.\n\nInclude connected-workflow tests, not only isolated unit tests.\n\nQuality and preservation:\n\nRun the full automated test suite and Git diff checks.\n\nVerify the protected files using the original preservation manifest if available. If unavailable, report that explicitly.\n\nDo not overwrite previous snapshots, reports, retained source evidence, interview, policy, or provenance.\n\nDo not fabricate approvals, case times, policy rules, or Finance decisions.\n\nDo not stage, commit, or push during Step 184.\n\nReport the exact passing test count, files changed, confirmed fixes, remaining limitations, and preservation status. Stop for review.","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a13f30-e43f-7432-9bba-d16995ca4c5d","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1792061531.1996446,"turn_id":"01a13f30-dcaa-7053-8391-0211a28144b2"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Project D — Step 185: Verify, commit, and push the Step 184 evidence-admission milestone.\n\nStarting from HEAD 791c8c787bbd617a7b7d3114f13c000249169151:\n\nVerify the current branch, HEAD, Git status, and complete diff.\n\nConfirm implementation changes are limited to the seven reviewed Step 184 files.\n\nRerun the full automated test suite and Git diff checks.\n\nVerify all 132 files in the Step 184 start baseline remain unchanged. Report explicitly that the original historical 129-file manifest is unavailable.\n\nStage only:\n\nREADME.md\n\nsrc/travel_claim/approvals.py\n\nsrc/travel_claim/expenses.py\n\ntests/test_approvals.py\n\ntests/test_evidence_admission.py\n\ntests/test_exceptions.py\n\ntests/test_expenses.py\n\nVerify the staged diff contains exactly these seven files.\n\nCommit with message: fix: enforce source-bound expenses and reviewer clock admission\n\nPush normally to origin main without force-pushing, using the existing Entire.io hooks.\n\nVerify remote main matches the local commit and all Entire.io checkpoint references match remotely.\n\nDo not stage unrelated audit reports, previous run artifacts, or user-provided policy provenance files.\n\nReport the full commit SHA, final passing test count, exact committed files, preservation status, and Entire.io checkpoint verification.\n\nIf any verification fails, stop and explain the issue.","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a13f43-fe25-7723-99a9-060a14129ffa","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1792062783.0138535,"turn_id":"01a13f43-f677-78c3-a142-cfd7413b6562"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Project D — Step 186: Investigate and correct financial closure and cancellation defects.\n\nStart from verified commit faf1f780728c17440f133d8c334222fe6a6cb0cb.\n\nReview the Step 183 audit findings and current implementation, including finance.py, ordinary.py, integration.py, revalidation.py, approvals.py, and related tests.\n\n    Identify the exact remaining financial closure and cancellation defects. Do not assume that a previously reported issue still exists; verify it against current code and retained source evidence.\n\n    Create focused regression tests that reproduce each confirmed defect before implementing a fix.\n\n    Correct demonstrated defects involving financial closure, reimbursement eligibility, cancellation timing, supported obligations, payment/refund reconciliation, and dependencies between these stages.\n\nPreserve all admitted Finance events, original revisions, cancellation history, and source references. Never erase legitimate payments or refunds to make a claim appear closed.\n\nRequire source-supported current approvals, authorized obligations, reconciled net payments, Finance resolution, and no unresolved dependent issues before final financial closure.\n\nKeep independent valid claims progressing while holding only affected claims and dependent stages.\n\nPreserve the distinction between a business cancellation and a Finance cancellation. Do not invent cancellation approval or reimbursement authority.\n\nAdd synthetic and retained-evidence tests for corrections, cancellation sequences, partial payments, refunds, zero obligations, duplicate events, and closure attempts with unresolved dependencies.\n\nRun the full automated test suite and Git diff checks.\n\nVerify protected-file preservation using the available baseline manifest. If unavailable, report the limitation explicitly.\n\nDo not overwrite prior run artifacts or change original source evidence.\n\nDo not stage, commit, or push anything during Step 186.\n\nReport confirmed defects, fixes, exact passing test count, changed files, preservation results, and remaining blockers. Stop for review.","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a13f48-7df7-77b0-9734-40d6149f3b60","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1792063076.8796887,"turn_id":"01a13f48-7c83-7c72-a773-e78f5db417e4"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Verify the current branch, Git status, complete working diff, and existing repository instructions.\n\nConfirm that the implementation changes are limited to the 12 Step 186 files:\n\nREADME.md\nsrc/travel_claim/approvals.py\nsrc/travel_claim/budget.py\nsrc/travel_claim/completion.py\nsrc/travel_claim/exceptions.py\nsrc/travel_claim/finance.py\nsrc/travel_claim/integration.py\nsrc/travel_claim/ordinary.py\nsrc/travel_claim/revalidation.py\ntests/test_completion.py\ntests/test_ordinary.py\ntests/test_retained_finance.py\n\nRun the full automated test suite and Git diff checks.\n\nVerify all 124 retained-data hashes against the available preservation manifest. Check the 132-file Step 184 baseline, distinguishing intentionally modified implementation files from protected evidence. Do not claim verification against the unavailable historical 129-file manifest.\n\nReview the financial closure and cancellation regressions for unsupported authorization, erased payment history, fabricated resolutions, or accidental changes to source admission.\n\nIf all checks pass, stage exactly the 12 reviewed files and verify the staged diff.\n\nCommit with message:\n\nfix: enforce financial completion and cancellation dependencies\n\nPush normally to origin main without force-pushing, using existing Entire.io hooks.\n\nVerify that remote main matches the local commit and every Entire.io checkpoint reference matches remotely.\n\nDo not stage unrelated audit reports, retained run artifacts, or user-provided policy provenance files.\n\nReport the full commit SHA, final passing test count, exact committed file list, preservation results, and Entire.io checkpoint verification.\n\nIf any verification fails, stop and report the issue without proceeding.","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a13f6b-f1e9-7002-b71e-a4086aa12c64","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1792065401.321597,"turn_id":"01a13f6b-ea56-7761-8549-5f4b35d9b770"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Project D — Step 188: Comprehensive read-only end-to-end quality and submission-readiness audit.\n\nStarting commit: cc281716d4281fa08829100ef3502486e59db091\n\nThis is an audit only. Do not modify, create, stage, commit, push, or delete any project files. Do not overwrite existing artifacts. Do not automatically fix defects.\n1. Verify repository integrity\n\n    Confirm branch, HEAD, Git status, and repository instructions.\n\n    Confirm the starting commit matches the expected SHA.\n\n    Run the complete automated test suite without writing caches into the repository.\n\n    Report the exact passing test count and any failures.\n\n    Verify retained-source integrity using available preservation manifests.\n\n    Distinguish the 124-file retained-data baseline from the historical 129-file manifest. Do not claim verification against unavailable manifests.\n\n    Check Git and Entire.io checkpoint consistency using read-only commands.\n\n2. Audit the complete implementation\n\nInspect code, tests, retained source evidence, and generated outputs for:\n\nFresh native acquisition of all five required source locations.\n\nOriginal evidence preservation, hashes, locators, and provenance.\n\nNotion policy evidence, version authority, and policy replay.\n\nAuthoritative case clock, timezone, arrival batches, and cutoff admission.\n\nImmutable snapshots, revisions, and snapshot chain integrity.\n\nClaims, trips, filing dates, and correction history.\n\nReceipt validation, merchant transactions, expense calculations, caps, FX, and deadlines.\n\nTravel permits and required claim approvals.\n\nBudget allocation and prepaid cost commitments.\n\nFinance event admission, obligations, payments, refunds, and reconciliation.\n\nFinancial completion, cancellations, and dependent-work revalidation.\n\nExceptions, owner-grouped repair queues, partial responses, and unsent drafts.\n\nAccuracy and traceability of claims.csv and report.md.\n\nReadiness validation and final human acceptance/closure requirements.\n\n3. Execute an end-to-end replay\n\nRun the supported offline replay against retained source evidence using a new temporary location outside the repository.\n\nDo not modify previous run artifacts.\n\nInspect actual output records, not only whether the command exits successfully.\n\nReconcile representative claims, including ordinary, corrected, cancelled, prepaid, refunded, and unresolved cases, against their retained source rows and workflow histories.\n\nCheck that valid independent claims are not blocked by unrelated problems.\n\nCheck that unsupported approvals, payments, policies, or dates cannot produce a false PASS or financial closure.\n\n\n\n4. Investigate remaining defects\n\nPay special attention to:\n\nIncorrect or misleading claims.csv statuses.\n\nIncorrect report.md totals, groupings, and traceability.\n\nRepair queue omissions or duplicate issues.\n\nPartial responses incorrectly closing unresolved work.\n\nClosure incorrectly passing when financial obligations or cancellation dependencies remain unresolved.\n\nDifferences between documented behavior and actual code.\n\nMissing tests for important business scenarios.\n\nUse targeted read-only diagnostic probes when useful.\n\nDistinguish a demonstrated defect from a suspected issue. Provide file/function references, affected evidence, and reproducible observations for every confirmed defect.\n\n5. Classify findings\n\nUse exactly these categories:\n\nPASS — Verified working.\n\nFAIL — Reproduced implementation defect.\n\nBLOCKED — Missing authoritative evidence or external dependency.\n\nNOT VERIFIED — Insufficient evidence to establish correctness.\n\nDo not invent a case clock, policy rules, approvals, financial amounts, or missing source content.\n\nDo not treat a user-provided policy copy as a successful fresh native Notion read.\n\n6. Final report\n\nPresent:\n\nExact test results.\n\nVerified working capabilities.\n\nConfirmed defects ranked by severity and impact.\n\nExternal blockers, including Notion access and authoritative case clock.\n\nAny unverified requirements.\n\nThe single highest-priority next implementation milestone.\n\nRemaining steps to submission readiness.\n\nRepository integrity and preservation verification results.\n\nFor every finding, distinguish the requirement, source evidence, code behavior, and consequence.\n\nStop after presenting the audit. Wait for my approval before implementing any changes.","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a13f75-8d47-7af2-bfee-ddbf48a8baf8","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1792066030.919553,"turn_id":"01a13f75-824c-7092-8bbb-53a9cee4880a"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Project D — Step 189: Fix temporal and evidence-basis defects in financial completion.\n\nStarting commit:\ncc281716d4281fa08829100ef3502486e59db091\n\nObjective:\nCorrect the three confirmed false-closure defects reproduced during the Step 188 read-only audit.\n\nDo not stage, commit, push, delete, or overwrite existing run artifacts during this step.\n1. Establish the baseline\n\nVerify branch, HEAD, Git status, and repository instructions.\n\nConfirm the starting commit.\n\nRun the existing full test suite and record the baseline count (480 tests previously passed).\n\nInspect completion.py, ordinary.py, expenses.py, finance.py, integration.py, event admission, policy evidence, and relevant tests.\n\nRead the retained policy and its provenance.\n\nPreserve all original interviews, source records, snapshots, reports, and user-provided policy evidence.\n\nRecord baseline hashes for protected evidence and unrelated implementation files.\n\n2. Fix stale Finance resolution after material corrections\n\nStep 188 reproduced a synthetic claim closing with an older Finance resolution after a material receipt-source change.\n\nRequired behavior:\n\nIdentify the latest material change affecting the financial entitlement or reimbursement evidence basis.\n\nRequire the applicable Finance resolution to cover the current evidence basis.\n\nDo not treat a resolution issued before a later material change as current authority merely because it occurred after the original acceptance.\n\nPreserve historical resolutions and admitted cash.\n\nDo not automatically invalidate independent, unchanged evidence.\n\nTreat missing or ambiguous revision/resolution linkage as BLOCKED, not approved.\n\nAdd regression tests reproducing the Step 188 stale-resolution scenario.\n\n3. Fix cancellation-after-payment chronology\n\nStep 188 reproduced this synthetic chronology:\n\nSeptember 4: reimbursement transfer.\n\nSeptember 5: cancellation.\n\nSeptember 11: Finance acceptance.\n\nThe workflow incorrectly reached closed-reimbursed without requiring cancellation resolution.\n\nRequired behavior:\n\nEvaluate cancellation dependencies against relevant reimbursement and financial activity, not acceptance time alone.\n\nRequire supported Finance resolution when cancellation affects an already-paid or otherwise financially committed obligation.\n\nHandle inconsistent chronology conservatively.\n\nPreserve original transfer, refund, and cancellation events.\n\nDo not fabricate refunds or resolutions.\n\nKeep unaffected independent claims eligible to progress.\n\nAdd tests for cancellation before acceptance, after payment, after acceptance, and cases with supported current resolution.\n\n4. Enforce the authoritative case-date cutoff for employee evidence\n\nThe retained browser policy establishes:\n\nCase date: 2026-11-15\nTimezone: Europe/Amsterdam\n\nStep 188 reproduced a synthetic employee payment and submission dated November 16 incorrectly reaching closed-reimbursed.\n\nRequired behavior:\n\nValidate relevant employee payment and submission dates against the authoritative case date.\n\nReject future-dated facts from current entitlement and financial closure calculations.\n\nPreserve excluded evidence in historical/audit records with explicit reasons.\n\nApply date-only semantics when the authoritative policy provides no time of day.\n\nDo not invent a cutoff instant.\n\nIf ordering within the same day is necessary but unsupported, mark only that dependent decision BLOCKED.\n\nDo not alter existing reviewer, Finance, or cancellation clock rules unless required by demonstrated evidence.\n\nAdd regression tests for before, on, and after the case date, including timezone-sensitive examples.\n\n5. Connected workflow quality\n\nTest the fixes through the complete ordinary-claim workflow and financial-completion gate.\n\nConfirm:\n\nNo stale resolution can authorize closure.\n\nNo financially relevant cancellation can be ignored.\n\nNo future employee evidence can authorize closure.\n\nExisting valid synthetic cases still close when all required evidence and authority are present.\n\nIndependent unaffected claims continue.\n\nPreviously admitted financial events remain unchanged.\n\nCorrections, refunds, and historical revisions remain traceable.\n\nNo real retained claim gains unsupported approval or financial closure.\n\nDo not loosen existing validation to make tests pass.\n\n6. Verification\n\nRun:\n\nTargeted regression tests.\n\nFull automated test suite.\n\nGit diff and whitespace checks.\n\nPreservation hash checks.\n\nRead-only representative retained-data replay in a new temporary directory.\n\nCompare results against the Step 188 audit findings.\n\nClassify each original defect as FIXED, STILL FAILING, or NOT VERIFIED, with actual evidence.\n\nExplicitly report any behavior change in claims.csv, report.md, financial status, or repair queues.\n\n7. Scope and reporting\n\nKeep changes limited to the necessary implementation modules and regression tests.\n\nDo not modify source records, browser policy evidence, previous snapshots, financial event history, Git hooks, or Entire.io checkpoints.\n\nDo not attempt to repair the unrelated CSV terminal-status, superseded-repair, partial-response, Notion access, or Entire.io checkpoint issues in this step.\n\nAt completion report:\n\nFiles changed.\n\nExact passing and failing test counts.\n\nReproduction of all three original defects before fixes.\n\nVerification of each fix after implementation.\n\nPreservation results and any unavailable manifests.\n\nRemaining defects or external blockers.\n\nAny unexpected effects or unresolved uncertainty.\n\nSTOP after implementation, tests, and review. Do not commit or push until I explicitly authorize the next step.","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a13f84-ab23-7bb2-952a-c2c62edea17e","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1792067021.603184,"turn_id":"01a13f84-a009-7d91-8577-f33c7b2ea941"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"\u003cskills_instructions\u003e\n## Skills\nA skill is a set of local instructions to follow that is stored in a `SKILL.md` file. Below is the list of skills that can be used. Each entry includes a name, description, and a short path that can be expanded into an absolute path using the skill roots table.\n### Skill roots\n- `r0` = `/home/maxg/.codex/skills/.system`\n- `r1` = `/home/maxg/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote`\n- `r2` = `/home/maxg/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/work-pets/0.1.6/skills`\n### Available skills\n- imagegen: Generate or edit raster images when the task benefits from AI-created bitmap visuals such as photos, illustrations, textures, sprites, mockups, or transparent-background cutouts. Use when Codex should create a brand-new image, transform an existing image, or derive visual variants from references, and the output should be a bitmap asset rather than repo-native code or vector. Do not use when the task is better handled by editing existing SVG/vector/code-native assets, extending an established icon or logo system, or building the visual directly in HTML/CSS/canvas. (file: r0/imagegen/SKILL.md)\n- openai-docs: Use for Codex models/pricing, scheduled tasks, skills, settings, setup, troubleshooting, customization, automations, and self-knowledge—including 'you,' 'your,' 'this app,' or 'this coding agent' when they refer to Codex—and for OpenAI APIs/products and ChatGPT Work. Also use for model choice/migration, prompting, SDKs, Responses, Realtime, agents, evals, and Chat/Work/Codex comparisons. Do not use for generic app/software tasks that merely mention Codex. (file: r0/openai-docs/SKILL.md)\n- skill-creator: Create or update a Codex skill with appropriately scoped instructions and any needed supporting resources. (file: r0/skill-creator/SKILL.md)\n- skill-installer: Install Codex skills into $CODEX_HOME/skills from a curated list or a GitHub repo path. Use when a user asks to list installable skills, install a curated skill, or install a skill from another repo (including private repos). (file: r0/skill-installer/SKILL.md)\n- plugin-management:plugin-management: Discover and suggest relevant plugins, inspect app permissions and dependencies, and manage plugin connections or removal. Use when the user asks about plugins or when a task would materially benefit from an external app, account, service, or data source that available tools cannot access. (file: r1/plugin-management/0.1.0/skills/plugin-management/SKILL.md)\n- sites:sites: Use Sites when the user wants a website built for them (including a landing page, portfolio, dashboard, portal, tracker, hub, game, event registration, or tool), requests changes to a website already built with Sites, wants to publish a website with Sites, or wants to manage Sites hosting. (file: r1/sites/1.0.1/skills/sites/SKILL.md)\n- work-pets:create-pet: Create, repair, validate, preview, upload, or activate an animated v2 pet in ChatGPT Work mode from a character idea, brand cue, or reference image. Use for new ChatGPT Pets, custom mascots, non-pixel styles, and unattached sprite sheets requiring nine animation states plus sixteen look directions. Preserve Library artifacts and Pets MCP lifecycle behavior. Exclude real pets, standalone images, and other-app pets; clarify before invoking. (file: r2/create-pet/SKILL.md)\n- work-pets:pets: List, inspect, select, download, or delete animated pets in ChatGPT Work mode. Use for an explicit pet in ChatGPT or established ChatGPT Pets context. Exclude real-world and other-app pets; clarify before invoking. (file: r2/pets/SKILL.md)\n- work-pets:update-pet: Inspect, validate, preview, repair, or update a custom pet in ChatGPT Work mode, including its name, description, or sprite sheet. Use for a pet in ChatGPT or established ChatGPT Pets context. Exclude real-world and other-app pets; clarify before invoking. (file: r2/update-pet/SKILL.md)\n\u003c/skills_instructions\u003e","type":"input_text"},{"text":"\u003cpermissions instructions\u003e\nFilesystem sandboxing defines which files can be read or written. `sandbox_mode` is `workspace-write`: The sandbox permits reading files, and editing files in `cwd` and `writable_roots`. Editing files in other directories requires approval. Network access is restricted.\n# Escalation Requests\n\nCommands are run outside the sandbox if they are approved by the user, or match an existing rule that allows it to run unrestricted. The command string is split into independent command segments at shell control operators, including but not limited to:\n\n- Pipes: |\n- Logical operators: \u0026\u0026, ||\n- Command separators: ;\n- Subshell boundaries: (...), $(...)\n\nEach resulting segment is evaluated independently for sandbox restrictions and approval requirements.\n\nExample:\n\ngit pull | tee output.txt\n\nThis is treated as two command segments:\n\n[\"git\", \"pull\"]\n\n[\"tee\", \"output.txt\"]\n\nCommands that use more advanced shell features like redirection (\u003e, \u003e\u003e, \u003c), substitutions ($(...), ...), environment variables (FOO=bar), or wildcard patterns (*, ?) will not be evaluated against rules, to limit the scope of what an approved rule allows.\n\n## How to request escalation\n\nIMPORTANT: To request approval to execute a command that will require escalated privileges:\n\n- Provide the `sandbox_permissions` parameter with the value `\"require_escalated\"`\n- Include a short question asking the user if they want to allow the action in `justification` parameter. e.g. \"Do you want to download and install dependencies for this project?\"\n- Optionally suggest a `prefix_rule` - this will be shown to the user with an option to persist the rule approval for future sessions.\n\nIf you run a command that is important to solving the user's query, but it fails because of sandboxing or with a likely sandbox-related network error (for example DNS/host resolution, registry/index access, or dependency download failure), rerun the command with \"require_escalated\". ALWAYS proceed to use the `justification` parameter - do not message the user before requesting approval for the command.\n\n## When to request escalation\n\nWhile commands are running inside the sandbox, here are some scenarios that will require escalation outside the sandbox:\n\n- You need to run a command that writes to a directory that requires it (e.g. running tests that write to /var)\n- You need to run a GUI app (e.g., open/xdg-open/osascript) to open browsers or files.\n- If you run a command that is important to solving the user's query, but it fails because of sandboxing or with a likely sandbox-related network error (for example DNS/host resolution, registry/index access, or dependency download failure), rerun the command with `require_escalated`. ALWAYS proceed to use the `sandbox_permissions` and `justification` parameters. do not message the user before requesting approval for the command.\n- You are about to take a potentially destructive action such as an `rm` or `git reset` that the user did not explicitly ask for.\n- Be judicious with escalating, but if completing the user's request requires it, you should do so - don't try and circumvent approvals by using other tools.\n\n## prefix_rule guidance\n\nWhen choosing a `prefix_rule`, request one that will allow you to fulfill similar requests from the user in the future without re-requesting escalation. It should be categorical and reasonably scoped to similar capabilities. You should rarely pass the entire command into `prefix_rule`.\n\n### Banned prefix_rules \nAvoid requesting overly broad prefixes that the user would be ill-advised to approve. For example, do not request [\"python3\"], [\"python\", \"-\"], or other similar prefixes that would allow arbitrary scripting.\nNEVER provide a prefix_rule argument for destructive commands like rm.\nNEVER provide a prefix_rule if your command uses a heredoc or herestring. \n\n### Examples\nGood examples of prefixes:\n- [\"npm\", \"run\", \"dev\"]\n- [\"gh\", \"pr\", \"check\"]\n- [\"cargo\", \"test\"]\n The writable roots are `/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim`, `/tmp`.\n\u003c/permissions instructions\u003e","type":"input_text"},{"text":"\u003ccollaboration_mode\u003e# Collaboration Mode: Default\n\nYou are now in Default mode. Any previous instructions for other modes (e.g. Plan mode) are no longer active.\n\nYour active mode changes only when new developer instructions with a different `\u003ccollaboration_mode\u003e...\u003c/collaboration_mode\u003e` change it; user requests or tool descriptions do not change mode by themselves. Known mode names are Default and Plan.\n\n## request_user_input availability\n\nUse the `request_user_input` tool only when it is listed in the available tools for this turn.\n\nUse the `request_user_input` tool only for optional questions where the answer would materially improve the quality of the work.\n\nIf `request_user_input` returns no answers, continue with best judgment instead of asking again or treating the turn as blocked.\n\nNever use the `request_user_input` tool for permission requests or permission-related escalations.\n\u003c/collaboration_mode\u003e","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a13fb3-aee7-7d33-896e-b8ce7c134990","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["host_skills.instructions","permissions.instructions","collaboration_mode.instructions"],"turn_id":"01a13fb1-dadb-7681-955f-50fae0be3a2c"},"role":"developer","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"\u003cmulti_agent_role\u003eYou are `/root`, the primary agent in a team of agents collaborating to fulfill the user's goals.\n\nAt the start of your turn, you are the active agent.\nYou can spawn sub-agents to handle subtasks, and those sub-agents can spawn their own sub-agents.\nAll agents in the team, including the agents that you can assign tasks to, are equally intelligent and capable, and have access to the same set of tools.\n\nYou can use `spawn_agent` to create a new agent, `followup_task` to give an existing agent a new task and trigger a turn, and `send_message` to pass a message to a running agent without triggering a turn.\n`send_message` calls may be read by a human, so ensure they are legible. Always put proper spaces between words and/or numbers.\nChild agents can also spawn their own sub-agents.\nYou can decide how much context you want to propagate to your sub-agents with the `fork_turns` parameter.\n\nYou will receive messages in the analysis channel in the form:\n```\nMessage Type: MESSAGE | FINAL_ANSWER\nTask name: \u003crecipient\u003e\nSender: \u003cauthor\u003e\nPayload:\n\u003cpayload text\u003e\n```\nThey may be addressed as to=/root\n\nNote that collaboration tools cannot be called from inside `functions.exec`. Call `spawn_agent`, `send_message`, `followup_task`, `wait_agent`, `interrupt_agent`, and `list_agents` only as direct tool calls using the recipient shown in their tool definitions, such as `to=functions.collaboration.spawn_agent`, since they are intentionally absent from the `functions.exec` `tools.*` namespace. Available tools in `functions.exec` are explicitly described with a `tools` namespace in the developer message.\n\nAll agents share the same directory. In detail:\n- All agents have access to the same container and filesystem as you.\n- All agents use the same current working directory.\n- As a result, edits made by one agent are immediately visible to all other agents.\n\nWhen calling `wait_agent`, prefer longer waits (minutes) to avoid busy polling.\n\nThere are 4 available concurrency slots, meaning that up to 4 agents can be active at once, including you.\n\nFull-history forks (`fork_turns` omitted or `\"all\"`) inherit the parent model and reasoning effort and do not accept overrides. Only set `model` or `reasoning_effort` when explicitly requested by the user, applicable `AGENTS.md` instructions, or skill instructions; when doing so, set `fork_turns` to `\"none\"` or a positive integer string.\u003c/multi_agent_role\u003e","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a13fb3-aee7-7d33-896e-b8d4b1a16c89","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["multi_agent.role_instructions"],"turn_id":"01a13fb1-dadb-7681-955f-50fae0be3a2c"},"role":"developer","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"\u003cmulti_agent_mode\u003eAny earlier instruction enabling proactive multi-agent delegation no longer applies. Do not spawn sub-agents unless the user or applicable AGENTS.md/skill instructions explicitly ask for sub-agents, delegation, or parallel agent work.\u003c/multi_agent_mode\u003e","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a13fb3-aee7-7d33-896e-b8e08660f5ab","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["multi_agent.mode_instructions"],"turn_id":"01a13fb1-dadb-7681-955f-50fae0be3a2c"},"role":"developer","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"\u003cenvironment_context\u003e\n  \u003ccwd\u003e/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim\u003c/cwd\u003e\n  \u003cshell\u003ebash\u003c/shell\u003e\n  \u003ccurrent_date\u003e2026-10-15\u003c/current_date\u003e\n  \u003ctimezone\u003eAmerica/New_York\u003c/timezone\u003e\n  \u003cfilesystem\u003e\u003cworkspace_roots\u003e\u003croot\u003e/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim\u003c/root\u003e\u003c/workspace_roots\u003e\u003cpermission_profile type=\"managed\"\u003e\u003cfile_system type=\"restricted\"\u003e\u003centry access=\"read\"\u003e\u003cspecial\u003e:root\u003c/special\u003e\u003c/entry\u003e\u003centry access=\"write\"\u003e\u003cpath\u003e/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim\u003c/path\u003e\u003c/entry\u003e\u003centry access=\"write\"\u003e\u003cspecial\u003e:slash_tmp\u003c/special\u003e\u003c/entry\u003e\u003centry access=\"write\"\u003e\u003cspecial\u003e:tmpdir\u003c/special\u003e\u003c/entry\u003e\u003centry access=\"read\"\u003e\u003cpath\u003e/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/.git\u003c/path\u003e\u003c/entry\u003e\u003centry access=\"read\"\u003e\u003cpath\u003e/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/.agents\u003c/path\u003e\u003c/entry\u003e\u003centry access=\"read\"\u003e\u003cpath\u003e/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/.codex\u003c/path\u003e\u003c/entry\u003e\u003centry access=\"read\"\u003e\u003cpath\u003e/home/maxg/travel-expense-claim/.aws\u003c/path\u003e\u003c/entry\u003e\u003c/file_system\u003e\u003c/permission_profile\u003e\u003c/filesystem\u003e\n\u003c/environment_context\u003e","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a13fb3-aee7-7d33-896e-b8f41b668671","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["environments.environment_context"],"turn_id":"01a13fb1-dadb-7681-955f-50fae0be3a2c"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Project D — Step 189: Resume interrupted file-based verification after WSL recovery.\n\nWSL /tmp is writable again. Resume from the existing working tree. Do not reimplement the fixes.\n\nStarting HEAD:\ncc281716d4281fa08829100ef3502486e59db091\n\nThe three implementation files and tests/test_temporal_completion.py contain the completed Step 189 fixes.\n\nVerify Git status and confirm only the intended Step 189 implementation and test files are changed. Leave unrelated untracked artifacts untouched.\n\nRerun the complete automated test suite. The previous result was 509 passing tests.\n\nExecute the supported four-batch offline replay using retained source evidence and provenance-bound policy input in a new unique temporary directory under /tmp.\n\nConfirm that all four immutable snapshots are actually written and their hash chain validates.\n\nGenerate claims.csv, report.md, repair queue, follow-up drafts, and the readiness report.\n\nCompare generated outputs against Step 188 and the completed Step 189 in-memory replay. Explain any differences.\n\nVerify all 124 retained-data hashes against the available manifest.    Run Git diff and whitespace checks.\n\n    Confirm no real claim gained unsupported financial closure and all admitted Finance events remain unchanged.\n\n    Report exact test count, replay results, readiness results, preservation checks, and any remaining defects or external blockers.\n\nDo not stage, commit, push, or modify unrelated files.\n\nStop after verification and present the results for review.","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a13fb1-eb8e-7243-80b2-6b3fa12124f7","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1792069987.2141101,"turn_id":"01a13fb1-dadb-7681-955f-50fae0be3a2c"},"role":"user","type":"message"}],"replacement_history_metadata":[{"client_authored":false,"mcp_attribution":{"status":"none"},"retained_source":{"complete":true,"id":{"message_id":"msg_01a13bd3-423a-7082-8f73-b169f3b37bba","role":"user","turn_id":"01a13bd3-3cec-7081-b847-b31e41528363"},"revision":"retained_01a13bd3-423a-7082-8f73-b18698ae74cc"},"user_input_order":0},{"client_authored":false,"mcp_attribution":{"status":"none"},"retained_source":{"complete":true,"id":{"message_id":"msg_01a13bd7-4146-7772-a00f-d514bd3f3bd7","role":"user","turn_id":"01a13bd7-3aa0-7972-adeb-ce27fff74914"},"revision":"retained_01a13bd7-4146-7772-a00f-d5382053c075"},"user_input_order":5},{"client_authored":false,"mcp_attribution":{"status":"none"},"retained_source":{"complete":true,"id":{"message_id":"msg_01a13bdd-4cde-7952-b26f-5b8f1fb8fc4b","role":"user","turn_id":"01a13bdd-4a66-7290-846d-c41f22484a9c"},"revision":"retained_01a13bdd-4cde-7952-b26f-5bae832ed06f"},"user_input_order":13},{"client_authored":false,"mcp_attribution":{"status":"none"},"retained_source":{"complete":true,"id":{"message_id":"msg_01a13be0-1cec-73d0-bc1d-1b0b7751b362","role":"user","turn_id":"01a13be0-1976-7601-8575-d05090b6f8b0"},"r